"And you can't write it."

"No."

"So the nearest you can get to *read it first* is that he hears it first." Lira considered that. "With us in the room, so he doesn't have to carry it across to us on his own."

"Yes. That's exactly why."

Lira looked at her a moment longer, and then she looked at Cael, and he did not know what she saw, but she saw it.

"Say it," said Lira, and went back to her book, and did not turn a single page of it for the rest of the evening.

Karis set it out as she set out every result: in order, plainly, hedged exactly as far as the evidence needed and not a word further.

"I've been documenting what I can see from outside. I don't know where any of it came from. You haven't told me, and I haven't asked, and I won't. But for a month three things have been sitting next to each other in my notebook, and I can't stop seeing them side by side.

"The first is the framework. I have a page on Lira's footwork, from two of her recorded bouts. I took it from the rail, for her, not for you." She did not look at Lira. "When you turn out of a strike, there's a small drop of the hip before the step. A quarter turn. Most Wind fighters don't do it. Lira does. You do it too, on the same count, to the beat. Not something like it. The same."

In the corner, Lira's finger did not move on the page.

"The second is the read. Brom told me, the week I came, that he waits on the second half of his turn longer than anybody teaches. When you read a body, through the cloth or without it, you have a wait in it of the same shape. You name one shift, and then you wait before you're sure of the next, the way he waits before he turns."

Brom had gone very still on the end of the bench.

"The third is the lead. I told you the framework and the read lead by the same quarter of a breath. Two different things you do, and they both come early by the same amount. And at your very first session I timed the gap between your eyes settling on where a strike would come from and your feet going. That's a quarter of a breath as well." She put one hand flat on her closed notebook. "So in both of them, the first thing that happens is that you look. Whatever comes after, the looking comes first. By the same margin, every time."

She drew a breath.

"So here's the hypothesis. Two capabilities, each carrying the signature of a person you've watched very closely, for a long time, at close range. Both of them started by the same act, which is watching. Whatever it is you do, the watching seems to come first. It's upstream of everything else." There was a pause. He knew what it meant: the part that mattered most was next. "And if watching is what goes in, then it's a thing you choose. You choose where to look. You choose how long. So in principle, whatever comes out of the other end ought to be directable. You ought to be able to aim it. Not only receive it."

The room went still. It had nothing to do with the barley going cold or the fire needing tending; it was the stillness of a floor in a house when something heavy has been set down on it, and everybody standing on it waits to hear whether it will hold.

# Chapter 59 — Concluded

Yorlan did not sit down to give it.

He stood behind the middle chair with the thin book closed in one hand and a single sheet in the other, and the other two stood on either side of him. The room stood with them, two hundred people and the passage behind them, and Cael at the respondent's table on his feet with his hands at his sides, and nobody breathing.

Yorlan read it in the voice he had used for everything, the voice that gave every word the same weight as the word before it, and the plainness of it was worse than any other voice could have been. It was the Compact itself speaking, and there was nobody left in the hall to blame for it but the Compact.

"The panel rules as follows.

"On the enrollment: the panel does not rule. Its validity as categorized is not properly before this tribunal absent established jurisdiction, and standing on that question is remanded to the academy as an internal matter."

He turned the sheet a little toward the light.

"On jurisdiction: the charter's definitions clause is closed. Classification, for all purposes under the charter, means designation under the enumerated schedule. The panel has heard the schedule in its entirety, on this record. The designation [SHATTERED] does not appear in it. The Compact has therefore not established that the respondent is a classified practitioner within the meaning of its own enforcement provisions, and has not established authority to void the enrollment through this challenge. The respondent's classification status is undefined under the current schedule."

A pause, the smallest in the whole of two days.

"The challenge is dismissed for want of established jurisdiction. The record is public. This tribunal is concluded."

He laid the sheet on the bench, and struck the small gavel once.

Cael heard it end and did not move.

*Undefined.* He had spent three weeks with the code, and he knew what the word was worth and what it was not. It was enough, for now, to have heard the Compact say it aloud.

The hall did not wait for him to think about it. The noise came up all round him at once, so loud and so sudden that it reached him first through the boards and the soles of his boots. It was louder than the main floor at the Ironyard on the night of the Reydan bout. He stood in the middle of it at the respondent's table with his palms on the wood and let it go past him. It did not get in yet. For one more breath he had the thing to himself.

Lira was first.

She was across the rope before the gavel's knock had died in the beams. She did not go round by the gap, or wait for the clerk to lift the cord. She put one hand on it and vaulted, as she had gone over the barrier of every fighting yard in Ardenmere, and he saw the clerk turn with his mouth open and shut it again.

Brom had not moved toward the table at all. He was still at the front bench, standing, looking across the boards at Cael, and nodding: once, and then after a long moment, once more. It was the nod Brom gave when a weight he had watched somebody struggle under came up clean off the floor at last.

Karis was still sitting. She had her notebook open on her knee and her pen was going across the page fast, and her face was wet, and she was doing nothing whatever about it. She wrote as if what was happening would get away from her if she stopped putting it down for one breath.

Quenna, in the row behind the table, had shut her eyes. As he looked she let a breath go out of her, so long and so slow that it seemed to take the whole winter with it, and she did not open her eyes until it was all gone.

Prynn was on her feet at the end of the front bench, with her arms unfolded for the first time that day, and her dry old face had lit as if somebody had put a lamp behind it. He had never seen her look like that, and he did not believe anybody living had. On the bench beside her the one place she had held all morning was still empty. Her folded slip still lay on it, the narrow kind she left in a gap on a shelf to say that something which belonged there was missing.

At the far end of the bench by the aisle Oona had not stood and had not shouted. She sat bolt upright with her slate across her knees, writing in letters large enough that he could see their size from the table. As he watched she drew a line under them, and another, and a third.

Across the boards, the Warden's last case was already closed. When Cael's eyes found him he did not look away. He looked back across the open boards, steadily, with nothing held back in his face, and bent his head. It was not a bow. It was a man who had lost, saying that he knew it, and that he knew how. Then he lifted his cases and turned toward the side door.

Cael stood in the last moment of quiet he was going to be given, with his hands on the wood.

Nine days, six, eleven, four. None of those four had ever waited at a table for anybody to come back through a door; there had never been anything for anybody to come back and decide. Three of their files did not end, they only stopped. He had read them at the long table by lamplight and brought all four into this hall with him without saying so to anybody. Now there was a record of the kind none of them had been given. It had his name across the top, in the Compact's own voice, where anybody who wanted to could read it.

The rest of the Compact was still out there, every office and every desk. Somewhere far above any door he could knock on, it would already be turning its head toward this hall.

Lira's hand struck the edge of the table. The quiet was over, and the whole roar of the hall poured in.

---

It took most of an hour for the hall to empty, and he stayed in it the whole time.

Lira and Brom and Karis stood round him for some of it and would not let anybody close. The passage would have taken him a quarter of an hour to get through in any case. And under both of those there was a reason he did not put into words even to himself: there was one more thing owed before this day could be called finished, and he had known it since the Warden bent his head.

The people who did not belong to the hill left in an order he could not help noticing. The two grey coats went first, fast, through the side door, already writing their letters in their heads. The woman in dark blue went next, with the man who had the look of a provost. The guild's legal officer stopped at the rope to say something to the academy's counsel and was not answered. The old woman in black with her cushion went last. She had sat through both sittings without writing a word, and now she stood a long while by the back wall as the benches cleared, her eyes on no one, and went out without having said anything to anyone. He kept her in mind for some other year.

The Warden came back in by the side door when the hall was nearly bare.

He came by himself. There was no aide, and there were no cases, and Cael understood with a small shock that in three weeks he had never once seen the man's hands empty. Coss did not come straight to the respondent's table. He stopped at the near end of it, where the low wall of Prynn's books still stood, and looked at the books. He put two fingers on the spine of the seventh volume of the schedule and took them away again.

"I read the enforcement chapters through once," he said. "At the regional school, when I was younger than your provost's clerk. Nobody told me to read the schedule. It didn't occur to me." He turned. "I'd like five minutes, off the record. I'd ask the assessor of record to stand witness, if you'll allow it. I'd not like either of us to carry this away different from the other."

In all the time Cael had known him, the man had never once asked his leave.

"Quenna," said Cael.

She left her chair in the row behind and took a place a few paces from both of them, near enough to hear, far enough that nobody could mistake her for a party to it.

"Understand what you won," said Coss.

He went straight to it, as he went to everything.

"This morning the Compact kept a file on you. A thin one, at my grade, with a word on it that it couldn't place. A file like that is read once a season by a man like me with forty others on his desk, and he hopes it stays quiet." He said it with neither bitterness nor triumph, as a man reads out a sum he has already checked twice. "Tonight the Compact has a ruling that says its own schedule is missing a word. In public. On a record any clerk in the country can send for. And the ruling has your name at the top of it. My grade reads files. Everybody above my grade reads rulings."

"You're telling me I've made it worse."

"No," said Coss. "You made it *true*." He held Cael's eyes. "That isn't the same thing, and I'd like you to hear the difference. Worse would mean you'd have done better to lose. You wouldn't have. If you'd lost, the enrollment goes, and you go down the hill, and then out of the next place, and the one after, each time a little smaller and a little quieter, until one morning you're in no record anybody keeps. A boy in no record can be mislaid. Easily. By anybody who decides it's tidier." He let that sit. "You can't be mislaid now. Two hundred people heard your name read into a ruling this morning, and a dozen of them had come a long way to hear it. That has a price. Only a different one from the road you turned down."

Cael turned it over, as he turned over anything this man said; in two years he had learned that Coss did not put a clause in a sentence that he did not mean.

"You've thought about which price is higher."

"Since you stood up yesterday I've thought about almost nothing else." Coss said it drily, setting it down like an exhibit. "I don't know. There isn't anybody who does. Nobody has ever stood where you're standing; that was the whole of your morning, as I followed it. What I can tell you is which of them I'm able to read. The road you didn't take, I could walk you down a step at a time. It's in four files, and from the way you said *nine, six, eleven, four*, I'd say you've read every page of them. The road you did take has nothing written on it yet." He paused. "If I'd been in your chair I'd have taken it too. That doesn't mean I know where it goes."

"Then why tell me?"

Coss took his time.

"Because tonight I write the last report on you I'll ever write," he said. "It will say what happened, as it happened. I've never once put a false line into a filing, and I don't mean to begin on the evening a boy of fifteen beat me fairly." Something moved at the corner of his mouth and was gone. "You'll never read it. Nobody below the desk it goes to will read it. So I say it now, in front of her: it will be true. It's the last thing of mine you'll have. After tonight your file goes up past me, and the hand that holds it next won't stand across a table from you and give its name."

He said nothing more. In the quiet Cael believed him entirely, and the belief was harder to carry than any lie would have been. He had learned, in a borrowed office in Ardenmere and again on the worn stair in the nineteenth week, that the road out of Denvash had never needed anybody cruel to build it. It had only ever needed people like this one, doing their work well. He did not know what to do with a man like that except tell him the truth back.
