editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:7:"Custom," said Brom, in Coss's patient voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:15:"Custom," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:83:He did not look toward the rail. He kept his eyes on Karis, and he did not reach for an answer, because he had none, and he had learned long ago not to build one just to get out of an awkward silence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:29:The first opponent was a third-year from the movement floor, Current Path, Copper Rank Seven by the board, a solid square boy with a reputation for doing everything the way the lecture said to do it. Current moved like water down a slope, a long gliding step that seemed to carry a body further than its legs had any right to. The boy had a textbook advance built on it. Two glides in, the second one curving, and a strike off the end of the curve while his opponent was still working out which way the water was going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:173:"Current theories. In order of popularity."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-03.md:157:"Everywhere else puts the Copper students in one room and the Iron students in another. Here all the movement Paths share a floor, Wind and Current and Storm, and all the hardening Paths share another, Stone and Iron Skin and Shield and Anchor. A Copper Rank One and an Iron Rank Seven of the same family sit through the same lecture. You learn the family first, and then your place in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:9:The notices were ordinary: a change to the lamp oil allowance in the residence wing, a lecture on Current Path footwork moved from the third bell to the second, a reminder that the kitchen garden was not a shortcut. The instructor read each one in the same patient voice, and Cael listened to every word as if it mattered, because the alternative was to feel the backs of two hundred heads deciding not to turn around.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:241:"Everything I'm going to say on Monday stands on one thing. The text governs. Not what people have always done. Not what everybody's always taken a word to mean. What's written, and only what's written. Custom isn't code. That's the whole argument." He looked at her two notebooks squared on the table. "If I stand up and say, look, here's an older custom, here's what they used to call it, here's a road-house clerk who'd have written me down kindly, then I'm asking the panel to weigh a custom. Mine. And the moment I ask them to weigh one custom, I've told them customs can be weighed. I've given them the scales." He shook his head slowly. "And their custom is generations of enforcement in every district on the map, with a seal on every page. Mine is one clerk who crossed his sevens. If customs count, theirs wins. I'd be handing Coss the one thing he hasn't got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-04.md:193:"Curves of what?" asked Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:99:On the first afternoon her floor instructor had paired her with the second-year Current Path girl whose footwork never stopped, and for most of the hour Karis did not fight at all. She laid points.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:105:The Current girl moved through them as she was told to, one drill after another. Karis placed a point where the girl's left foot was about to land and let it go a breath before the foot came down. She placed three in a row along the line the girl would take to reach her and let them go one by one, a breath apart, so that the girl ran down a little corridor of fading warmth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:123:Karis did not. She did not angle her best work toward the edge of the section or lay her points a fraction cleaner. She did not even stop laying the late one that had hissed under the Current girl's heel the day before; she laid it late again, on purpose this time, and wrote the result down. At the end of the hour she packed her notebook, nodded in his direction once in a way that acknowledged he was there and offered him nothing to look at, and left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:197:She ran the Ardenmere sequences in the near corner, the ones she had run every morning for a year in a cold yard with Cael watching her across the circle and neither of them knowing what he was doing with it. Slip and turn and step and slip. She had run them yesterday and the day before, and the day before that, and every time she had felt them come out of her pulled tight, like a rope with something heavy on the end. Today they came out loose. She did not try to make them loose; she would have hated that, it would have been a performance, and she had never forgiven anybody for a performance in her life. They simply came out that way. On the third set her back foot did the work on the third turn so cleanly that she laughed out loud, alone in the corner. Two Current Path first-years at the next chalk looked round at her as if she had gone mad.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:75:But things did, and he had seen them do it. The Ardenmere circuit had run for generations on rules nobody had ever written into any charter. A market interview had been held over him on an authority that nobody in the square could have named. And at fourteen his own life had been turned off its road by one morning's procedure that everybody in the room called law, because everybody in every room always had. Custom in law's coat was nothing strange where he came from; so far as he could see, most of the world wore it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:95:"*Classification means designation under the schedule. For all purposes. Shall mean, not includes.*" The finger moved. "*The schedule names everything the Compact has ever reached for. Being named there is the only way the code lets it reach.*" Down again, across his ticks. "*Not in the list. Not in the annex. Not in the appendix, not in any amendment. Not struck. Not refused.*" She stopped at the circle. "*What the instrument writes. Then generations of treating it as though it were listed.*" And last, in the corner, where he had written it smallest: "*Custom isn't code.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:141:"Anybody like you." Karis picked up the bread. "Not yet. I've read four hundred entries and every one of them is somebody the old words could hold. Ash and Stone and Current. People nobody had any trouble naming." She tore the bread in half. "That isn't a finding. It's the shape of a shelf I haven't finished. There are a great many shelves."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:337:"Custom can be wrong," he tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:21:A chart hung on the wall behind the lectern, large and painted on stiff canvas, old enough that its corners had been mended twice. Across the top ran the seven tiers in their colours: Copper in dim orange, Iron in silver-grey, Bronze in warm gold, Silver bright as a coin, Gold a deep amber, Platinum white touched with blue with a small note beside it that said *historical*, and at the far end a seventh box with no colour in it at all, only the word *Void* and a question mark. Down the left side ran the Paths themselves, grouped into families and painted in neat capitals — Movement: Wind, Current, Storm; Hardening: Stone, Iron Skin, Shield, Anchor, Wall; Edge: Blade, Glass — and then Ember and Ash, Force and Pressure, Mire and Tide, and more below those, a long column of names, some of which Cael had only ever seen written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:205:The traveller registers were not all alike, and that was still the thing about them she found most interesting. The early clerks wrote a traveller down in a line. Name, if given; whence; whither; goods; beasts; paid. If the traveller was a practitioner of some kind, worth an assessor's look, there might be a word added: *Stone*, *Current*, *Ash*, old words, most of them, that she had met in the schedule's oldest pages and some she had not. Then, somewhere in the third clerk's years, the lines got longer. Not every one. But the waystation had begun keeping an assessor, as the bigger houses on the bigger roads did. When a traveller came through who could do something worth writing down, the third clerk wrote down what the assessor said, in sentences: what had been shown, and how well, and what the assessor made of it, and sometimes what the assessor could not make of it. Once, in a margin, in a different ink, a year later, the third clerk had added that the assessor had been wrong about a woman who came through in the spring, and had said so himself, and asked for the line to be marked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-15.md:55:Lira's second recorded bout of the month fell two days later, against a Current Path third-year with quick feet and a habit of circling left, and Cael watched it from the rail of the formal yard with his wrist wrapped under his sleeve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:35:The second bout went to her, and it was worse to watch than the first. The opponent was the second-year Current Path girl whose footwork never stopped, the one Cael had watched in his first fortnight. She came into the haze without a moment's hesitation, because she did not know any better, and the haze took her feet. Inside it her endless footwork became slow and pale and easy to read, and Gerda could have touched her four times in the first exchange. Cael saw her see it. He saw her not take it. She waited until the girl had slowed almost to a stop in the grey, and then she reached out and touched her breastbone with two careful fingers, as if she were laying a cup down on a table she did not trust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:39:She did it once more in the second exchange, the same way, from the same distance, without taking a single step that might have cost her anything. Wray called the bout for her at the end of the second. The Current girl walked off the ring looking pleased to have lasted so long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-11.md:91:He watched it reach her. Curiosity would never have moved Lira an inch, but this was defence, and defence she understood in her bones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:5:Lira was at the front, with her shoulder unstrapped at last and a purple-yellow bruise showing at the edge of her collar. Brom was beside her, and Karis, with her hands in her pockets and no notebook. Hobb was at the far corner, as always, with his hands in his pockets too. The other seven Cael had not expected. Two of them were Current Path second-years from the movement floor. One was the girl whose footwork never stopped, who had lost to Gerda at the review and seemed to have come to see what she had lost to. Three were from the heat-Paths floor, Ash and Ember first-years standing together. They had the shy bright look of children who have heard that somebody from their own floor is going to do something, and do not want to miss it. One was the second-year tout, who had no wager on it, because nobody was taking wagers on a re-certification candidate's supplementary bout at first bell, and who had come, he told Cael, out of professional interest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:45:The yard did not cheer, because it never did. But the three heat-Paths first-years at the rail made a sound of pure delight they could not keep in. The Current Path girl who had lost to Gerda at the review clapped twice before she remembered where she was and stopped. Lira, at the front of the rail, put both hands flat on the cold wood and leaned on them and let her head drop between her shoulders, and Cael heard her breathe out for what he thought must have been the first time since the first bell.
