editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:7:Yorlan read it in the voice he had used for everything, the voice that gave every word the same weight as the word before it, and the plainness of it was worse than any other voice could have been. It was the Compact itself speaking, and there was nobody left in the hall to blame for it but the Compact.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:15:"On jurisdiction: the charter's definitions clause is closed. Classification, for all purposes under the charter, means designation under the enumerated schedule. The panel has heard the schedule in its entirety, on this record. The designation [SHATTERED] does not appear in it. The Compact has therefore not established that the respondent is a classified practitioner within the meaning of its own enforcement provisions, and has not established authority to void the enrollment through this challenge. The respondent's classification status is undefined under the current schedule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:19:"The challenge is dismissed for want of established jurisdiction. The record is public. This tribunal is concluded."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:37:Quenna, in the row behind the table, had shut her eyes. As he looked she let a breath go out of her, so long and so slow that it seemed to take the whole winter with it, and she did not open her eyes until it was all gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:43:Across the boards, the Warden's last case was already closed. When Cael's eyes found him he did not look away. He looked back across the open boards, steadily, with nothing held back in his face, and bent his head. It was not a bow. It was a man who had lost, saying that he knew it, and that he knew how. Then he lifted his cases and turned toward the side door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:47:Nine days, six, eleven, four. None of those four had ever waited at a table for anybody to come back through a door; there had never been anything for anybody to come back and decide. Three of their files did not end, they only stopped. He had read them at the long table by lamplight and brought all four into this hall with him without saying so to anybody. Now there was a record of the kind none of them had been given. It had his name across the top, in the Compact's own voice, where anybody who wanted to could read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:57:Lira and Brom and Karis stood round him for some of it and would not let anybody close. The passage would have taken him a quarter of an hour to get through in any case. And under both of those there was a reason he did not put into words even to himself: there was one more thing owed before this day could be called finished, and he had known it since the Warden bent his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:65:"I read the enforcement chapters through once," he said. "At the regional school, when I was younger than your provost's clerk. Nobody told me to read the schedule. It didn't occur to me." He turned. "I'd like five minutes, off the record. I'd ask the assessor of record to stand witness, if you'll allow it. I'd not like either of us to carry this away different from the other."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:71:She left her chair in the row behind and took a place a few paces from both of them, near enough to hear, far enough that nobody could mistake her for a party to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:77:"This morning the Compact kept a file on you. A thin one, at my grade, with a word on it that it couldn't place. A file like that is read once a season by a man like me with forty others on his desk, and he hopes it stays quiet." He said it with neither bitterness nor triumph, as a man reads out a sum he has already checked twice. "Tonight the Compact has a ruling that says its own schedule is missing a word. In public. On a record any clerk in the country can send for. And the ruling has your name at the top of it. My grade reads files. Everybody above my grade reads rulings."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:81:"No," said Coss. "You made it *true*." He held Cael's eyes. "That isn't the same thing, and I'd like you to hear the difference. Worse would mean you'd have done better to lose. You wouldn't have. If you'd lost, the enrollment goes, and you go down the hill, and then out of the next place, and the one after, each time a little smaller and a little quieter, until one morning you're in no record anybody keeps. A boy in no record can be mislaid. Easily. By anybody who decides it's tidier." He let that sit. "You can't be mislaid now. Two hundred people heard your name read into a ruling this morning, and a dozen of them had come a long way to hear it. That has a price. Only a different one from the road you turned down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:87:"Since you stood up yesterday I've thought about almost nothing else." Coss said it drily, setting it down like an exhibit. "I don't know. There isn't anybody who does. Nobody has ever stood where you're standing; that was the whole of your morning, as I followed it. What I can tell you is which of them I'm able to read. The road you didn't take, I could walk you down a step at a time. It's in four files, and from the way you said *nine, six, eleven, four*, I'd say you've read every page of them. The road you did take has nothing written on it yet." He paused. "If I'd been in your chair I'd have taken it too. That doesn't mean I know where it goes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:93:"Because tonight I write the last report on you I'll ever write," he said. "It will say what happened, as it happened. I've never once put a false line into a filing, and I don't mean to begin on the evening a boy of fifteen beat me fairly." Something moved at the corner of his mouth and was gone. "You'll never read it. Nobody below the desk it goes to will read it. So I say it now, in front of her: it will be true. It's the last thing of mine you'll have. After tonight your file goes up past me, and the hand that holds it next won't stand across a table from you and give its name."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:117:"I did say that." Brom did not pick it up at once. He looked at it lying there on the scarred wood with its old creases, and then he put one big finger on it and drew it toward him and unfolded it, and read it through, slowly, as he read everything, the third exchange and the floor and Vell's capitals and the line at the foot. Then he folded it again on its creases and put it in the inside pocket of his own coat and buttoned the coat over it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:133:Oona did not come to the table. She stood by the stable door when they went out, with her slate under her arm and her tag polished, and she held the slate out to him without a word, and he took it and read it in the light from the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:135:*HE READ THE WHOLE LIST. IT ISN'T ON IT. I SAID THE NAMES WITH HIM.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:151:Coss had not minded that. Over three weeks he had come rather to like it. It had a narrow bed with a patched counterpane somebody had sewn with care, and a washstand with a cracked jug, and a single chair, and a table under the window just wide enough for a document case and a lamp. On the wall by the door a nail held a little framed sampler worked in faded thread, the alphabet and a row of crooked birds. Some mother, some winter, had sat by this window waiting for a child to come down the hill from a Kindling, and had passed the time with a needle, and left it behind. He had looked at the birds a good many evenings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:155:Every evening for three weeks he had sat at this table with the file open and looked up now and then at the dark bulk of the academy against the sky. On most nights there had been one lamp burning there long after the rest had gone out, low down at the back of the old building, where the archive was. He had known what it was from the second night, and he had made himself not think about what was being read under it, which had been harder than he had let anybody see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:157:Tonight every window on the hill was lit. He could hear, very faintly, when the wind came the right way, a fiddle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:161:*Final report of the officer of contact of record,* he wrote at the head of it, and the file's number, and the date. Then he sat for a while with the pen lifted, because a final report was a different thing from a quarterly one. A quarterly report said what had happened since the last. A final report said what the whole of it had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:165:He wrote the morning in Denvash: the station, the instrument, the designation it had returned, the registry's notice to the household on the second morning, the departure. He wrote it in the flat plain prose he had used for every quarter of two years, and it went down onto the sheet at the pace it always went, a subject located, a subject cooperative. He wrote the market interview at Ardenmere, which had been another man's, and the summons, which had been his own. He wrote that the boy had answered the summons truthfully, and that he had entered it so. He wrote the circuit, and the ledger. He wrote the quarters that followed, *nothing crossing the threshold*, every one of them true when he had written it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:183:He knew what the desk above him would want. He had known since the morning, two years ago, when a note he sent upward about a field on his own cover sheet had gone up the chain and nothing had come down. He had known it again every time the ruled bar had appeared on a packet in his hands, neat as a line drawn under a sum, with the small type under it telling him what he was not cleared to read. He had known it when his request to file in person had come back approved within a morning, faster than the man who signed it could have signed it, from a desk somewhere above, where somebody had been waiting. A report that recommended what that desk already meant to do would be read and filed. A report that recommended anything else would be read and filed as well. The difference would be that nobody would do anything about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:187:*The officer of contact of record recommends as follows. The subject is a boy. He is fifteen. He has answered every question put to him by this office, over two years, truthfully and without exception, including at deposition the one question whose truthful answer was against his own interest. The anomaly is the schedule. The respondent's designation does not appear in it, and the panel has found on the record that the Compact's enforcement authority does not reach him while that remains so. The officer recommends registry review, not escalation: that the question raised by this record be put to the Compact's drafting offices as a question of the schedule, and that no further action be taken against the subject on account of a designation the schedule does not contain.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:189:He read it over once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:191:It would be ignored. He knew it as surely as he knew the weight of his own cases. He had written honest reports into that silence for two years, and he would have written this one into it if he had known for certain that it would be burned unread. That was part of the work as well, whatever else the work was. A man who stopped writing true reports because nobody read them was a man who had decided the reports had been for the readers all along, and Coss had never once thought they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:197:He wrote about the town's bad bread, which had not improved, and the cold, which had, a little. He wrote about a little framed sampler on the wall of his room with a row of crooked birds on it. He told her he would be home before the month was out. He had never once written to her about a case, not in all the years she had been old enough to read, and he did not mean to now. But at the bottom, after he had signed it, he sat with the pen over the page. Then he wrote one more line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-59.md:225:The cover was plain. It carried no ruled bar and no field marked restricted, nothing at all that a careful assessor or a careful recorder had been trained to stop at. To the very few who knew how such covers were read, the plainness was the whole of the message. Along its spine somebody had written, small and upright, a reference. It was not a name. It named no boy, no town, no school on any hill. It named an article of a charter, and a schedule, as amended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:3:They told Lira and Brom at breakfast, at the end of the scarred table, under the outline of the old hay door, with their heads close and their voices down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:7:He opened the observation notebook and turned it round on the table so that the two of them could see the box with the lines in it, and Lira read it upside down, as she read everything, without turning it. Brom read it the right way up, slowly, with one big finger hovering just above the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:53:She built it in the stable, after the second-years had been driven off to the residence wing at the eighth bell by somebody's older sister, on the long trestle nearest the fire, out of books. She had brought them up from the archive in two armfuls with Prynn's leave: volumes of the enforcement digests, three of the charter, and the schedule's index volume for the top, with Prynn's hand-ruled concordance card, folded, tucked inside the index's cover. She laid them in a row along the trestle's far edge and then a second row on top, squared, with their spines all facing outward, until there was a low dark wall of books along the table with a gap in the middle the width of a person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:63:She had gone to Prynn on the Friday afternoon, she explained, while the clerks were on the toll books, and asked for every hearing in the enforcement digests where the magistrate's name in the margin was Yorlan. There had been fourteen, and Prynn had brought them down without a word and gone back to her stool. Karis had read all fourteen, and copied out, word for word, every sentence Yorlan had said at the opening of each sitting, and then laid the fourteen openings side by side. They were the same. Not nearly the same. Word for word the same, fourteen times, across eleven years, in nine districts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:67:"Read it," said Lira, from the bench by the fire, with her boots up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:69:"I'm not going to read it. I'm going to say it." Karis put the card face down on the books. "Stand up, please. The respondent stands to be named."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:75:"This tribunal sits under the charter, in public, and on the record. The matter is named in the notice. The parties are named in the notice. The panel will hear the challenge first, and the answer after. The panel will hear what is put to it and no more. The panel will not hear argument for which it has not been shown the ground. The respondent will state his name."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:105:They went on until the fire was low, and she made him cite the clause four times, and point every time. She made him read out the five places in the schedule, and then the sixth, the refused categories bound at the back of the seventh volume, which he had not counted separately until she did. She made him say the concession, the first sentence of the whole argument, eleven times, until it came out of him flat and plain and short, with nothing on it at all, as Yorlan would say it if Yorlan ever had to concede anything. At the eleventh she said *the panel notes it* and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:123:The clerks worked down the last two cases at the back against the old wall, where the traveller registers stood. Cael could see them from the door if he leaned. They took the volumes down one at a time and laid them in a flat box in a row, spines up. One of them read the shelf mark off each spine aloud as it went in, and the other wrote the mark on the box's slip. When a box was full they folded the lid over and tied it crosswise with grey string. Then they carried it up the long room between them and set it down on the flags in front of Prynn's knees, and stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:131:He read them off the slip. Prynn followed each one in a list on her knee that she had written out herself the night before, from her own catalogue, shelf by shelf, so that she would know without looking what ought to be in each box before it came to her. At every mark she made a tiny tick. Once she said "Again," and he read the mark again, more slowly, and she ticked it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:135:He signed the slip, and she held out her hand for it, and read his signature, and wrote under it in her own small hand, *Counted at the door*, and the date, and handed it back to be tucked under the string. Then the other clerk dripped wax where the strings crossed and pressed the delegation's seal into it. They picked the box up between them and carried it out past her knees into the passage.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:139:Cael watched them do it eleven times. It was slow, and it was meant to be. The clerks did not like it, and did not say so, and by the fourth box they had stopped minding and begun to read the marks out without being asked, in the same order Prynn wanted them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:141:At the seventh box the clerk read off a mark that Cael knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:143:He did not move. Beside him, on the other side of the door, he felt rather than saw Karis hold a breath and let it go again. He did not look at her. He watched the clerk read the mark, and Prynn tick it, and the clerk sign. The seventh box went out past Prynn's knees with nine volumes of the crosshatched sevens lying in it spine up under the grey string, and one of them was the register from the second-to-last case, with a faded brown smudge on one left-hand page where a traveller's name had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:155:"He did." Prynn turned and looked at him over her spectacles. "And when they come back up that hill I'll count them at this door, every box, every mark, against fifty-three signatures in my own hand. A thing that goes down on a list can be counted when it comes back." She looked at Karis, and at the hand still flat against Karis's coat, and something moved in her face that she did not let stay. "Whatever's been read can't be unread by a cart. That's the one thing the general clause forgot to cover. Go and eat something, both of you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:173:"Every one," she said. "In the same state it went out. Two of them in better." She looked at him over her spectacles. "You read the provision's boxes in their order and put them back in their order. Nobody does that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:177:"I tell everybody to." She laid her hand on the top volume of the pile, and then, instead of picking it up, she took her hand away again and turned back to her stool. "You know where they live now. Put them back yourself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:197:Havel did not watch her read it. He watched the window. In the glass he could see, very faintly, her shape against the lamp, and the white of the sheet in her hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:199:She read it once. She read it a second time. Then she laid it flat on the table, and smoothed it with the side of her hand, and took a printed form from her case and uncapped her pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:205:She wrote without stopping, in the small exact hand. When she had finished she read it over once, and then she brought it down the table to him, as she had brought the query, with the registry's sheet laid on top of it. She stood at his shoulder while he entered both, word for word, because the rule was the rule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:209:*Return, registry, to the senior evaluation seat. Origin of designation Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4: Systemic protocol, registry sub-layer. Authorizing official: none of record. Issuing office: none of record. Determination: no originating authority of record; designation predates file creation.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:215:*Referral, senior evaluation seat, filed in the matter of the Greyvane adjudication: query as to originating authority for designation Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4, appearing in the priority field of subject file. Determination: no originating authority of record. Designation predates file creation. No action available at this clearance. Administrative irregularity, referred.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:223:She did not go back up the table. She stood at his shoulder a moment longer, and he thought, for the length of a breath, that she was going to ask him after all. She had not asked on Wednesday. She had seen his name on the visit record and said *hm* and gone back to her bundle. He had spent three days waiting for the rest of the question, and it had not come, and he had begun to understand that it was not going to. She did not ask questions she could not do anything with the answer to. That was procedure. She had built her whole name out of it, one digest at a time, and had never asked anybody to give her credit for anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:227:And she read the designation a third time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:231:Looking did not change it. He saw that too. She folded the sheet along its crease and put it in her case and went back up the long table to her own end, and sat, and untied the next bundle, and began to read it from the top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:235:There were three people in the whole of the service, so far as he knew, who had ever looked at that line in that file and asked where it came from. The first had been himself, in a third-row desk by a window, with a manual open. He had been told in four lines to follow procedure and file his reports. The second, he thought, had been the Warden. Havel had never been told so, and could not have proved it. But the four lines had come back within the same afternoon, from a man who did not as a rule hurry anything, and a man who closed a question that fast had usually asked it himself first. And the third was a senior assessor with a clearance two grades above his own, who had asked it on the record, in the proper form, and been answered. The answer was nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:245:He read the first two before he wrote it, though he did not need to, slowly and in order, as the assessor read bundles: a market, a marker, *Noted*; a manual, four lines, *Asked. Told to stop. Stopped.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:249:*Greyvane, the delegation. The senior evaluation seat found the designation in the routing history of the autumn records request. The request's own authority sits two grades below what the designation needs. Designation predates the subject file. She queried its origin on the record. The registry returned: systemic protocol, registry sub-layer; no originating authority of record. She referred it, administrative irregularity. She read the designation a third time after she had referred it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:261:Nobody saw it, and he told nobody it was there. The next morning he sat at the recording case in the assessors' room with the brass glass on its side and read the deposition's fair copy through once more from the beginning. At the nine dashes after the single word he stopped, as he had stopped every time, and sat looking at them, and did not know why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:305:"If the panel finds the schedule doesn't reach me," said Cael, "that isn't making a hole. It's finding one. In public, on the record, where somebody who's allowed to write law can see it." He stopped, and found the last part, and said it plainly. "A hole in the law has one remedy, and it's the same as it's always been. Somebody writes the law. Nobody ever mended a hole by pretending to stand on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-52.md:331:"But that's the whole wrong of it. They've been walking people out of their lives under that word for generations, and not one of them was ever given a hearing, and now you'd tell me that because they did it so often, it was lawful all along—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:5:*Public adjudication. In the matter of the enrollment of the candidate on the unclassified observer track, on challenge filed by the Compact's field service. A magistrate of the Compact presiding. Assembly room, lecture wing. The first day of the twenty-second week, first bell. The record will be public.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:7:He read it through, while a second-year with an armful of practice staves went past behind him and slowed and went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:11:He read the last line again. *The record will be public.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:13:Somewhere in that line the fear in him changed into the other thing, which he knew well. It had come in Ardenmere the week Reydan's name went up beside his on the circuit board, and on the morning of the first sitting with Hobb waiting at the chalk. It was what fear turned into when it had been given time and something to lean against. A hearing had rules, and the rules had been written down by somebody, once; and whatever somebody had written down, somebody else could sit and read, slowly, until it showed him how it moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:39:It reached from the flagstones to the ceiling and was as deep as Prynn's arm. Bound volumes stood in it, dark-spined and all alike, row above row, and beside them the amendments were filed in thin paper fascicles tied with faded tape, so many that the ends of the tapes hung down like roots. The spines had their names in worn gilt. *Charter. Schedule of Recognized Paths and Standings. Obligations of Practitioners. Enforcement.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:41:He knew those words, because he had lived inside them since Denvash as a fish lives inside a river, and he had never seen them in ink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:45:"I'll tell you three things about it," said Prynn, "and then you can do as you like." She held the lamp higher. "It's long, and it's drier than anything else I keep. I've caught it disagreeing with itself four times, and there's never been a soul down here to tell." She paused. "And I don't believe one person who enforces it has read it from end to end in my lifetime. They read the part that lets them do what they came to do. Then they stop."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:57:He felt it everywhere he went. In the stable at meals the talk came in two volumes now, the ordinary one and a lower one that followed him from the hatch to the scarred table and stopped when he sat down. In the lecture wing an instructor lost the end of his own sentence when Cael came in late, and the whole room performed not turning round. In the main hall the notice had been read so many times by the second afternoon that its corners had gone soft as cloth. There were something over two hundred students at Greyvane, and by his own careful count every one of them knew that in three weeks a magistrate of the Compact would sit in their assembly room and rule on whether the observer could stay. A few were sorry for him, and showed it; others stared with the interest people give to a house that has had its roof blown off in the night. And some, more of them than he would have expected, and he counted them honestly, had started giving him a small firm nod as they passed, the nod people give to somebody whose side they have privately chosen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:63:He read the enforcement provisions first, because that was where the teeth were, and the teeth were what Coss would use. He read them as he had learned to read a fighter's declaration timing, not for what any single clause did but for how all of them moved, and he copied their shapes into the observation notebook as he went, one structure under another. They were dense and old and cross-referenced to death, and they were full of words like *voidance* and *sanction* and *removal* that he had never seen written down before, though he had lived inside every one of them. By the middle of the first night his eyes ached and he had nine pages of nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:67:It did not arrive as an idea, and it never had, for him, with a fighter or with anything else. It came by piling up: hour after hour of reading with the lamp hissing low and the cold rising out of the flagstones under the table, until the same small thing had happened in front of him so many times that he could no longer pretend it was not there. He checked it until the letters swam, chapter by chapter, and it held: there was not one power in the whole of enforcement that came in any shape but this. Authority *over classified practitioners*. Sanctions *proportionate to classification and standing*. Procedures that came into force *upon classification*. Powers exercised *according to classification*. He had read the word four hundred times in two nights. It carried weight in every sentence it was in. The whole of the Compact's reach rested on it like a roof on a single beam.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:111:"I know what they're going to decide." Her chin came up. "I mean it isn't fair that it's you. You're the only one who never once told me anything that wasn't so. I'm going to be in the gallery. On the first day. It says the record will be public, so I'm allowed. I checked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:121:"They'll read my lines aloud," she said. "At the hearing. Every sitting I've sat in. The Warden's had the first of them since the autumn, and he'll stand up and read them into the record in front of a magistrate and two hundred people."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:169:Quenna had not moved from the window, and the light behind her made her face hard to read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:187:"There's a word in the code I can't find the bottom of," he said. "*Classification.* Every power the Compact has over anybody is written on it. Over *classified* practitioners. According to *classification*. Upon *classification*. I've read the enforcement chapters through twice, and that one word holds up the whole roof, and I haven't yet found where the code says what it means." He had said this to himself in the archive so often that it came out level. "I'd like three weeks to look. Perhaps it means what everybody's always taken it to mean. Perhaps they've all just taken it, for generations, because nobody with a reason to look ever looked. And if it doesn't mean what they think, then this challenge rests on an authority over me that nobody ever wrote down." He put his hands flat on his knees. "So I won't defend the enrollment. You're right, sir; there's nothing there to defend, and trying would only use up the magistrate's patience on something false. I'd rather fight on the ground underneath it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-43.md:217:"Don't thank me yet." Naveth laid his long hand on the challenge. "And hear one more thing before you go down there, because nobody else in this room is going to say it to you. The Warden doesn't need authority over *you*." He tapped the blotter once. "He has it over *me*. Read his last page again. He didn't name you as the thing at risk. He named my charter. Whatever you find in that case about your word, Greyvane is still a chartered academy, and the Compact still charters it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:9:"Not like this one." Lira sat, tore the loaf in three and pushed the pieces across the table like a dealer laying out cards. "Ternhall. She came in at the gate yesterday afternoon with a trunk and a letter with the Ternhall seal on it, and the clerk at the standard desk read the letter twice and then looked at her rank for so long that the girl behind her in line started to worry the clerk had died."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:25:She was dark-haired and plainly dressed, in clothes that looked chosen once and then left alone, and she was copying the board. That alone would not have made him stop, because new students read the board all the time; he had done it himself on his second day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:27:What made him stop was the order she did it in. She had started at the bottom. The last names on the board, the Copper first-years with two recorded bouts and a loss beside each, were already copied out in a small, upright hand. She was working steadily upward, one row at a time, without once glancing at the top, where Edran's name had sat for longer than anyone could remember.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:29:He stood at the yard's edge for perhaps half a minute, watching her pen. She did not look round. If she knew she was being watched, she gave no sign of minding, and she did not hurry or slow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:47:By the end of the week Cael had begun to feel it before he saw it. He felt it as a faint pressure on the skin, like being read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:73:The hall laughed. Karis, in the third row, wrote *Ardenmere. Circuit. Unranked boy. Beats Iron. No Path* before the laughter had finished. Underneath it, smaller, she added: *Marlowe heard it third-hand and improved it at least once.* He was an excellent lecturer and a very poor witness.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:77:She had kept two since the winter she turned eleven. The first was the one everyone expected a serious student to keep: bout logs, timing tables, declaration notes, the slow honest record of a girl becoming very good at something the world had agreed to measure. When she Kindled at fourteen and her Arbiter named her Path as Ember, it simply grew a new section and went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:123:Ternhall's registrar received a weekly digest of new enrollments from every chartered academy in the region. It was meant for recruiting, and almost nobody read it. In the spring Karis had asked the junior clerk to set aside any digest that carried a certain name, and had not explained why. The clerk was used to that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:135:Her advisor took a full hour to talk her out of it, with the door shut and the weary patience of a man who had talked many promising students out of many things. He set out the sums: where Ternhall's graduates went and where a minor academy's went, and which guilds would read which name on a letter. At the end of the hour he folded his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:151:There was one person at Ternhall she told after the papers were filed rather than before. She did not record, in either notebook, why she had chosen that order.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:153:The coach south took four days. She spent most of them doing something she had never done for any subject in either notebook, which was writing a list of the questions she would not ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:155:She began it as an exercise, and by the second morning it was the most important page she had written all year. Every subject in the second notebook had been a thing, a timing or a curve or a shell that held too long against cold, and none of them had been able to object to being written down. This one could. If he was what the ledgers said, he had spent his whole life being written down by people who never asked his leave, and she had read enough of those people's work, at second and third hand, to know what it looked like from outside. She did not intend to add a page to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:163:*If he says no, the finding is no. Record it and go home.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:169:She went to the standard desk, because transfers went to the standard desk. The brisk young clerk there read the Ternhall letter twice, then looked at Karis's rank in the margin for long enough that the girl behind her shifted her feet. Karis waited. She had been looked at that way at every desk since she was fourteen, and she had learned that the look passed sooner if one gave it nothing to push against.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:183:The next name on the instructor's list was a tall, narrow woman of perhaps twenty with ash-pale hair and a training coat patched at both elbows, who had been waiting by the wall throughout without watching. Her tag carried the academy's crest and, beneath it, a small raised dot. Karis had read the floor roster by then and knew what the dot meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:191:There were four theories about the observer and, by the second evening, three about herself, and she ranked them all as evidence, and every one proved worthless about its subject and very useful about the academy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:207:Later, when anyone asked, and she was certain someone eventually would, she wanted the record to show that Karis Dellenmoor had come to Greyvane as a student and not as a hunter with a question. It would matter which. In her experience a difference kept carefully enough, for long enough, always came to matter in the end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:211:On the last afternoon of the week she went to the archive, because she had a rule about reading an instrument before she studied what it held. The observer was enrolled under a demonstration provision, and she had not yet read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:215:"I'd like to read the demonstration provision," Karis said. "The academy's copy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:5:She stopped at the end of the scarred table under the bricked-up hay door, where the three of them sat with porridge and the last of yesterday's dark loaf, and held the note out to Lira without a word. Lira took it, read it, and folded it again along the clerk's crease. Then she went on eating, which was how Cael knew it mattered, because Lira had never in her life gone on eating through good news.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:15:"The full record of the finding. The provision says the old academy has to send it before a formal review can sit, and it came up the hill on Saturday under their seal." She turned the note over on the table as if there might be more on the back, and there was not. "Quenna has it. The annex by the archive, the fourth bell. I can bring whoever I like, or nobody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:39:"She'll read this one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:41:"She'll read it," Cael agreed. He had watched Lira decide things across a great many tables, and he had seen her face when she folded the note, and he knew that whatever else happened at the fourth bell, she had already decided that much.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:59:"That's Fenmark's record of the finding." Quenna did not touch the envelope; she only looked at it. "What came here with you at intake was the notice that your enrollment had ended, and your standing record. That was enough to put you on the track. It isn't enough for a formal review. The re-certification provision says the panel must have the originating academy's full record of the finding before it sits, so I wrote to them for it three weeks ago, when your review went on the calendar." A pause, very small. "They took their time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:63:"It came up on the Saturday coach under their seal. Nobody here has broken it." Quenna laid one hand flat on her folder. "You're entitled to read it before the panel does. You're also entitled not to read it at all. Some candidates prefer not to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:69:"Then on Thursday the panel reads it without you, as the provision requires, and it goes into your file here with everything else. You'd find out what it says when anybody finds out what's in a file." Quenna's voice did not soften and did not harden. "Afterward. From people who'd already read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:77:She had not read the file at intake. She had stood in front of the clerk with the half-moon spectacles for most of an hour while he compared her thin folder to a sheet of rules, and she had stared at the opposite wall the whole time. Looking at the file would have meant admitting it was a real thing with real words in it. Not a thing that lived in her chest. For two years she had known one word that was in it. She had built all the rest out of that one word, as you would build a whole house on the only stone you own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:79:She knew what Cael would do in her place, because she had watched him do it with a category line and a cold cup of tea. He would read it three times and then write it down. She was not Cael. She was the person who walked into a ring first so that she could choose which way the sun fell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:97:She read them standing up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:99:*Record of finding. Candidate: Lira. Path: Wind. Tier: Copper. Enrollment: scholarship.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:101:*During a Wind Path evaluation the candidate corrected the examining assessor's technique aloud, without deference to the assessor's authority.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:113:She had been right. She knew that the way she knew her own feet. The examiner had been teaching the third turn of the evasion form to a room of Wind candidates, and he had been teaching it off the front foot. You could not leave a strike in time off the front foot. Your weight was in the wrong place, you left late, and against anybody quick you left with a bruise. She had said so. She had said it out loud, because she was fourteen and it was true. When he told her to show him, she showed him. His own demonstration strike went past her shoulder into empty air while her back foot did the work, and the room went so quiet you could hear the windows tick in the cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:117:She read the fourth paragraph again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:129:She had spent two years furnishing that room. That was the thing she had not understood until this minute, standing in a narrow annex with one sheet in her hand. She had furnished it like somebody setting up a house she meant to live in. She had added the jug and the cloth and the cold coming off the windows, and then, night after night, she had added the three faces, one by one, until she knew them better than her own. She had decided the man in the middle had wanted her gone from the start, because he had looked at her boots when she came in. She had decided the woman on the left, who had once moved Lira's grip on a training staff with two kind fingers in her first month, had taken her side and been outvoted. She had needed that. She had needed somebody in that room to have argued for her and lost, because if somebody had argued, then there had been an argument, and if there had been an argument then she had been a question, and a question can be answered wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:133:The room had never been listening, because it had never been a room for listening. Three people had sat behind a table because that was where people sat, and a man had stood up and read out a thing that was already finished. A form said it was finished. A form said it did not need to be heard. *Below the severity threshold.* She was small enough to be dealt with on one sheet, and there was a rule that said so in advance, written for girls like her before she had ever been born.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:135:And the words he had added, the man standing at the end of the table, when he had read out the one word and then looked up at her over the paper, to make sure she understood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:139:She read the four paragraphs one more time, all the way down, looking for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:153:"Procedures written for people who aren't expected to object are always short," Quenna said. "That isn't a judgment of you. It's a judgment of the people who wrote it, and the paper doesn't know the difference."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:159:"It isn't either. It's simply true." Quenna turned her head very slightly toward the door, where Cael still stood against the frame, and back. "What you do with it is yours. On Thursday the panel will ask you a different question. Not what you did at fourteen. What you are now, on our floor, against our students, in front of three people who have read that sheet and are about to watch you. The record says Copper. They'll be measuring your advancement against that tier, and nothing else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:209:"I read something."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:221:Lira stood at the desk with her own slip in her hand and watched her go, straight-backed as ever. She thought about the box at the bottom of the Acknowledgment of Prior Standing, the little square she had not marked at intake, the one that would have said to anybody who read her file that she meant to dispute it. There was still a process for that, through channels outside this academy, slow and expensive and full of more people who had never been in the room. She could start it tomorrow. She could stand up in front of somebody and tell them about the third turn and the man who had added a sentence that wasn't written anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:223:She thought about it all the way back across the yard in the cold, and at the door of the residence wing she knew she did not want to start it tomorrow. She wanted Thursday first. She wanted a floor to see her before anyone read her again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:235:He had stood in the doorway of the annex for the whole of it, because she had told him to, and he had kept his mouth shut, because she had told him to do that too. It had been one of the hardest quarter-hours he had ever spent standing still. He had not seen the words on the sheet. He had not tried to. He had seen her read them more than once, and he had seen her hand want to throw something and decide not to, and then he had seen her set the envelope down in a way he did not have a heading for anywhere in the binder. She had not put it away. She had put it down like a pack at the end of a road, and then she had walked out from under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:251:She looked at him, and then she nodded once, and went back down the corridor to her own door without another word. He heard it close.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:255:*Fenmark's record came. Lira read it in the annex with Quenna and me at the door. Four paragraphs, one sheet. She came out lighter than she went in. I don't know what's in it. She'll tell me Thursday, after the floor.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:257:He read it back and added a line underneath. Tomorrow was Tuesday, the first session since he had told himself on a frozen wall that he would find out, and he had not once in two days let himself think about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:3:The fourth sitting was as dull as he had meant it to be, and he was proud of every minute of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:9:"For the record," said Quenna, and the room went flat and slow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:11:He showed the framework, four passes, at panel speed. Wray broke the count on the third pass, as she had broken it at the first sitting, and he read Hobb's hips through the broken rhythm as easily as through the steady one. He showed the surface read, four calls at two paces with the last made eyes shut, and Hobb's weight went where Cael said it would go every time. On the fourth call Hobb tried to change his mind halfway through the lean, and Cael named the change before it was finished, and Hobb huffed through his nose as he had huffed at the first sitting, like a man hearing someone finish his sentence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:21:That was all. Four sittings, and the record said the same thing four times in slightly different words. Somewhere at the far end of a road, at a desk he had built in his own head under a lamp, someone would one day read four pages that agreed with each other and find nothing on any of them worth underlining, and he had spent a quarter of an hour in a swept ring making certain of that. Show enough to pass. Bank everything that matters. It had become a craft, as taking a hit on purpose had become one for Lira, and he was beginning to be good at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:31:Quenna stayed in her chair with her slate face down on the little table. When the door had shut behind the others she looked up at him, and her voice came down out of the record into the lower one she kept for things she meant to say only once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:59:"The panel does want that," said Quenna. She did not blink. "Four sittings of a framework and a read against a partner told how hard to hit. A careful reader starts to wonder what the candidate does when nobody's told the partner anything. I'd rather the record answered that question while I'm the one holding the pen." She paused. "It's also true. Both are true. Most things worth doing here have two versions, and both of them true. It's the one rule of this building I'd write on a wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:63:"Three exchanges," Quenna went on. "Faculty discretion to halt at any moment. I'll preside. Wray will take the instructor's seat. The rotating seat will be whoever the rota gives us, and I won't change the rota." She turned to Karis. "You're entitled to refuse. You're a student of full standing and this isn't a standings bout. Nothing goes on your record if you say no."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:69:Karis read it twice, as she read everything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:77:He read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:79:*I, Karis Dellenmoor, Ember Path, Iron Rank Three, a student of full standing, consent to a supervised match against the candidate enrolled under the demonstration provision, on whatever conditions the presiding assessor sets. I have read the standard waiver and accept everything in it. I ask that nothing in the conditions be softened on my account. I will fight at full intensity because a match fought at less would not be worth recording. I want to be the first documented source. If it works, the documentation matters. If it doesn't, that matters more. I have read this twice before signing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:85:Quenna turned the sheet round toward herself and read it. Cael watched her come to *source*, watched her eyes stop on it a fraction longer than on any other word and then go on and not come back. She did not ask what it meant. She initialed the margin beside Karis's signature, dated it, and laid it in a folder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:107:He had the sentence in the binder in his own square hand, under the four names. *It takes what's used on me. The stakes aren't the weather round it. They're part of how it works.* She had written *We are missing a condition* under a ruled line in her ledger while he sat three paces away with the condition in his chest, and he was keeping it there still, on a frosted path in the middle of the morning, with her walking beside him close enough to touch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:115:"Good. So am I." At the stable door she stopped and turned to him, and she looked, for the first time since the terms were signed, a little uncomfortable. "There's something I want to say about those four weeks. You've watched me at demonstration intensity for three weeks in a room. You've watched me fight Edran once. You haven't watched me build. I've watched you fight Edran, and I've timed your framework at every session since the first, and you've read every line I wrote about it before anybody else. That isn't even."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:133:He laid the fair copy on the table between the bowls. Quenna had given it to him on the stair as he left, folded once, without being asked. "It's part of your record now," she had said. "You read every other part of it first. I can't see why this one should be any different."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:135:Lira picked it up and went through it slowly, her thumb moving down the margin, which was how she read anything she had not yet decided to believe. At the underlined line her thumb stopped. Then she set the sheet down very carefully beside her bowl and kept her hand on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:179:"No. That's the first thing I worked out." Lira drew her knees up and put her chin on them. "Quenna isn't arranging anything for your binder. She's never read it. She wants a match because a match is what a careful reader would want to see, and she'd rather the record had one in it while she's holding the pen. Whatever else she thinks, she's never asked, and she won't." She was quiet a moment. "And Karis isn't being arranged. Karis walked into Quenna's room and asked for a clean sheet. Nobody put her anywhere. She put herself there and wrote down why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:231:"*By her written consent*," she read. "That means she asked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:261:"After she beat me," said Edran, without looking at him, "I sent for Ternhall's standings record. Theirs is public, if you write for it and pay the copying." He kept walking. "It goes back three years. At Ternhall nobody has crossed that grid for two years. I thought you'd want the number."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:269:*Quenna has put a match on the calendar for the fifth sitting, week sixteen, against Karis. Both versions true. Karis wrote her own consent and underlined one line of it. Quenna read the word source and didn't ask. I have the fair copy in the back pocket.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:13:He was not alone. Three or four students were nearby, doing other things very slowly; one was retying a boot that had not come untied, and two were talking with the particular attention of people listening to a different conversation. Cael saw the shape of it before he was close enough to see faces. It was not an ambush. It was an audience.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:19:"That's not what the category is for. I've read the provision. I've sat beside observers in lectures since my first year. Observers watch. They take notes. They don't get a panel and a closed hall and a training partner at first bell." He tilted his head very slightly. "So either the faculty spent a morning watching you do nothing, which I don't believe, or somebody wrote a form that says one thing and means another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:25:It was not hostile, and that was the hard part. Cael had faced hostile questions all his life, and hostile questions were easy in their way, because they told you what the person wanted and you could refuse to give it. This was something else. Edran sounded genuinely curious, almost academic, like a student who has found a hole in a rule and would like to know how deep it goes. Anger burned out. Curiosity kept a notebook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:27:Cael felt the yard around him the way he felt a crowd before a bout, the loose half-ring of listeners, the boy with the boot. He knew that whatever he said in the next breath would be in every common room by the seventh bell, word for word, and improved on by the eighth. He went through what he had: a form that said a thing its signers knew was not so; a binder in his pack that no one here would ever read; and nothing between the two that he could say out loud and keep. He would not lie. He had decided that long ago, on a different road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:67:*First assessment. Function shown: Wind framework, four passes at panel speed, one with the count broken; surface read, four calls, eyes closed on the last. Benefit: assessment satisfied. Wray's words, on the record: Unorthodox architecture. Consistent execution. No safety concerns. Cost: one strike taken, left forearm, third pass. The Compression fragment woke for it and I gave it no road. It let go cleanly. Bruise, about the size of a hand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:83:At the end she signed the session record, and then, instead of handing him only the strip, she took a second slip from inside her folder and held it out between two fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:101:He walked back across the yard with the date on the card fixed in his head, a purple stamp on a grey corner, the first thing that had come back up the road the brown envelope had gone down. It did not frighten him. It was more like hearing, from inside a quiet house, a door close somewhere at the far end of it, and knowing that someone else was home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:113:*Stone believes that if you refuse to move, the other person has to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:115:The defensive branch posted its drill pairings on a board outside the hall. Cael had walked past it every day for a week without reading it, because it had nothing to do with him, and that afternoon he stopped and read it. Brom's name was there three times in the week, and each time, paired beside it in the same clerk's hand, was a single short name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:137:It was not an assessment, not exactly. The re-certification track required its candidates to fight a set number of recorded bouts each month against students from the standard roster, with the results entered in the candidate's file beside an assessor's initials, and it was meant to be routine. The clerk who scheduled it had paired her, by the luck of the roster, with a third-year standing high on the Copper side of the board: a Force Path, tall and long-armed, Copper Rank Eight by the board, with a clean record and a reputation, Cael learned from the rail, for pushing opponents out of the ring before they had properly set their feet. He had no dot beside his name anywhere. He came into the formal yard with the easy, half-bored air of somebody keeping an appointment.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:141:Cael stood at the rail. Gerda came and stood two places along, arms folded, her ash-pale hair bright in the cold sun, and nodded to him without speaking, and a minute later Brom arrived, still moving carefully on his right side, and leaned on the rail between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:155:Gerda considered that, and something passed over her face that Cael could not quite read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:161:The first exchange went the way a procedural bout was supposed to go. The Force Path opened at the textbook distance and tested her with two straight pushes, each carrying a thickening of the air in front of his palm, a shove made solid. Lira slipped the first and let the second brush past her shoulder without committing to anything. She was measuring him. Cael could see it in her feet, which never quite settled, and in the way her eyes went to his hips and not his hands. The third push she let come all the way in before stepping off its line at the last instant, the Wind in her moving her a half-pace sideways as easily as a door swinging, so that the push went past her into empty air and the Force Path's weight followed it a little too far. She did not punish it. She stepped back to the centre, and the crowd made a small, satisfied sound, because that was what a procedural bout looked like: careful, polite, a testing of edges.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:171:The Force Path was not a fool, and in the third exchange he tried to take the fight back to where he wanted it. He reset to the textbook distance, five paces, and set himself properly, rear foot planted, and when she came in again he was ready for her and threw a push low and wide at her legs to take away her footing. She jumped it, the Wind lifting her a hand's width higher than her legs alone could have managed, and landed already moving, and he had read that too. His second push was waiting for her landing, high this time, aimed at the place her shoulders would be when her feet touched down. It caught her half-turned and drove her back across the ring toward the south chalk, three paces, four, until her rear heel came down a finger's width from the line and she stopped there with her arms out like someone on a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:177:He learned. Cael saw him learn it, and liked him a little for it. For the rest of the third exchange the tall boy stopped planting, pushing instead from wherever his feet happened to be, short, quick shoves with less weight behind them, which she could no longer read early but which no longer had the weight to move her far. The exchange ended with the two of them back near the centre, his chest heaving and her shoulder beginning to stiffen, neither ahead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:181:She did not let him have distance. A Force Path's push needed room between palm and target to gather itself, the way a door needs room to swing. She simply refused him that room: she came in and stayed in, inside his elbows, so close that any push he threw had nowhere to travel before it reached her and arrived as little more than a shove. He stepped back to make space. She followed, and slightly to his left. He stepped back again. She followed, a little more to his left. Each step he took to find room for his arm she turned into a curve, so that without ever once hitting him she walked him in a slow arc across the ring. Cael, at the rail, watched the chalk come up behind the tall boy's heels a foot at a time and saw that he could not see it, because he was looking at her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:253:Glass, from Edran. Stone, from Hobb. Shield, from Wray, from the way she stood and the way she changed one condition at a time, though he had never once seen her use her Path. Iron Skin, from Brom, which he had known for a year but had never before watched taken apart by someone better at taking things apart than he was. Wind, from Lira, in a formal yard, with a clerk's dot beside her name. Force, from the third-year she had walked off the edge of a ring. Ash, from Gerda, glimpsed once in a supervised block, a low grey haze that seemed to slow everything that passed through it. Blade, from a pair in the training hall who drilled every day in total silence and had plainly stopped needing to tell each other anything. Current, from a second-year girl whose footwork never once stopped moving. Storm, from a fourth-year off the movement floor who left the formal yard smelling of rain. Anchor, from a Copper first-year who planted himself so absolutely that two people together could not shift him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:257:Eleven Paths, watched closer than a spectator stood and closer than the circuit's crowded nights had ever let him get, because nobody's result depended on him and he was allowed nowhere else, so every scrap of his attention had gone into looking. He turned to the bottoms of the pages and read down the line he had added to each.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:261:*Stone believes that if you refuse to move, the other person has to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:267:*Wind believes the place a strike is going is a place you can leave.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:287:He read back over the two weeks before he closed the binder, as he did on nights that felt like hinges. Brom had a teacher, a woman who wanted to be proved wrong and had said so out loud to twenty people, who had found the soft place in him and offered to mend it. He was still holding his right side when he sat down and was happier than Cael had ever seen him. Lira had put something down at an intake desk and picked something else up on a formal floor. She was carrying both now, the signed page and the third-year's heel on the chalk, and neither of them had won; she was letting them share the bench. He himself had a name on a public board in the main hall, in the same ink as everyone else's, and a hollow circle on his tag, and a refusal in writing behind the stranger's note in the back of his binder, and a grey card with a purple date on it in a folder somewhere in Quenna's keeping. He had a first assessment satisfied and a second coming in a month. He had a question asked in the open by the best fighter in the building, and an answer that was true and was not an answer, and a strong feeling that the question had only been set down, not dropped. And he had a name he owed a thank-you to. Hobb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:17:*Session nine,* the ledger said. *Source: K., Ember, Iron Rank Three, demonstration intensity, consent given. Subject's reach: want, under irritation supplied by witness L. (pen withheld). Notice: none. Result: null.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:25:Brom, when he came, asked every time whether there was a clause about barley yet, and every time Karis wrote *Witness requests barley clause. Declined.* By the end of the week the line stood four times in the ledger, word for word, and Brom read it over her shoulder with deep satisfaction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:37:"No," she said slowly. "I can't." She looked at the place in the air where her last point had been. "Ternhall timed it at the start of sessions. Never at the end." She wrote something, and turned the ledger round for him to read. *Extinguish-lag lengthens under fatigue. Observed by subject. Not felt by source. Ternhall never measured late.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:91:*Lira has read what half cost. She is going to read this, and she is going to say something.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:99:She was not looking at his line. She was looking at a line far down the column, weeks off, in the clerk's small even hand. He knew what it was before he read it. He had been counting the weeks too, without ever deciding to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:153:He was at three paces. They had come in closer in the last hour, because closer was the only variable left that day that they had not run long. He had opened the read, the Iron-adjacent pressure-sense along his skin, not because it had any business with Ember but because he had begun to open everything he had, every channel of attention at once, on the theory that if something was going to come in, it might come in by a door he was not watching. The read had nothing to do. There was no weight in the air between them to tell him anything. Karis stood still and her points bloomed and died, and the read lay open on the surface of his skin like a hand on a still pond.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:167:"Before your points light. At three paces. I think the read picks up the place they're going to be. A thickening, where it'll bloom, before it blooms." He made himself say it exactly. "Three times in about a dozen. Not every time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:169:She let the point go on its count, and the shimmer of it hung a long time in the air, because she was very tired now. Then she bent and wrote, slowly, and her hand was not quite steady. She wrote it in his words, and read it back to him, and he listened and changed nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:171:*At three paces, with the surface read open, subject reports a thickening at the coordinate of the next point before ignition. Three in about twelve.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:173:"That's yours," she said. "Not mine. I can't see it, and I can't feel it, and I've never heard of anything that could." She looked at the place in the air where the last point had died. "It isn't an acquisition. Nothing happened to you. It's the read you already have, doing something to my Path I didn't know it could." She capped her pen. "Write it down. Don't build anything on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:193:That evening Karis read the ledger back to him in the archive, at Prynn's long table. She read all fourteen sessions in order, as the fourth clause said she must before any of it existed anywhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:195:She read it in her flat even voice, the voice she used for timings, and he listened to fourteen sessions of his own failure set down in another person's handwriting. *Distance nine paces. Distance one. Source's back turned. Declarations unannounced. Source tired. Witness eating. Witness withholding pen. Duration three hours and some minutes.* And after every one of them, the same word, written the same size, with the same care.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:199:He waited to feel what he had always felt, all his life, when somebody read him back a list of the things he could not do. It did not come. He had been studied his whole life by people for whom his failures proved something they had already decided about him, assessors and clerks and a patient official in a district office somewhere. When those people wrote down what he could not do, they were adding a stone to a wall that had been going up since before he was born. Karis wrote down what he could not do as if each failure were a stake driven into a field she was surveying, a small exact mark that said *the edge is not here, look further on*. Even his nothing, in her hand, deserved to be got exactly right. Nobody had ever told him, in all the years of being written down, that a person could be measured and feel kept company by it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-29.md:5:He did it as he did everything that mattered, on paper, slowly, and without letting himself skip to the end. He had read the binder backward once already, on the night after Karis said *aimable* at the stable table. He had found on every page what she had found from outside: every fragment had come after long close watching. That had been true, and he did not doubt it now. But fourteen nulls had just told him, in Karis's careful hand, that it was not the whole truth, because he had watched Karis longer and closer and more deliberately than he had ever watched anyone in his life, and no fragment had come, and no notice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-29.md:9:Wind-adjacent was first, and Lira's. He made himself go back past the notice to the mornings themselves, not the summary of them but the things themselves. A cold yard in Ardenmere before either of them had money for a hall. Lira across a chalk circle scratched in the dirt with a stick, teaching him to survive the circuit because if he did not learn he would not last a month on it, and both of them knew it. He had not been watching her from a safe distance. He had been losing to her, every morning, badly, with his ribs and his pride and the rent riding on whether he learned fast enough. Every slip she taught him had been taught by her coming at him with her whole weight and his failing to get out of the way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-29.md:13:Iron-adjacent was Brom's. Weeks of sessions in Brom's sparring circle, and for most of them the read had not come at all. It had come after he asked Brom to stop being careful with him. He had wanted to learn faster, and Brom had obliged, and for a fortnight every wrong read had meant a hit that rang in his bones until the next morning. He had been reading a body that was trying, honestly and on purpose, to hurt him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-29.md:23:*Watching is necessary. It isn't enough. Every fragment came when the person I was watching was using what they had against me, for real, with something on it — my ribs, my rating, the rent, the fight. Nothing has come from watching Karis show me what she has with nothing on it, however long and however close. Whatever is in me doesn't take what it's shown. It takes what's used on me. If that's right, then the stakes aren't the weather round it. They're part of how it works.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-29.md:25:Read back, it sounded like something true. It closed on the shape of the last three months like a lid on a box. He distrusted it exactly as much as he liked it. He liked it a great deal, and he had learned long ago that a thing he wanted to be true was a thing he had to check twice. It might be the missing condition. It might be a story he was telling himself because fourteen nulls had made him want one. No amount of thinking at a desk would tell the difference.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-29.md:49:"You wrote that something behind your eyes looks at people like a hand looks at bread. Three weeks ago, in that binder. I read it. Don't tell me about words." She did not say it unkindly. She turned her head and looked at him, and in the light from the residence wing's one lit window he saw that she was frightened, and that she was letting him see it, which she did not often do. "It never came while you were safe. That's what you're telling me. Every time, somebody was trying to hurt you, or beat you, or you were trying to beat them, and it was real."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-29.md:127:"I was frightened," said Karis. "More than I'd expected to be. I'd read everything there was to read about it, and I thought reading would help, and it didn't help at all. My mother came with me. She sat on the bench outside the station with her hands in her lap, and when they called my name she didn't say anything. She just stood up when I stood up." She looked at her own hands on the table. "Then it went very quiet inside. Quieter than I've ever been, before or since. And then I knew. Nobody had to tell me. There was a light only I could see, and words only I could read, and I read them four times, because I didn't trust myself to have read them right the first three."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-29.md:177:Wray had Edran on the defensive floor that same afternoon, across the chalk from Brom. Cael understood within a minute what she was doing, and why she had asked him on his way past whether he might like to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-29.md:189:They ran nine sequences. In every one, Brom held his wait on the second half of every redirect, and in every one he still began his turn before Edran's next structure arrived. The gap was there. Cael could see it now that he knew where it lived, the held instant between the read and the turn. Edran, who was faster than anyone else at Greyvane, never once arrived inside it. He was quick enough to make it show. He was not quick enough to use it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-29.md:229:Karis noticed, as he had known she would. At the end of the sixteenth, on the Tuesday evening after Wray's floor, she wrote without looking up, and then turned the ledger round for him to read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-29.md:231:*Session sixteen. Source: Ember, Iron Rank Three, demonstration intensity. Distance seven paces. Subject used to watch each point after it went out, as if something might still come. He no longer does. His state has changed; he has not said how. Not asked.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:7:Quenna had written hers in the record's language. Her page would stand only until someone unfriendly watched him do it a second time, and under it sat a question he had no answer to. *Whether you can not do it.* Karis's had been the stranger and the kinder, for she had spent two days searching herself for a cost, as patiently as she would search a table for a bad figure, and where the cost should have been she had found something she wanted more of. That was dizzying in its own way, because it meant that whatever lived in him did not only take; it joined, the join ran both ways, and none of them had a word for it yet. The third hand was his own and the oldest, the binder's: two misfires, with the date and the hour, a price charged in half-seconds of nothing for a thing he had done without ever being told what it cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:11:*Known. A directed acquisition is possible. Once. Under six weeks of close watching, and then a match where she was trying with everything she had, against me, and I had nothing left.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:13:*Known. It costs the others. About a day. Two misfires in the Wind, this time, and then clean.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:15:*Known. The panel saw an effect and not how. The record is drafted. It will hold unless I give somebody a reason to open it again.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:31:He read it back, and it was the truest line he had put in the binder since the week of Karis's hypothesis, and it was only half finished; he could feel the rest of it waiting. So he sat with the pen lifted until he could make himself say the rest exactly instead of bravely.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:33:*It isn't me I'm afraid for. I can carry my own bill; I've decided that. It's the three people who keep standing close enough to be in the room when I find out what the next one costs.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:45:Nothing about it felt like winning, but he had never before put a true number to something heavy, and the honest weight sat easier on him than a vague and comfortable one would have. Thursday had taught him that the reach was there. The two days since had taught him its price, and whose shoulders the price might fall on, and that there was only one way of carrying it he could bear to live inside: the slow way, counting and dating, with no glory in any of it. Taking was something anybody could do. The choosing happened in the ledger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:69:At the end Karis said, from beside the pump, "A quarter of a breath. The lead. It's back to where it was before the eleventh week."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:133:"I was on the front bench," she said. "I was close. Closer than the panel. When you went down on one knee." She looked at his right hand, the good one, and then back at his face. "There was a light under your hand. Not hers. Yours. I saw it. Nobody believes me. I told the girls on my landing, and they said it was hers and I didn't know where to look." Her chin came up. "I know where to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:153:Oona looked at him, and then nodded, once, briskly, and wrote something on her slate and underlined it twice, and he did not try to read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:173:"Lira's right." Gerda tucked the slip into her coat. "I'm sparring Hobb again on Tuesdays. Nobody asked me to. I asked him." She said it in her procedure voice, every word set down separately. "I lay the haze and walk out of it on purpose, every time, before he's even moved. It's the opposite of what I did for a year." She nodded once, formally. "I wanted somebody to know. It isn't in any record."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:227:"Because the shelves want reading," said Prynn, "and they don't much care what hour it is. Most of the people who come in here want one page and go away again. You read the page next to it." She looked at her cup. "Drink your tea. It's getting cold, and I don't make it twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:249:He read it back. There was nothing in it that was untrue and nothing a stranger could have made anything of, and the whole week was in it all the same, folded small. He wrote the Denvash direction on the outside and set it on the corner of the desk for Lira to take down on the third-day post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:265:It answered on the left turn and the right, on the third sequence and the eleventh, and on the double that Lira threw at the end, two pushes one on top of the other, the way Wray had broken the count at the first sitting, and it read both off her shoulders and slipped him out of both, and there was no empty pocket anywhere in it. It was quick and light and his, the oldest thing he owned, and the quickness in it was Lira's and always had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:281:*Monday, week seventeen. Third clean day. The framework answered every call, at three-quarter speed, double included. Lead a quarter of a breath, unchanged. K. counted. Lira's rule met. The new one may be touched from tomorrow, small, with somebody in the room.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-39.md:283:He looked at the page above it, where the misfires were written, and the columns, and the small box round the line he could not answer, and at the back pocket, where a consent and a soft-cornered paragraph sat side by side, and he thought of Quenna's question, which was not written anywhere, in any record, in anybody's hand, and was the heaviest thing he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:3:He took the binder up to his room that night and read it from the back to the front.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:5:He had not done that since the road. He read it often, but always forward, from the newest page he needed back to the first heading of the section it belonged to. Tonight he sat on the narrow bed with the lamp turned up higher than the oil allowance strictly permitted, opened it at the very last page, and turned the pages backward, one at a time, all the way to the beginning. The Greyvane pages went first, the eleven Paths and the line at the bottom of each one, the board and the tags and the refusal, the ribs and the post and the cloth. Then the road, and then Ardenmere, a great thick block of it, the circuit and the Ironyard and Vell's ledger copied out in his own hand. Then, at last, came the oldest pages of all, in the too-careful lettering of a boy who wanted his notebook to look official, since something official had just spat him out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:7:He knew every page, because he had written every one, but he had never once read them in this direction, looking for this one thing, and he found it on every page he looked at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:9:Wind-adjacent was first, and Lira's. Before that fragment came there had been months of morning sparring in Ardenmere, back when neither of them could pay for better, and through all of them he had given her the whole of his attention, because attention was the one thing he had more of than she did. He had written at the time, under Integration, that he did not know where it had come from. Reading it now from the back he could see where it had come from: a hundred mornings of watching one person turn out of a strike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:13:Iron-adjacent was third, and Brom's, and it had come after weeks of sessions in which learning how Brom's body read force had been, in plain fact, the thing they were there to do. Watching Brom had been the assignment.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:15:Compression-adjacent was fourth, and Reydan's. The hardest fight of his life had produced that notice, in the third exchange, with defeat close enough to touch: an opponent he had studied more closely than any before, and every last scrap of his attention thrown at something he could not name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:17:Under every one of the four, in the column headed Integration, he had written the same word, *partial*, and under every one the Cost column had grown longer over the months than the Benefit. Wind was nearly whole by now and had cost him only fatigue. Iron was short-ranged and thin against speed. Pressure had three clean uses mapped and a shoulder to pay for each. Compression was a quarter on a good night and a half once, at double the price. Four fragments, four tolls, and four names.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:21:He sat with the binder open on his knees and the lamp hissing and the pattern lying on the pages in front of him, so plain that he could not understand how he had never once seen it. Then he understood why. He had been living inside the entries instead of holding them at arm's length, writing every one of them on the day it happened and never once stepping back far enough to see them as a row. Karis had stepped back far enough. She had done it from the outside, without the binder, without any of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:27:For as long as he had had fragments at all, he had believed one thing about them before anything else: they came. The first nameless one, which had let him move as Lira moved in a sparring circle in Ardenmere, had come that way, and so had every notice after it. They arrived when they arrived. Nobody sent for them. A person did not pick his rain, or decide which storms would cross his piece of sky. He learned the shape of a storm once it had passed, wrote it down, and made what use he could of the wet. Cael had put it in the binder in those words or close to them more than once. He had as good as said it to Karis in section four. *Does it feel borrowed? I don't know.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:37:Then it had been closer to hunger. Call it appetite. Something in him had looked at people, and kept on looking, and taken, deep below the place where he could see it working. It had taken from the people he loved best, without his knowledge and without theirs, on a timetable he had spent years swearing nobody controlled. Lira, in those Ardenmere mornings, never dreaming that the boy across the circle was doing anything but learning to fight. Brom, holding out his arm to be read because Cael had asked him to. Feryn, who had beaten him fair and owed him nothing at all. Reydan, who had stood across from him in front of six hundred people and fought him straight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:79:She was standing very close, with her hands flat on the paper and her face turned up to his, waiting. Before the winter was out she would walk down the hill to the grey building on the corner, and an Arbiter would come for her, and she would have a Path and a rank and her first declaration and everything that came after. She would stand on a floor somewhere and use it with her whole attention while people watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:127:He sat through a lecture at the third bell, the declaration ethics block for the senior years, which he had chosen weeks ago because an observer could attend any lecture he liked. He did not see, until he was sitting on the back bench with the binder on his knee, how the subject would land on him that morning. The instructor was a quiet woman from the movement floor, and she talked for most of an hour about the ethics of sharing a declaration: when it was right to show one, when it was right to ask, what a teacher might and might not ask of a student. Cael filled half a page with notes he could not remember taking, and when he read them back afterward they were in his handwriting and he did not recognize a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:131:"If you learn something about another person's Path by watching them, closely, for a long time, without their knowing you're doing it. Have you read their declaration?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:139:He watched the Glass-work. He had watched Glass for a month and knew its grain, knew where the sliver lived between the second structure and the third. He found it in the nearer of the two third-years almost at once, wider than Edran's by half, and watched it open and close and open again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-22.md:161:He had come up off the defensive floor still in his training clothes, chalked to the elbows, his number sheet poking out of his coat pocket, and he had taken the length of rail next to Cael's without a word. Then he had leaned his forearms on it and watched two whole bouts go by below, so that anyone passing would have thought the two of them were simply enjoying the quiet together. That was as close to a method as Brom would own up to. He let silence open the case for him, and spoke when it had finished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-35.md:9:He read it slowly in front of the board, with students going past behind him toward the second bell, because Quenna had written it and he had learned that nothing Quenna wrote did only one thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-35.md:11:*The presiding assessor may halt the match at any moment, at the faculty's discretion, without stating cause.* That was for Karis: if anything happened on that floor that Quenna did not like, she could stop it without saying what she had seen, and nobody could ask her afterward why. *Logged as a demonstration assessment under the provision* was for him. It meant that whatever happened in the formal yard on Thursday would go into his record in the same column as four quiet mornings with Hobb, and anybody hostile who ever read it would count it as one more sitting, not a standings bout and not anything new. It fed the category, as everything he did at Greyvane had to feed the category or the category would starve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-35.md:15:"She's very good at this," said Lira, at his elbow. She had come up without his hearing, with a stolen apple, and she read the sheet once, fast, as she read anything she had already guessed. "*Without stating cause.* That's the best three words on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-35.md:25:"Three counts," she said. "Back to where it was before the drills." She wrote, and turned the page round so that he could read it, as the clause said. *Recovery three counts. Unchanged from the sessions before the sixteenth week. Cause of the earlier slowing stated by subject: Lira. Cause of the recovery: not stated, presumed the same.* She looked at the page and then at him. "That's the last thing I'll write about you before Thursday. I've decided."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-35.md:33:They did the post work, with the felt on and Brom leaning and shifting on the far side, and the read gave him four in twenty, as it had the week before. When the hour was done Quenna signed the strip and held it out to him, and this time she did not hold on to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-35.md:63:Oona found him on the Wednesday evening at the board in the main hall, in front of the conditions, which she had plainly read several times already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-35.md:121:"I'll be taking my breakfast at a separate table tomorrow. By the far window." Karis looked round the three of them. "Fraternizing with the opposition on the morning of a match is methodologically unsound. It introduces a variable. I don't want either of us to be able to say afterward that the other one was put off by porridge."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-35.md:181:"Twenty-two times," she said, "you stood at the far end of the quiet room and reached. I watched every one. I wrote every one down. I watched you try attention and invitation and want and stillness and all of them at once, and I wrote *null* under every one of them, and I've read back the ledger since more times than I'll admit." She looked up. "And every time, the whole time, you were watching yourself do it. I could see it from nine paces. Half of you was reaching, and the other half was standing behind you with a notebook, checking whether the first half was doing it properly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-35.md:205:"Then I'll write it down on Friday morning with exactly as much care as I'd write anything else. A null in a formal yard, in front of a panel, at full intensity. Nobody's ever recorded one." She lifted one shoulder. "It would close a door. I like knowing where the doors are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-35.md:221:When the door had shut he did not move. The notebook lay shut on the table before him. For once, he let it stay so. The reading room ticked and settled round him as old buildings do at night, the stone of the waystation letting the day's small warmth go out of it a little at a time. Prynn's lamp at the high desk had been put out, and only his own burned, low, at the end of the long table, and the shelves went back from its light into a dark that held more of other people's recorded lives than he could have read in ten years of evenings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:5:Someone had cleared it. The chalk lines of the six sections had been swept away, leaving only faint pale ghosts on the boards, and a single fresh ring had been drawn at the centre of the floor. The high windows let in a thin grey morning, and the air smelled of new chalk and cold stone and, faintly, of the lamp oil someone had burned before dawn to see by while they swept. At the far end stood three chairs in a row facing the ring, each the same distance from the next, with a faint chalk tick on the boards beside each leg to tell him somebody had used a rule, and in front of the middle chair a small table with an inkwell on it. There was nothing else: no benches, no rail, no crowd. He had fought in front of six hundred people at the Ironyard. He had never once walked into a room this quiet to be looked at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:11:"For the record," said Quenna.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:15:"Demonstration assessment. Unclassified observer track. First sitting. Present: the presiding assessor, the instructor's seat, the rotating faculty seat. The candidate demonstrates at his own election, under the panel's observation; the instructor's seat will direct the exercise partner." She looked up. "Begin when you choose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:27:It was an honest strike with nothing theatrical in it. The boy came in on a straight line from the left with his weight committed exactly as far as a textbook recommended, a solid, workmanlike blow aimed at the centre of Cael's chest. Cael read it off his body before it had properly begun. The read came the way it always came, not from the fist but from the hip, from the small settling of weight into the boy's front foot and the quarter-inch drop of his shoulder as it loaded, so that a half-beat before the strike had any speed in it Cael already knew where it was going to be. The framework moved him out of that place and into the space beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:31:The second strike came from the right. He read it and moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:41:He ran it again. The partner came in from a new set of angles, each called by Wray with a small movement of her fingers that Cael could see from the corner of his eye and did not try to read, because it made no difference what she called. The framework did not care where a strike came from, only that a body was about to send one. Read, move. Read, move. When he came back to his mark he was breathing a little harder than he would have liked, not from tiredness but from the effort of holding a thing at a pace it did not want to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:43:Between the second pass and the third, Wray raised a finger and held it there. She did not speak. She looked at the partner, and something passed between them that Cael could not read, and then she lowered her finger halfway and held it again, and lowered it the rest of the way at a moment that was not on the rhythm she had used before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:49:Cael knew within the first strike what she was testing. If his framework ran on the count, if over two passes he had learned the panel's rhythm and was moving on the beat rather than on the body, then breaking the count would break him. He would step into a strike he had expected later, or away from one that came sooner than he thought. But the framework did not run on the count and never had. It ran off the boy's hips and shoulders and the weight in his front foot, and those told the truth whatever rhythm Wray chose, so that the first early strike came at him and he read it off the boy's body as easily as the others and moved at panel speed into the space beside it, and the late one he read too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:65:He finished the pass — read, move — and came back to his mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:77:"Your lead." His voice was mild and dry and addressed to the room in general. "The half-beat. Is it the same against any partner, or does it change when the partner is faster?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:83:Then, because the room ran on exactness and he wanted his own words in the record to be exact: "It has limits. It reads the opponent's body. The quicker someone is, the less of his body I have to read from. Their weight shifts later, and for less time, and the faster they get, the less there is to go on. I haven't found where it stops working. I know which direction that is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:89:"Second demonstration," said Cael. "Surface read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:91:It was simpler to show and stranger to watch. Through Wray he asked the partner to stand at the edge of reach and shift his weight in any direction he chose, whenever he chose, doing nothing to hide it, and then he stood still two paces off and let the read open. It came in through the skin, as it always did — not through his eyes, though he kept them open, but as a kind of pressure along his forearms and the backs of his hands and the front of his body, as if the air between them had grown thicker on one side than the other. A big body carried a great deal of weight, and weight that was about to move leaned on the world a moment before it went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:115:The panel withdrew to the end of the hall farthest from him and stood close together by the wall with their slates, speaking too low for him to follow. He read what he could anyway, because he could not help it. Wray spoke first and briefly and then folded her arms. The rotating seat spoke longer and used his hands, sketching something in the air with one finger that might have been a curve. Quenna said almost nothing; she listened with her pen still, and twice she nodded before the speaker had finished, and he guessed from that that her mind was already made up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:141:It was not the voice she had used for the record. The record voice was flat and slow and built for clerks. This one sat lower and came at him more directly, and Cael had begun to notice that she kept it for things she wanted left off every page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:147:"Today proves you're real." She folded her hands. "Nothing more than that. It's the first line in your record. From this morning on, the file holds a candidate under this provision who did what the provision asks, in the provision's own terms, and gave a hostile reader nothing to take hold of. That was the whole task. You did it, and you did it well."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-07.md:151:"The sittings that matter are later ones. Those are the ones a person will read who wants you gone. This morning we were the writers. One day the reader will be somebody who has never sat in any of these chairs, and every line we put down from here on has to stand up in front of that somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:39:Coss sat at the middle of the far side of the table, with his back to the chart. His document case lay open in front of him and a clean sheet on top of the case, and his pen lay across the clean sheet. His aide sat on his right with both satchels at his feet. At the end of the table, on Cael's left, the young man from the coach had a sloped wooden case open in front of him, with a long ruled roll wound between two spindles and a little brass sand-glass standing at its top corner, and he had a pen in his hand already, held a finger's width off the paper, waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:47:"For the record," said Coss.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:49:He did not change his voice for it. He had never had to. He read the heading of the matter from the clean sheet, slowly, every word in its place: the deposition, the challenge, the field service, the candidate on the unclassified observer track. He gave the date and the hour. He named himself and his grade and his service, and the recording officer, and the attendance of the presiding assessor of the candidate's sittings, without voice, under the charter's procedure. Then he looked up from the sheet, across the table, for the first time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:77:"Before you signed it, you read the category line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:85:The question sat on the table between them, small and plain. Cael looked at it the way he would have looked at a strike that was coming too slowly to be what it seemed. Under it he could feel the other question, the one Quenna had told him would be there, waiting for him to reach over. *Why more than once?* *What did you see in it that you had to read it again?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:119:Coss took a single sheet from the case and held it up so that the light from the window fell on it, though he did not look at it as he read, because he had it by heart, and Cael knew he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:127:"It's a question of fact that needs a classification word to answer it," said Cael. He listened to his own voice from a little way off. It was level, and he was glad. "The registry never gave me one. If I make one up in this room, the record will hold my made-up word as if it were what I am. The instructor wrote what she saw, in her own words. Those are the facts. They're in the record already."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:135:"I understand that everything may be noted. That's what a record is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:145:"Before that sitting, was that capability entered in any record of this academy?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:199:He picked up the pen then. He drew one short line under whatever was on his sheet, as neat as a line drawn under a sum, and capped the pen, and closed the case. He stated the hour for the record, and the close of the deposition, and thanked the deponent for his cooperation, formally and correctly, and Cael believed, as far as he could read anything in that tired, careful face, that he meant every word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:213:He stopped too, because she had, and turned, and she was looking at him with an expression he had never seen on her face in twenty weeks: not the blank of the record, nor the lower voice she kept for things she did not want written, nor fear. It looked very nearly like a person who had placed a bet a long time ago and had just been shown, at an awkward moment, how much she had won.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:217:Lira was at the scarred table when he came in, with her boots up on the bench and a pencil and a chalk end and a folded slip of paper lined up on the wood in front of her in a neat row, like a fence. She did not take her boots down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:231:So he told them the formalities, and the name as the registry held it, and the three readings of the category line, which made Lira close her eyes. He gave them *a classification question*, and *that's what a record is*, and the ignition, and four weeks, and the question nobody asked. Then he told them how Coss had laid his pen down square and leaned back the width of a finger, and what he had asked, and what Cael had said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:251:"He'll read that into the record on Monday," she said. "In the first sitting. In his own voice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:273:The delegation had been given the assessors' room for its own use, and by the middle of the afternoon Havel had the long table's near end to himself, with the roll unwound across it and a clean fair-copy book open beside it, and the brass glass lying on its side where he had laid it at the close.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:289:She had asked for the case file's supporting documentation after the midday meal, the whole bundle, and the Warden's aide had carried it in in three tied stacks and gone away again. She had untied the first and begun at the top. She was reading it as Havel had heard she read everything and had never before watched her do: in order, every page, without skipping, past the place where anybody else would have decided they had the shape of it and started turning faster.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:291:The enrollment submissions first. The provision, in Prynn's certified copy, with the archivist's pencil at the foot. The first two sittings, with the assessors' names. Then the Warden's monitoring summaries, two years of them, quarter by quarter in his flat plain prose, a subject located, a subject cooperative, nothing crossing the threshold. Havel had read some of those summaries himself, years ago, when the file was a district file and he was a district officer. He did not look at them now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:293:He was copying the close of the deposition when he heard her stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:295:He did not hear anything. Something in the room had changed, and he felt it before he saw it. He looked up. She had the second stack open, and in front of her was a small stiff sheet he knew the look of without being able to read it from where he sat: a routing history, the record a request kept of every office it passed through, each one stamping its line in turn. This one would be the autumn's request for the academy's records, the one the Warden had drafted in his district office months before there had been any challenge to draft.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:297:Ilsev was reading one line of it. Her lips moved, very slightly. Then she read it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:301:He watched her, without seeming to, as he had been watching people without seeming to for five years. She put a finger on the routing sheet and held it there and reached with her other hand for the request itself, and turned to its first page. She looked at its header, where a request stated the authority it was made under. Then she looked back at the routing line. Then she untied the third stack, which was the subject's own file, and turned to the very first sheet of it, the one with the opening date on it, and looked at something there for a long time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:313:"Then I'll want an entry." She took a query form from her own case, a plain printed sheet, and uncapped her pen and wrote on it without hurry and without pausing, in a small exact hand. When she had finished she read it over once, and then she got up and brought it down the table to him herself, which a senior assessor did not need to do, and laid it beside his fair copy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:317:He read it, because entering a thing word for word meant reading it, and the rule was the rule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:319:*Query, senior evaluation seat, delegation in the matter of the Greyvane adjudication, as to originating authority for designation Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4, appearing in the priority field of the routing history of the records request of the autumn, in the subject file of the respondent. The request states authority at the grade of the field service officer of record. The designation requires sign-off two grades above. The designation is entered on the subject file before the date of that file's creation. Origin requested, of record.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:321:He read it twice, the way he had once read a classification manual twice in a third-row desk by a window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:327:"You recorded on this file before," she said. "I saw your name on a visit record. A district compliance visit, more than a year ago."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-49.md:333:He waited for her to ask him the next thing. He knew what it would be, and he had known since the moment her lips moved over the routing line. *Did you see this?* He had, in a third-row desk with the manual open beside it. *Did you ask?* He had. *What were you told?* He knew the four lines by heart. He waited, with the pen in his hand and the day-book open and the entry drying, to be asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:13:The three chairs stood at the north end with their chalk ticks and the little table. Quenna sat in the middle with the review ledger open and a folder beside it that Cael knew held one sheet of weak-tea-coloured paper. Wray sat on her left in the brown coat. The rotating seat was the broad-faced woman from the movement floor who had sat Cael's second sitting. Cael thought that was a piece of luck or of somebody's quiet arranging. She taught the movement Paths, and Wind was hers to read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:17:"For the record," said Quenna.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:21:"Re-certification review, formal. The candidate: Lira, Wind Path, re-certification track. Two recorded bouts before the panel, under standings rules. The originating academy's record of finding has been received and read by the panel. The candidate's recorded tier is Copper." Quenna did not slow down as she said it, and did not hurry. "The panel will assess advancement against the recorded tier. First bout. The candidate takes the south mark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:25:Cael watched her take it and knew from how she set her feet that she had read the sheet in the folder on Monday, and every night since in her head. She had come out here to make three people who had read it too sit and watch a floor disagree with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:43:The third exchange he tried to change it, and Cael liked him for trying. He came in short, one glide only, and struck off the end of it early. It was a sensible answer and she had already read it. She read it off his back foot before he had moved, slipped it so close that his sleeve brushed her ear, and was behind his shoulder when he turned. Then the fingers were on his back, between the shoulder blades, light as a moth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:47:The third-year stood for a moment with his hands at his sides, breathing, looking at the chalk at his feet as if it might have something to tell him. Then he nodded to her, once, a decent nod, and walked off the ring with the face of a boy who intends to go back to his lecture notes that evening and read them again with some suspicion.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:55:The second opponent was the Storm Path fourth-year who had stopped Cael on the stair before the exhibition to ask whether he meant to fight Edran close or at range. He had come over the threshold into Iron at the end of last term, and the board gave him Iron Rank One. That put him at the lower edge of the tier, and above Lira on every list in the building except the one this yard was writing this morning. He was quick and narrow and long in the arm, and when he took his mark on the north side the air around him already smelled faintly of rain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:75:He had not seen her use it since Ardenmere. It was a thing she had found in the market yard behind the grain exchange, where the circuit fighters who could not afford a hall trained in the evenings between the stacked barrows. The lanes between the barrow lines were so narrow that you could not slip anything wide. You had to go under. When somebody came down the lane at you with his arm out, you ducked under the arm and turned in against him, so close that your shoulder went into his armpit, and you kept turning, the way a carter turns a laden barrow on a lane too narrow for it. His own weight, still going forward, went over his own front foot, and there was nothing he could do about it. She had used it on half the market yard in her first season. She had stopped using it when the fighters she faced got good enough to see it coming.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:77:The Storm fourth-year did not see it coming, because nobody at Greyvane had ever seen it at all. He threw a release at her as she came in, and she went under it and turned, close, her shoulder into him. He went over his own front foot and caught himself on one hand on the chalk. Her two fingers were on the back of his neck before he had got the other hand down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:91:They came back to their chairs. Quenna opened the folder beside the ledger and took out the sheet of weak-tea-coloured paper and laid it flat, beside the ledger, where she could see both. Then she wrote, and then she lifted her pen and her voice fell into the flat slow cadence of the record, every word set down by itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:95:She read it off the ledger without hurrying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:107:Lira walked to the little table. She took the pen and signed, quickly, in her slanting hand. Then she stood a moment and looked down at the ledger, at the line Quenna had written, and Cael saw her read it, once, as she had read nothing else that morning. Her face did not change. Her shoulders did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:117:She went out through the gate toward the residence wing, holding the shoulder. Gerda watched her go from the end of the rail with an expression Cael could not read at all. Then she looked up at the clock above the main hall door, and went in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:121:He spent the fifth bell in section four with the post, as Thursday required, and Karis did not say a word about the morning. She timed him and wrote and read back. Once, near the end, when he was getting his breath by the felt, she looked up and said, "She took both of those on purpose." He said, "Yes." She nodded and wrote nothing at all, and that was the whole of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:153:It was the thing Karis had asked him about in the first week, the second half that he held longer than anybody taught. Cael had written it on Brom's page as the best thing about him, the patience that let a strike commit itself fully before Brom took it away, because if he turned too soon the other fellow felt it and kept his weight back. It was the whole of what made his redirect work against people who meant it. And at this speed, for the first time, Cael could see the shape of it as a shape. Between the instant Brom's body had read the strike and the instant his body began to turn it, there was a gap. It was very small, small enough to live inside a breath with room to spare. At Iron speed it was invisible, and at Copper speed it was a gift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:157:"There's a gap," she said, "between the moment you've read a strike and the moment you begin to turn it. You hold it on purpose. It's the best thing you do." She looked at Brom steadily. "And it's a gap. Against anybody you've ever fought, it's worth holding, because they can't use it. I can see it. I'm Bronze, and I'm not quick enough to use it, but I've stood in front of Silver, and I can tell you what a fast Silver would do with it. He'd be through it before you'd started your turn."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:163:"I can show it to you. I've just done that." Wray bent and picked up her coat and put it on and buttoned it to the throat, one button at a time. "Closing it is another matter. You'd close it by meeting strikes fast enough to punish it, every day, for months, until your body learned to start the turn before the wait was done without losing what the wait gives you. That needs somebody faster than the gap." She buttoned the last button. "I haven't got anybody faster than the gap. Not me. Not Edran, not the Ternhall girl, not anybody on this academy's roster. Greyvane doesn't have one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:199:"When Vell gave me the sheaf," he said slowly, "the last night in Ardenmere. Her copy of my whole record off the Ironyard ledger, every bout. She said, the records know you existed here. I didn't understand what she meant. I thought she meant it as comfort, because the registry had me down as a word and nothing else, and her ledger had me down as everything I'd done." He looked out at the yard going dark. "I think she meant it as a fact. Whatever anyone writes somewhere more important, the people who were there and wrote it down honestly, their page stays. It knows what happened. Nobody gets to argue it away by writing something different in a better building."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:221:He told it as plainly as he told everything, the gap between the read and the turn, the wait that was the best thing he did, a fast Silver going through it before he'd started. Nobody at Greyvane quick enough to teach him to close it. Not Wray, not Edran, not Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:231:*Lira's review. Two bouts, two won. She spent two hits in the second like coins and finished with the barrow turn from the Ardenmere market yard. The notation, read aloud, in their words: Advancement inconsistent with recorded tier. Recommend continued track with priority review. First paper anywhere that says her file might be wrong. She's keeping Fenmark's four paragraphs. No dispute.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:233:*Brom's wall. Wray struck him herself and found it on the seventh strike, the gap between the read and the turn. She called it a wall and not a ceiling and meant the difference. Nobody here is fast enough to close it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:59:It came through the iron-banded door in the low flat arc Brom used along the chalk, and it sat on the enforcement digests like a small sewn stone. Prynn looked up sharply from the high desk, and Lira was in the doorway, at an hour Prynn's card on the door called *quiet study*, wearing the face of somebody who had read the card and decided it did not apply to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:81:"The sixth sitting's tomorrow," he said. "Quenna posted it on Monday. And she posted a note under it." He could have said it with his eyes shut. *Under the charter's procedure for a challenge filed and pending, the Compact's officer of record may attend the respondent's assessment as an observer, without voice. The Warden has given notice that he will attend.* "He'll be on a bench by the wall, writing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:97:"Not him," said Cael. "The record." He folded the cloth. "Three hundred people saw something in the formal yard. Most of them saw a lattice fall down. A few saw a light under my hand. There's a story going round the stable that the observer made fire, and you've spent a month squashing it, and you can't squash it any more, because now a young man with satchels is buying bread for the tout to hear it told." He looked at her. "In three weeks, that story is going to be in the assembly room. If it's still only a story, then it's whatever Coss says it is. A mystery. Something the academy hid. Something nobody's ever written down because nobody dared to. A mystery can be made into anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:101:"If it's on the record, it's small," he said. "It's a thing with a size, that does the same thing three times when I say it will, at contact, with a rest between. Consistent, observable practice. That's all the provision has ever asked of me. It asks what I can do. It doesn't ask where any of it came from. It never has." He turned the cloth over in his hands. "Wray told me before the match. Nothing should be spent that doesn't have to be. Spend it on purpose. I think this is on purpose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:107:"Once it's on the record, it's on the record. Somebody reads it one day who wants me gone." He thought of Quenna in the empty hall after the first sitting. *The assessments that matter come later.* "That's what she told me after the very first one. That this was the one that would be read. I think she's been waiting for it the whole term."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:113:"No," said Lira. "It isn't. It's the other half of it, maybe. Doing it exactly as much as you said, and not one hair more, with him watching." She bent and picked up the coil of rope from where she had dropped it by the pump, and began to wind it. "Tell Quenna before. Tonight. Not at the chalk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:143:"For the record," said Quenna, and the hall went flat and slow. "Demonstration assessment, unclassified observer track. Sixth sitting. Present: the presiding assessor, the instructor's seat, the rotating faculty seat. Present also, under the charter's procedure, the Compact's officer of record, as an observer without voice. The candidate demonstrates at his own election. Begin when you choose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:145:He showed the framework, four passes at panel speed, and Wray broke the count on the third as she always did, and he read Hobb's hips through the broken rhythm as easily as through the steady one. He showed the read, four calls at two paces, the last with his eyes shut, and Hobb's weight went where Cael said it would go every time, and on the fourth Hobb did not try to change his mind halfway, which Cael understood was a courtesy. It was what he had shown five times before. He had built it to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:157:"The candidate will state what he elects to show," said Quenna, in the voice she used for everything else on the record, "and the panel will take note before he begins."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:207:Then Quenna wrote, for longer than she usually wrote, and read it out in the flat even voice of the record, and he understood as she read it that she had drafted every word of it the night before in her tidy room with the cold grate, and had then sat and taken words out until there was nothing left that anybody could take hold of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:213:At the back of the hall, by the wall, Coss had stopped writing. He sat with his pen lifted and his eyes on the post, on the three small black rings in a row on the grey oak, and his face did not change at all. Then he wrote one more line, and capped the pen, and closed the document case, and the aide beside him closed his own. As the benches began to empty Coss stood, and came along the wall to the end of the panel's table, and stopped, and spoke to Quenna, and not to Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:235:"It's dead," said Lira, sitting down at the scarred table with her bowl and his bread. "The story. *The observer made fire.* You killed it this morning. I watched it die on the benches." She tore the bread. "Nobody can tell it any more, because now it's on the record, and the record's the dullest place a story can go. Three little black marks on a post. A size. A count. The second-years were bored by the time Hobb fetched the sand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:243:"It's on the record as a thing with a size," she said. "That's the best place for a thing to be, if it has to be anywhere. I said in my consent that if it worked, the documentation mattered. I meant my documentation. I didn't think about yours." She looked at him. "Quenna's sentence is better than anything I'd have written. There's nothing in it to pull on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:247:"I saw," said Karis. "He was looking at the post. Not at you." She was quiet a moment. "He'll read the record more times than anybody. He'll find exactly what's in it, and nothing else, because there's nothing else there to find."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:255:"It's written down now," she said to him. "In the record. Three times. Nobody on my landing can say I didn't know where to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:269:He got most of a page down that road, out of a fighter's reflex: never step onto a floor without a second way off it. Then he read the page back, slowly, as Prynn read things, and heard what it would sound like in the assembly room. He heard Coss standing up with the first draft in its leaning hand. He heard Quenna on his first afternoon at Greyvane, over cold tea, telling him without any softening that the form's words and the truth under them were not the same thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:285:He read it back and did not cross anything out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:287:Then he went back to the fourth shelf of the second case for the rest of the enforcement digests, because an argument was only worth as much as the record it would be held against, and he did not yet know what the record held about people like him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:303:Beside the first three lines he drew a short stroke, because their files simply stopped. The sheets ran out as sheets run out when the system has decided that nobody needs to read about a person any more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:307:The fourth file closed on one line of notation, and he read it six times. Then he turned to the back of the observation notebook and copied it out whole. He counted the words afterward with the tip of his pen, one by one, and there were eleven of them: a person's whole life, folded small enough by procedure to fit on a single line. He did not mean to forget any of the eleven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:313:The letters shook, and he let them shake. Vell had said, at the Ironyard, with her ledger open, that a keeper who scrubbed the feeling out of a record only kept half of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:317:*If this wins, it isn't one enrollment. It's a crack in all of it, in public, on a record with my name across the top. Every office that ever flagged anybody with my word has to look at the fact that it never read its own code. Everyone who looks at that will know who made them look.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:319:He read the margin note back, and it was true. He wrote under it, in a hand that had steadied a little:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:335:"Not that," she said. "The whole of it. From the beginning. On my own." She pulled out the chair beside his and sat down in it, and set the lamp between them. "You've read it three times and found nothing there. I believe you. But you've read it looking for one word, and anybody who reads looking for one thing will see the shape of it in places it isn't, or miss it in a place it is." She opened the first volume of the schedule at its first page. "I'll read it as if I'd never heard of you. If I find it, you're wrong, and we'll know in time. If I don't, there are two of us who didn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:341:He sat and watched her read, her pen lying capped beside the book, her lips moving very slightly on the clause numbers, her whole attention gone into the page. Then he went back to his outline. They worked side by side at the long table without speaking until Prynn's lamp at the high desk had burned down to its last finger of oil. She did not put it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:345:He wrote the log that night at the desk by the window, with the observation notebook open beside the binder and the outline folded into its back pocket, next to Karis's consent and her soft-cornered paragraph, and the fair copy of the sixth sitting's record, which Quenna had given him on the stair without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:347:*Twentieth week. The sixth sitting: the new one shown, at my own election, three of three at a thumbnail, with the count between. Q.'s record: an ignition-point capability, contact-adjacent, single channel. Consistent, observable. C. watched and wrote, and stopped, and wrote one line more. He asked the date. He didn't ask anything else. Nobody did.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-46.md:359:He read the last line, and then, for the sake of accuracy, he put the pen back to the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:5:He did it at the long table before the first bell, with the lamp turned up and Prynn's pot not yet stewed, just as he had once spent the first hour of every circuit morning looking for the place in his own guard where somebody else would look first. He read the outline from the top. He read it again from the bottom, one sentence at a time, asking each sentence what it stood on. Then he took the page and folded it twice and put it in his pocket, because it had come through both readings whole, and there was nothing left that a third would find.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:19:It was all on one page now. The concession first, plain and whole: the enrollment was invalid as categorized, and he would not waste a magistrate's patience pretending otherwise. Then the charter's definitions clause, in its own words. Then the schedule, every part of it where a category could lawfully live, and his word in none of them. Then the question he meant to put to the room, which was not *why should I stay* but *on what authority do you propose to move me*. Coss held the other side of it. Coss held the drafting records and the three precedents, the autumn bundle with the first two sittings in it, and the record of the sixth, with its three small black rings described in Quenna's dullest language and entered as of its own date. Coss held everything that could be held in a document case, and Cael held one page and the order he meant to say it in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:33:"You were about to." Her mouth moved at the corner. "You'd have asked whether I'd found anything, and I'd have had to say *not yet*, and you'd have heard something in the *not yet* that wasn't there, and spent the whole morning listening to it." She settled the box against her hip. "A second reader who reports as she goes isn't a second reader. She's an echo with a delay. I'll tell you when I've read the last page, and not one page before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:45:Across from him Magistrate Yorlan was reading the thin volume he had read for two days, with a grey rug over his knees and a finger keeping his place on the turns. He was a long narrow man in the plain grey a magistrate of the Compact wore on the road and in the chair, and he had not complained about the inns or the bread, or said anything that was not a question about the next stage. Havel had recorded three of his hearings as a junior. In none of them had Yorlan asked a question to which he did not already know the answer, or raised his voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:51:He did not take out his notebook. He knew both entries by heart, and it was not a thing he read in front of people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:53:The first was dated a little more than a year back and written in a district office with one window on an alley. It concerned a market, and a boy of fifteen who had answered every question exactly and not one word past it, more settled than the officer questioning him. Then there was a marker in the boy's file that Havel had not placed and could not trace. *File and subject do not match. Marker origin not found. Noted.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:63:The officer of record was waiting inside the gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:85:"They'll be left open," said Yorlan. "The record is public." Students had stopped in the yard to watch them pass, and he did not look at any of them. "And who speaks for the respondent?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:115:"She read it again herself, in front of me. Her lips moved." It came back whole as he said it: the cramped office, the handbook, a woman of about forty going quiet over a definitions clause because a fourteen-year-old had pointed at it. "She said she wouldn't misapply a regulation to suit somebody's timeline. She said it to the Warden, through the wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:163:He went up the narrow stair at the back of the main hall and knocked, and she said *come* in the voice she used for the record. He went in past the cold grate and the unused kettle and the shelf of folders tied with tape, to where she sat at the desk under the window with her hands folded on a clean sheet that had nothing written on it at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:181:"Two. You cannot win a deposition." She said it flatly, as she said everything she meant him to keep. "Nobody has ever won one. There's nothing in it to win. You can only lose it, and there are three ways, and they're all the same way. You volunteer something nobody asked for. You describe something in your own words when the record already has better ones. Or you explain." She tapped the bare sheet once with one finger, where nothing was written. "Explaining is the one that'll tempt you, because you're good at it. He'll ask you what happened, and you'll want to tell him why. Don't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:211:"That was the second half. *Yes* was the answer. *I wanted to know if I could* was you explaining why, and now the record has a boy who went looking for ranked fights, in his own words, which the Warden can read to a magistrate in any voice he likes." She put her hands flat again. "Again. Before your enrollment, you competed on a circuit in Ardenmere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:223:Quenna held his eyes as she held a sitting's record before she signed it, a long while and without blinking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:225:"That will do," she said. "He's read every report ever filed about how you answer questions. He'll have planned for exactly this. So answer exactly like this anyway. It's still the right way. Only don't expect it to surprise him." She stood, which meant he should. "Sleep on Tuesday. Not tonight, I don't care about tonight. Tuesday. You never do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:231:"I know. I read your old file before I ever went to Ardenmere." Quenna was already sitting down again and reaching for the next folder on the shelf. "She'll read every word of yours and every word of his, in order, past the place where anybody else would stop. If there is anything at all in either case that isn't so, she'll find it." She opened the folder, and told it the rest. "So don't have anything in yours that isn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-47.md:239:He did not open anything, because he had nothing left to open. He sat with his hands on the wood and watched her read, and watching her read turned out to be a kind of work too. She read the way she watched a point in its last third of a breath, wholly. Every so often she stopped and turned back a page and read it again, and then went on, and he did not let himself wonder what had made her turn back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:9:A Storm Path fourth-year he had never once spoken to stopped him on the stair outside the lecture wing and asked whether he meant to fight Edran close or at range. A pair of Copper first-years from the movement floor asked, very politely, whether observers were allowed to wear gloves. A girl from the hardening family asked if it was true that he had fought on the Ardenmere circuit, and when he said it was, she said, "Then you've done this before, with people watching," and went away looking reassured on his behalf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:39:"He wanted you to refuse. Touts never want the answer." She pushed half her bread across the table to him. "Three to one on the stairs. I've been asking. It was four to one this morning, and then somebody found out you fought on the Ardenmere circuit and it came in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:51:*Sport. That's how they're taking it, not as a verdict. I keep bracing for the dread, and every time it's a boy wanting to know whether I'll fight close or at range instead. So this is a school asking its question out loud, I think, instead of passing it round behind me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:53:He read it over and added one more line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:69:Glass ran on discrete commitments. Cael had known that in outline since his second week, when he crossed out a page of notes about brittleness and started again, but now he watched it close enough to see the grain. A Glass declaration did not grow or bend. When Edran called a shell along his forearm it came up all at once, bright and hard and finished, like ice that had decided to be ice everywhere in the same instant. Once it was there, it could not be changed. He could not thicken it on one side or thin it on another, or turn its edge a little further round as a strike came in. If he wanted a different shape, he had to let the first one go and call a new one, and letting go meant bursting: the shell broke outward in that glittering spray that carried force away with it, and then it was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:71:It was the exact opposite of Lira's Path. Wind could be feathered; Lira could start moving one way and change her mind halfway through the motion, and the wind in her went with her, adjusting as she went. It was the opposite of Brom's, too, because Iron Skin could be laid on late and pressed harder as the blow arrived. Glass did neither. A Glass fighter chose before he moved, committed everything to the choice, and lived with it until it broke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:85:On the third day, in the afternoon, he went to the archive and asked Prynn for the standings record.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:93:"Standings bouts. Every one, every term, as the assessor entered them. Public record. Anyone may read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:103:It was a plain record, kept in columns like Vell's ledger: the date, the names, the assessor, the number of exchanges, the result, and a short line in the assessor's hand. He found Edran's first entry near the front of the section three years back, a Copper first-year of fifteen beating another Copper first-year in five exchanges, and beside it the assessor had written *Glass. Over-commits. Will learn.* The next entries said the same thing in shorter words and then stopped saying it, and by the end of the first year the line beside Edran's name said only *Clean*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:105:He followed the name forward through three years. It climbed the columns as it had climbed the board, steadily and without any great leaps, through the Copper ranks and over the threshold into Iron, and it lost only four times in the whole record. Every one of the four losses was in a bout that went past the third exchange.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:109:He read the four entries again, and then went back and read every bout Edran had won in more than three exchanges, and there were only eleven of those. Edran won early, in two exchanges or three; he pressed from the first count and finished things while they were still going his way. When a bout ran long, the record began to say other things in the assessors' small hands. *Late in the fourth.* *Lost his line after the third.* And once, in Wray's square hand, *Tires in the sequence, not the body.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:139:"Chart it. Clause six still applies. I read it first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:203:It was not Edran's mistake. Any Glass fighter alive would carry that sliver in a sequence of three or more, and the better trained they were the smaller it would be, but it would always be there, because it came out of the Path itself. Edran had narrowed it as far as three years of perfect mornings could narrow it. He could not close it, not without paying for the closing somewhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:205:The record had said it too, in Wray's square hand. *Tires in the sequence, not the body.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:215:The third direction was the record. He went back to the archive on the sixth afternoon and read the four losses again with Prynn's terrible tea at his elbow, and this time he knew what to look for. Every one of the four had been lost to a fighter who outlasted the ready pair in an exchange, and every one had been lost after the third exchange, when Edran was pressing to finish. The gap held from every direction. It was structural, not incidental, and it was there whether Edran was fresh or tired, alone or watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:221:So he ran Lira's circuits in his supervised hour, and the framework against nothing, and the slow shoulder work for the Reydan joint, and Quenna wrote less than usual on her slate. Once she looked up at him across the section with an expression he could not read at all, and then looked back down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:279:He had the binder open on the end of the scarred table under the bricked-up hay door, and he was not writing in it. He was reading the Glass page from the top for perhaps the tenth time. She came down the long room with a mug of something hot in each hand, set one by his elbow, read the page over his shoulder without asking, as she had read every page of that binder since Ardenmere. Then she sat down across from him and wrapped both hands round her own mug.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:293:"It's a good seam. I read it. It's real."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:297:"Then stop reading the page." She reached across and closed the binder for him, gently, with two fingers on the cover. "You've read it ten times. I counted from the door. There's nothing on it tonight that wasn't on it at supper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:299:He let her close it, and sat with his hands flat on the cover and the hot mug steaming beside them, and for a while neither of them said anything at all. The fire at the far end of the room was down to its last coals, and two second-years playing tiles beside it had very carefully not looked up when Lira came in, and somewhere upstairs a door closed. It was the kind of quiet the two of them had got good at, on more nights like this than either of them could have counted, across a table in some building that did not belong to them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-03.md:21:"Greyvane keeps a board. You'll find it in the formal yard, and every student with full standing is on it." Tap. "You are ranked by your record this term, within your cohort. Not by your tier, and not by your rank, but by what you do on our floors against our students in bouts we schedule and record. A Copper student with a good term can sit above an Iron student with a poor one. It happens often enough that the Iron students learn to take it seriously."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-03.md:37:At the end the tracks were read out, standard and re-certification and transfer, and then, after the smallest pause, "Observer," and two names: a girl Cael did not know, and his own. Somebody on the front bench turned around to look, and then a second person did, and Cael looked back at them pleasantly until they turned away again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-03.md:71:"No. But it's the right thing to wonder, and I'd think less of you if you hadn't." He turned in his chair, ran a finger along the brown books without looking at them until it stopped on one, drew it out, and laid it open on the blotter facing Cael. "Before I explain anything, read me an entry. Any entry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-03.md:75:"Copper Rank Two at intake," Cael read. "Kindled at sixteen. Left at Bronze Rank Three. Twenty-four."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-03.md:103:Cael sat in the plain chair and understood what he had been given, and what he had not. It was not warmth. In the first months after Denvash he had wanted warmth badly, had wanted some grown person to be moved by a boy's bad luck, and it had gone the same way every time. On the day of his Kindling the certification office had been gentle with him. The clerk had lowered her voice. She had apologized for the classification while her other hand stamped it. Then the appointment ended, and the gentleness ended with it, on the step outside, at the exact moment the door shut. Naveth was offering something else. He had opened a book of numbers and shown Cael why he was worth having, and that steadied Cael more than kindness would have. Anybody could call kindness back without warning. An incentive came with its terms written down, and you could read the terms, and you could see from a long way off when they were going to run out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-03.md:111:"Then I was wrong, and the school lives through it. It has before. Plenty of names in those books went nowhere much. A few washed out before the first frost." His palm rested on the cover. "That's the half people forget. We take the students other schools priced wrong, knowing from the start that some of those bets won't come in. The losses don't sink us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-03.md:157:"Everywhere else puts the Copper students in one room and the Iron students in another. Here all the movement Paths share a floor, Wind and Current and Storm, and all the hardening Paths share another, Stone and Iron Skin and Shield and Anchor. A Copper Rank One and an Iron Rank Seven of the same family sit through the same lecture. You learn the family first, and then your place in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-03.md:159:Cael wrote that down twice, in two different places, because it seemed important twice. Every other academy he had ever read about sorted its students by tier. Tier was what institutions ranked people by, the number on a file that decided which doors a person could go through. Sorting by family meant that this place cared more about what a student could come to understand than about where he currently stood on the ladder. He thought it was Naveth's ledger again, written into a building.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-03.md:167:Cael stood at the edge of it longer than the others and tried to picture himself in the ring, alone, in front of a panel. It ought to have frightened him, and it did not, very much. Whatever happened inside that chalk would happen under rules written down where anyone could read them, and hidden rules had hurt him far more often than hard ones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-03.md:183:Prynn looked at him — not at the group, at him — with a frank, weighing look like the one Naveth had given him across the desk. Naveth had been weighing a risk. She seemed to be weighing whether he could read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-03.md:201:A girl of about thirteen stopped beside him, and after a moment he knew her: the other name read out under *Observer* that morning. She was small and dark-haired, with a slate hugged to her chest, and she read the board with her lips moving until she found something and frowned at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:7:"I'd like to do the read today," Cael said. "Not the framework."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:9:"The read needs a body," said Quenna.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:17:So Brom came over the rail into the section and stood two paces from Cael and shifted his weight wherever he liked, whenever he liked, doing nothing to hide it, and Cael stood still and let the read open and called it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:25:Brom was a better body for it than Hobb had been, because Cael had read Brom for most of a year and knew the grain of him, and a worse one, because Brom also knew what Cael was doing and kept trying, with a scowl of deep concentration, to beat it. He leaned one way and went the other. He started forward and stopped and went back. He stood perfectly still for a long count and then shifted with no warning at all. Cael called every one of them, and called most of them before Brom had properly decided, and after the twentieth Brom straightened up and said, with real indignation, "That isn't fair," and Lira was not there to laugh at him, so Cael did it for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:41:The page was dense with her small upright hand, columns of calls and times and Brom's weight in a shorthand he could not quite read. In the margin, at two places, she had drawn a short vertical stroke. Beside the first stroke was a page number, and beside the second the same number again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:51:"It stops when something I'm seeing now agrees with something I wrote down before," she said. "It doesn't happen very often. When it does, I stop writing for a moment so that I don't write down what I expect instead of what I see." She touched the page number. "That's the page I wrote at your first session, when you ran the framework with nobody striking. You make up your own strikes out of the air. So I timed the gap between your eyes settling on where a strike would come from and your feet going. Today I timed how far ahead of Brom's weight you called it." She turned the notebook back toward herself. "It's the same amount. A quarter of a breath, near enough. Two different things you do, with two different results, and they both lead by the same margin."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:61:"It fits something," she agreed. "When I know what, you'll read it first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:65:The afternoon had turned cold. The leaves on the hill below the wall had gone yellow at the edges in the last week, and the wind coming up over the training yard smelled of smoke from the town and of the first frost that had not arrived yet. Karis walked with her notebook held against her chest and her free hand in her pocket, and neither of them said anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:79:"He read the floor correctly," she said. "Every line of it. He's very good." She looked out at the yellowing hill. "I'd rather he'd crossed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:97:He thought about it. The binder was the one thing the first clause kept from her, and she had promised never to ask for it, and he did not mean to make her break the promise by accident. So he opened it at the page, kept it turned toward himself, and read the chart aloud to her, line by line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:99:She listened standing in the cold, with her lips pressed together, all the way down: the two points at contact, the six behind her, the lag on every release, the nine on the floor, the channels, the yield, and at the bottom, *She didn't spend anything she couldn't spare.* She asked him to read the last line again, and he did. Then he closed the binder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:105:"Ternhall thought so." She looked at him with an expression he could not read at all. "You count the way I count," she said. "I didn't know anyone else did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:117:Cael was at the edge of the open floor during the fifth bell, watching a pair of Storm Path fourth-years trade pressure across a chalk ring and trying to work out why the air between them kept smelling of rain. He did not see Edran come in. He only became aware that someone had stopped beside him at the rail, closer than a stranger would have stood and not as close as a friend, and when he looked round it was Edran, with his arms folded on the rail and his eyes on the Storm pair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:131:Edran let out a breath through his nose, slowly. "That's what I saw too." He watched one of the Storm fighters stagger and recover. "I've been trying all night to decide whether I yielded because I read the floor right or because I was afraid of how it would look written down. *Crossed a channel, touch. Crossed a second, touch.* In Wray's hand. With a date." He was quiet a moment. "I still don't know. I think I read the floor right. I'll never find out, because I didn't cross."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:149:"I came to Greyvane at fifteen," Edran said, "and I've earned every line on that board, in public, bout by bout, against everyone they put across the chalk from me. Every one of those lines has a date and an assessor's initials beside it. I've been first since the end of my first year." He looked back at the ring. "Yesterday a transfer took it. I don't mind that the way you'd think. She beat me on the floor, in front of forty people, and the board wrote it down, and that's what the board is for. I can live with it. I'll take it back or I won't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:173:Brom opened the notebook at the page and turned it. Beneath the struck paragraph, below the younger hand's *Strike until there is one*, someone had added a line in a hand Cael did not know, square and plain, every letter separate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:177:"She read the whole page first," Brom said. "Twice. Standing up, in the middle of the floor, with Hobb waiting and the fetch drill stopped. Then she asked me whose it was, and I told her, and she said, *Ternhall*, the way you'd say the name of a good knife." He closed the book again. "Then she said a fourteen-year-old with no body to test it on had got further than half the instructors she'd trained under, and that if I meant to prove it I'd better start keeping numbers like the fourteen-year-old did, because she wasn't redrawing a curve on the strength of my feelings."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:239:The petition was three pages, pinned in a row with brass tacks beside the calendar, written in a clear, careful hand that did not shake. Across the top of the first page, in capitals:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:243:He read it all, from the first line to the last, before he let himself think anything about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:245:The first page set out the provision. It did so accurately. It quoted the half page Cael had read more times than he had counted in the archive, and then explained, in plain sentences, what the provision was for: the academic exposure and supervised training of candidates still waiting on their formal Kindling assessment, which was to say, in nearly every case, children. It noted that such candidates were assessed monthly by demonstration, and that the standard was consistent, observable practice. It observed, correctly, that a child who had not yet Kindled would ordinarily have very little to practise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:247:The second page set out the record. It did that accurately too. It noted the dates of two closed sittings and the finding of each, *assessment satisfied*, which the public calendar had recorded. It noted that the candidate had been seen at the rail of every floor in the building, filling a binder, and had been present at the standings bouts of the cohort's leading students. It said, without any heat at all, that whatever the candidate had demonstrated to the panel in closed session, it could not have been the practice of a child without a Path, because the panel had found it consistent and observable and the provision did not reward nothing. And then it drew its conclusion, in a sentence Cael read three times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:263:She read on. She was a slow and thorough reader, and he waited for her. Somewhere behind the lecture wing the first bell began to ring, slow and unhurried, and the crowd came apart reluctantly round them, in twos and threes, still talking, until the hall had nearly emptied and the two of them stood alone at the board with the brass tacks. When she reached the bottom of the third page she frowned at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:277:Oona read the sentence again. "Aren't they?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-16.md:291:Cael did not answer her straight away. He read the conclusion on the second page one more time, the way he had once read a category line three times over a cup of tea going cold, and did not resent a word of it. Edran had asked in the open, in the yard, in front of a dozen people. Now he had asked again, in ink, in front of everyone, with his own name signed at the bottom, by the book and in daylight. It was the most honest thing anyone at Greyvane had done about him, apart from a girl who had walked into an archive to ask permission. It was right about the provision, and right about the children, and right about the board, and right about him in every particular but one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:17:The grey-bearded assessor had laid his pen down and was looking at the row of shut volumes on the respondent's table as if somebody had just introduced him to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:29:"Four, in the Compact's own digests, in all the years they go back, who carried this word. I've read their files, one by one, at the long table downstairs. The first was ordered out of the place they lived nine days after the Kindling that flagged them. The second in six. The third in eleven. The fourth in four." He kept his eyes on Yorlan's. "Not one of them had a hearing. Not one of them was asked anything that their file sets down. In every one, an order went out under an authority nobody looked at, because the word sat on the form in the place where a classification goes, and everybody who handled it trusted that somebody before them had checked. Three of those files don't end. They stop. I'm the fifth. I'm the first who's ever been let stand in a room like this and put the question out loud."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:33:"So I want to say exactly what I'm asking, and not one thing more, because being exact is all that's earned me this floor. I'm not asking the panel to strike the word, or to say it was wrong of the Compact to worry about whatever the word was meant to warn it of. What I am isn't the question I've brought. Nobody on that bench could answer it, or anybody in this hall, or any file; every record there is about people like me agrees on that much, if it agrees on nothing else." He lifted his hand off the books. "I'm asking something narrower, and harder to set aside. A word that isn't in the schedule isn't a classification. A person who holds no classification is outside the reach of powers that, in their own words, reach only the classified. So this tribunal can't void my enrollment. Not because the enrollment is sound; I've given the Warden that it isn't. Because the hand stretched out to void it was never given an arm long enough. I didn't build that. The Compact built it, and wrote it down, and kept it. All I've done is read what it kept."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:51:"The officer of record may answer," said Yorlan.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:55:Every paper in the Warden's cases had been given away an hour ago, before he could use it. He stood now behind a table of good, true documents with nothing on it to read from but himself. He did not look down at the cases. He looked at the bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:57:"The respondent has read the panel an empty line," said Coss, "and asked it to see a locked gate there. The Compact asks the panel to see what it is: a line nobody ever thought wanted filling." His voice was as level as it had been all through yesterday, and as unhurried. Only a man who had listened to it every quarter for two years would have heard that it was finding each sentence as it went. "The word isn't new. It is as old as the instruments that write it. Every officer of the Compact who has stood in front of one of these cases has handled the word as marking something real that wants watching. The instrument enters it. The registry files it. The enforcement offices act on it. Taken together, over every year the digests run, that has been classification to the people who carry the Compact's work, in every way that touches their work. And each committee that amended the schedule knew the word was there. Each of them could have written it in. None of them saw any need. Nobody doubted where it stood."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:61:"And the panel should be plain with itself about what the respondent asks it to write down," said Coss. "He asks it to find that the people who founded the Compact made, on purpose, a place in it where no rule runs. A person who owes nothing. Watched by nobody, answerable to nobody, past the reach of any office the Compact keeps or ever could. The charter can't sensibly be read to mean that. And a finding that it does would tell every district in the country that the Compact's arm stops short exactly where the Compact is most afraid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:67:He did not stand. The officer of record had answered, and the floor was the bench's now. He sat with his hands flat on the wood and the answer ready in him, worn smooth by a week of nights, and waited to see whether anybody would ask him for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:79:"Undefined, Assessor," he said. "What I was when I woke up today. What I've been every morning since the station in Denvash. Only, tomorrow the record would admit it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:85:"It's there already," said Cael. "It's been there as long as the word has. Everything that was ever done to people like me was done inside it, with no rule to say how and no limit to say how far, and no appeal, because there's nothing to appeal under when nobody wrote the rule down." He kept his voice even. "The only thing that changes is whether anybody says so out loud. If the panel finds the schedule doesn't reach me, it isn't digging the hole. It's pointing at it, in public, on a record, where the people whose work is writing the law can see it. A hole in the law has the same remedy it's always had. Somebody writes the law. Nobody ever mended a hole by pretending to stand on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:121:"The practice isn't what I asked for." Ilsev's voice did not rise at all. "Nobody disputes the practice. The respondent gave it to you in fuller terms than you gave it yourself. I asked you for an entry: a volume, a section, a designation, the way you would cite any other in a filing. The whole schedule has just been read into this panel's own record, every line. If you give me the place, I'll know it." She waited. "Give me the place."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:143:He could feel Quenna a row behind him, where she had said he would. Once, a long way into the waiting, he heard her chair creak as she leaned forward, and he thought she was going to speak, and she did not. She only leaned forward and stayed there a moment, near enough that he could hear her breathe. Then the chair creaked again as she sat back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:9:The notices were ordinary: a change to the lamp oil allowance in the residence wing, a lecture on Current Path footwork moved from the third bell to the second, a reminder that the kitchen garden was not a shortcut. The instructor read each one in the same patient voice, and Cael listened to every word as if it mattered, because the alternative was to feel the backs of two hundred heads deciding not to turn around.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:27:"You read it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:33:"It's right," said Cael. "Nearly all of it. He's got the provision right and the record right. He's got the children right." He turned the binder over on his knees. "He's only got one thing wrong, and it's the thing in the middle that the rest stands on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:47:He did not take a single note. He sat in the back row with the binder open to a clean page and the pen in his hand while the thin woman at the front talked about the third revision of the station fees. Twice he caught himself reciting the conclusion on Edran's second page from memory. *Evidently a practitioner of some classification, which the faculty has elected not to disclose.* It was a good sentence, careful in all the right places, and he would have been proud to write it if it had been true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:67:Naveth sat behind his desk. The three pages of the petition lay squared on the blotter in front of him, and Cael saw at once that they were not the originals, which were still downstairs on the board under their brass tacks where the rule said they must stay. These were copies in a clerk's hand. Naveth had had the petition copied before he read it, so that he could read it as often as he liked without once being seen standing in front of the board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:73:"You've read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:77:"Then I won't read it to you." Naveth laid one long hand flat on the pages. "I'll tell you what I can and can't do with it, and then Quenna will tell you what she thinks I should do instead, and then Wray will tell us both whether it's possible. That's the order we agreed on the stair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:87:"I can't grant it either. Suspending a student's floor access pending review of his enrollment isn't a thing a provost can do alone; it needs a formal review of the enrollment itself." He looked at Cael over the copied pages. "And a formal review of your enrollment, a week after a sealed bundle of your assessment records went down the hill to the Compact, is the one thing in the world I least want to sign. Whatever that review wrote would be the first thing anyone read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:99:"The petition asks two things. Suspend him, or assess him in public under the same framework as students of full standing. Naveth can't do the first. So read the second one again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:101:Cael did not need to read it again; he had it by heart. *That the candidate be assessed under the same framework as students of full standing, in public, so that the academy's standings might continue to mean what every enrolled student had been told they meant.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:109:"An exhibition bout, held in the formal yard, under standings rules, with the full faculty seats present and the school free to watch. Consistent, observable practice: the provision's own three words, and the bout goes into the record under them as this month's sitting. We don't grant his petition and we don't refuse it." She closed the hand. "The petition asked a question. We let the whole school watch it answered." She opened the hand again, as if there were one more piece on it. "It isn't a standings bout. It carries no rank and no tier, and it can't move him or you a single line on the board. A demonstration assessment says one of two things, satisfied or not, and that is all it will say. But it will say it in front of the whole school."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:123:"Not partly. In front of everybody. In the record."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:125:"Yes." Quenna did not soften it, and she did not hurry past it. "If you lose, the record will say that the candidate under the demonstration provision was assessed in public against a student of full standing, an Iron Rank Two, and was beaten, and the petition will stand on that page for as long as the page exists. I would not be proposing this if I thought you would lose. But I want you to walk into it with the stakes named, not with the stakes I'd like them to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:131:Cael looked down at his hands. He thought of the board in the main hall with its three pages pinned in a row, and of Oona's face working through *Then he's asking the wrong question*. He remembered his own answer to her, that it was a fair question with one fact wrong in the middle of it. A fair question deserved to be answered where it had been asked. Edran had asked in the yard, and then in ink, and answering him now in a closed hall with three people and a slate would only be one more door shut in his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:135:Wray had not spoken. She spoke now, without moving her hands from her thighs, in the voice she used to read a measurement off a gauge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:151:"You understand what Quenna has turned this into. Eight days from now most of this school will be round that ring watching you, and whatever you show them goes into the assessment record with two hundred witnesses to it. Lose to Edran and we're in difficulty." He paused. "If you beat him by too much, I'm not sure the problem we'd have instead is any smaller."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:155:"I'm not telling you how to fight. I wouldn't know how. I'm telling you that this fight has three audiences. The students on the benches, who want to know whether the standings still mean anything. The panel, who will write down what they see. And a file, which will be read some day by people who were never in the yard and don't care how the morning felt." He folded the posting form flat on the blotter. "Only one of the three will be standing across the chalk from you. Don't fight for that one alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:163:Naveth looked at him, and something moved in the old weathered face that Cael could not read. Then he uncapped his pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:185:"The same day it was posted." She looked back at the board, at the single sheet beside the three. "That's the good kind. They didn't put it in a drawer and let it go cold. They didn't refuse it in a sentence. They gave him what he asked for, in a shape they could live with." She looked at him again. "Now it's yours to answer properly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:195:Edran was not at the board, and Cael had not expected him to be. He had already been once, early, before anybody else, to read his own petition pinned up in his own hand, and he would not go and stand in front of it now with a crowd watching him read the answer. But at the change of the sixth bell, crossing the formal yard, Cael saw him on the far side by the standings board, where his name now sat second, in the same clerk's hand at the same size, one line below Karis's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:197:He was not looking at the standings. He was looking across the yard at the main hall door, where a few students were still coming out from the board in twos and threes, talking with their hands, and then his eyes moved and found Cael. He did not come over. He lifted his chin very slightly, a single small movement that meant, as plainly as if he had said it across the chalk, *I've read it. Accepted.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:205:Lira had read the answer by the time he reached the stable. He could tell from the far end of the long room by how she sat, very upright at the scarred table under the bricked-up hay door, with her bread untouched in front of her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:241:"Not the read?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:243:"Not even the read." He turned his bowl a quarter-turn on the table. "The Iron-adjacent read is on the record twice already, so it's safe. But a fight won with one fragment is smaller on paper than a fight won with two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:323:*Exhibition. Eighth day from posting. Formal yard. Standings rules, Wray's count. Edran, Glass, Iron Rank Two, second on the standings, first for most of three years. Function: answer the petition in public, with the Wind-adjacent framework and nothing else. Benefit: if it goes well, the question gets answered where it was asked. Cost, if I lose: the petition stands, in the record, for as long as the record does. Cost, if I win too well: whatever the record says I did, somebody will read it who wasn't there.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:5:She held her left hand out over the blanket, palm up, close above her face, and her off hand, the right, went still at her side. She counted it without meaning to, the way she had counted it since she was fourteen: a quarter of a second, near enough, a hair less than at the end of yesterday. Then a point bloomed over her palm, small and dim and steady, a coin of light the size of a thumbnail, and its warmth reached her skin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:19:She wrote the match into it as she would have written any bout: the conditions, the panel, the three exchanges, her point counts in each, the time she held them through the resets, the sustain cost that had come due afterward in her fingertips and gone by morning. She wrote that the candidate had read her near points before they lit, and that she had moved her floor outward in the second exchange to answer it. She wrote that he had crossed a channel, the shortest one, and that she had not believed he would. She wrote that her fourth channel's last point had failed to take, because the place she had laid it was already burning, and that the assembly had lost its sequence. She wrote, because it was true and hers to record, that she had been holding every lit channel on that count, and that when the count broke she had lost her hold on all of them at once, and they had gone out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:31:*Supplementary. Not a session. Formal yard, fifth sitting, full intensity, under the presiding assessor's conditions. Source: K., Ember Path, Iron Rank Three. Consent: written, filed. Distance: from eight paces to one. Duration: three exchanges. Result, as reported by the subject: a fragment notice. Ember-adjacent. The field reads: acquisition, directed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:47:"I wanted you to know that I'm not. In case you'd been bracing for it." Quenna looked ahead at the main hall door. "Your consent is in the file, with my initials beside it. It says exactly what you meant it to say. Nobody is going to read it any other way while I'm the one who holds the file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:51:"You read the word *source*," she said. "In your room. I saw you stop on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:77:She had been trying. That was the thing he had read aloud from the back of his binder by a shaded lamp, and it was true, and she had known it was true before he had finished the first sentence. She had been trying with everything she had, for the first time since the paired floor. She had come out of her mark mid-argument and laid sixteen and held them through two resets and never let one go. She had spent more of herself in three exchanges than in any bout of her life, because she had wanted very badly to win, and she had wanted to win against him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:103:Her window looked down across the corner of the training yard to the low wall at the end of the residence wing. Late in the evening, when she got up to draw the curtain, there were two shapes sitting on it in the dark, close together, with their knees drawn up. One of them had a coat pulled round her in a way Karis would have known at any distance. The other sat with one arm held against his chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:121:She wrote it the first time at dawn, at the table by the window. It came out as a report, which was the only shape she knew how to make a thing come out. *Session subject reports no distress. The finding is anomalous and the source is unable to account for it.* It went on like that for half a page. When she read it back she found it true in every word and saying nothing at all, like one of Quenna's records, which was useful in a record and not what she meant. She tore the page out, carefully, along the fold, and set it aside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:123:She wrote it the second time at the third bell and it was the other way, all feeling and no shape, so that she could not bear to read it back past the second sentence, and she tore that out too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:125:The third time she tried to say only what was true and nothing more, and she went slowly. Twice she stopped and went to the window and looked at the yard and came back. Once she wrote a sentence and then scraped it off the paper with her penknife, very carefully, because she did not want a crossing-out on this page, and the ghost of it stayed in the grain of the paper anyway. She decided to let it stay, as a true record of having been wrong once on the way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:127:When she had finished she read it four times, because she did not trust herself to have read it right the first three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:133:Then she took it out, and unfolded it, and read it again, and folded it the other way, and put it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:153:"I asked to be the source. I put it in writing and I underlined it. *If it works, the documentation matters.* I meant every word. You're the biggest question in the one subject I care about, and you have been for most of a year. Going after you is the thing I'm proudest of. That hasn't changed." She was watching her hands as she spoke, not him, and that was new. Karis looked at everything in the world except her own hands. "The part I never planned for was being the result. Some of what I've drilled since I was eleven is inside you now. It stays there. It stays because I signed a sheet, and the sheet worked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:161:He had half expected two close pages of data, and what he held was a single paragraph in her small upright hand, which he could tell before he read a word had been written more than once, because the paper said so. It had been folded and opened and folded again until the creases crossed each other and the corners had worn soft as cloth. Close to one edge an earlier sentence had been scraped off with great care, and its ghost still showed faintly in the grain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:163:He read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:165:The archive was very still around them, and back in the stacks Prynn's lamp was burning low, left alight once more as if she had forgotten it, and the shelves ran away into the dark with their centuries of other people's lives written down. It seemed to him that a room like this had been built for nothing so much as this: a person setting down the one thing that mattered and passing it across a table to someone else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:185:Lira read it on the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:189:She read it, and turned it over, and there was nothing on the back. She read it again in the light from the residence wing's one lit window, slowly, the way she had read the consent across the scarred table. Then she sat with it in both hands on her knees and looked out at the black yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:197:"Somebody left her bread." Lira did not look at him. "I wouldn't know who. People are always leaving bread about in this building." She folded the page once along its softest crease. "Two days. She did it in two days. It took me two years to read four paragraphs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:203:Brom was in his room with his boots off and his feet up on the sill and Wray's book open on his chest. He read the page standing, when Cael held it out. Halfway down he nodded, and at the bottom he nodded again. He gave it back, and delivered a verdict Cael suspected he had settled on weeks ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:221:She went in. He stood in the corridor with the soft-cornered page in his hand, and then he went and put it in the back pocket of the binder, with the papers that proved things. It did not prove anything that would ever stand up in front of a panel. He put it there anyway, behind Karis's consent, because the two of them belonged together, and because the back pocket was where he kept the things he meant to have read again when it mattered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:7:She did not touch the table as she rose, or look at him, or do anything at all that a room could have read as a word passed between them. She walked to the chalk square on the open boards where Edran and Hobb had stood that morning, and stood in it with her hands folded in front of her and her slate left behind on the respondent's table, face down, beside his empty hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:33:He had known this question was coming. He had known it since the morning in Naveth's office, when she had said that the next three weeks could have whatever they wanted of her. He had known it every time he read one of her records, until he could have recited them. Knowing did not help at all. The person in the chalk square had put her name to him in front of a panel when nobody else on the hill would, and now she was being asked, in front of everybody, to choose between that and the truth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:37:"Every record is a choice," said Quenna. "No assessor ever wrote down everything she saw. She couldn't. She'd be writing still." Her voice was the voice of the record, flat and dry, and it did not tremble anywhere. "I chose narrowly. I chose inside the standards of my seat. I chose in a case where one loose word could do harm out of all proportion to the truth in it, and I knew that, and I wrote with it in mind." She looked at Coss, and only at Coss. "Nothing in those records is false."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:51:It was harder because Naveth had never stood on a floor in his life. He had sat behind a desk for longer than Cael had been alive, making careful paper for people the registry had priced wrong. He knew a great deal about how to build a sentence so that it held, and nothing at all about how one sounded when a better builder read it back to you across open boards in front of two hundred people. He walked to the chalk square slowly and stood in it like somebody asked to stand on ice, with his long hands hanging. At the academy's table Cael saw counsel shut his eyes for a moment and open them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:55:He walked the provost through the enrollment from the beginning. Who had first raised the provision, and when? Who had read the category line and the gloss? What had the academy's council been told, and by whom, at the autumn meeting that approved the observer track? He asked each question plainly and accepted each answer as it came. And every careful thing Naveth had said in that council room in the autumn, every hedge he had meant as caution, came back to him across the boards in the order it had happened. Set out in that order, it drew a man who had seen the truth on the first afternoon and had signed his name under it anyway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:57:"The council's minute of that meeting," said Coss, and read from it. "*The provost advises that the enrollment is defensible on the face of the form, and expects it to be tested.*" He looked up. "Defensible on its face. You chose that phrase."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:97:He could hear it happen. Nobody said so; nobody had to. The benches creaked as people eased their backs. Somebody in the second row said *well, that's that*, not unkindly, and somebody else asked in a whisper whether the kitchen would keep the barley hot, and was told to hush, and did not much mind being told. It was the sound of two hundred people who had decided how a thing ended and were waiting politely to be let go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:117:Across the boards, something crossed the Warden's face, small and complicated, and was gone almost before Cael could see it. He knew it anyway. For three weeks Coss had sat at a window over the draper's and watched a lamp burn late in an archive across a dark town. He had guessed, and made himself not ask, and now he had heard the thing he guessed come out onto the record in a boy's voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:121:He opened the thin book again and turned a page, as if what had just happened had a sentence of its own printed somewhere inside, and read it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:151:He ate. Brom ate beside him, steadily, and every so often looked up at the room over his spoon with the expression he wore at a rail when somebody was leaning on it who ought not to be, and the somebody always moved. Karis did not eat at all. She had her notebook open beside her bowl and was writing in it with her face very close to the page, and twice she stopped and looked at Cael across the table, and opened her mouth, and shut it again, and went back to writing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:155:"I'm not going to ask. I know what you're going to say. I sat behind the bench of books for a week." Karis put the pen down. "I was going to tell you something, and then I decided it would only be me talking because I can't bear to sit still. So I've written it down instead, and you can read it in a month." She closed the notebook. "His face. When you stood. Did you see it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:169:Lira took the card out of her coat pocket, the one she had confiscated at breakfast, and laid it on the table in front of Karis, face down, and then the pencil and the chalk in front of Cael, side by side, in a row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:179:"Procedure," she said. "Then you can go to bed. Tomorrow you are the respondent answering a challenge under the charter, and the charter has a rule about that, and I want you to have heard it from me before you hear it from the magistrate." She put one finger on the sheet. "The respondent's primary presentation is made standing, and it is made uninterrupted. Nobody may break into it. Not the officer of record, not counsel, not a member of the panel. The magistrate may stop you for order and for nothing else. You may speak until you have finished, and you will be the one who decides when you have finished." She looked up. "That's very rare. Most people who stand on a floor like that never get it, because they don't speak for themselves. You do. So it's yours, all of it, from the first word to the last. I'd like you to know it's there before you need it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:187:"Today I sat beside you," she said. "I'll sit beside you tomorrow, if you want me there. I'm your assessor of record. Nobody will think anything of it. It's where I'm supposed to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:201:"A row back," she said at last. "Behind the table. Close enough that you'll know where I am. Far enough that the record will show a respondent who answered for himself." She pulled the sheet toward her and folded it in half, and then in half again. "That's the last thing I can give you on that floor, you understand. Taking the chair away."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-56.md:215:He read it once and did not change it, and the last thing he heard before he slept was the town's lamps going out below the wall, one by one, though he could not have heard that, and knew he could not, and heard it anyway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:45:They went on until they had made twelve tries between them, and most of the twelve were failures of one kind or another. On the next two nothing caught at all; the fragment sat in him like a word on the tip of the tongue, present and unreachable, and Brom's push simply arrived and left. On the fourth he caught something and sent it the wrong way, not into the knee this time but into his own right shoulder, the one the Reydan bout had already used hard. It flared so sharply that he hissed through his teeth and Brom stopped at once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:67:It went back into Brom's wrist with a small, solid knock, like a door shutting in another room. Brom's eyebrows lifted. Under the Iron Skin his forearm had felt it, and Cael knew he had felt it, because nobody read where weight had gone better than Brom did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:107:When he had read it back, he turned to the front of the binder, where the oldest notices were copied out, and went through the four confirmed fragments as a carpenter runs a thumb along his chisels before starting work, not to learn anything, only to know they were all still there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:109:Wind-adjacent was first, and Lira's. It was an evasion framework he had once needed a full second to assemble and now did without thinking, reading a strike off an opponent's body a half-beat before it landed and moving him into the space beside it. Its range was the length of a fight. Its price was fatigue, and a choice of direction he had to make before he moved, and against faster opponents it read less. He had shown it on the circuit a hundred times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:113:Iron-adjacent was third, and Brom's: a pressure-read along the skin that told him where a person's weight was about to go. It worked at contact and out to a few paces, thinner with distance and thinner still with speed, and it was quiet enough that people who watched him fight saw only that he was rarely surprised.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:136:The road climbed all morning, through scrub at first and then low oak, and then out into open hill country where the grass had been cropped short by sheep and drystone walls stitched the slopes into uneven squares. The sky was high and pale. Early autumn had put a cool edge on the wind without yet taking the green out of anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:142:She asked Brom, not Cael, because Brom was the one who had grown up close to an academy's way of thinking; Velmere had been a family that kept tutors. He thought about it for fifty paces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:182:"I'm Copper," she said. "On paper. I'm going to walk in that gate as Copper, and people who never saw me fight are going to read the word and think they know what it means."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:208:Cael knew it too. Declarations were the language an Arbiter used, and every practitioner received them from Kindling on: a small glowing sigil only you could see, and then words in a form only you could read, telling you what your Path had just given you at your new rank. They were the private record of a person's growth, written in a hand nobody else ever saw. People did share them, brothers sometimes, or married couples, or a teacher and a student who had trusted each other for years. But you did not ask, any more than you would ask to read someone's letters, and you did not show unless showing meant something. Lira knew all of this perfectly well. She asked because she liked watching Brom refuse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:224:"You've read the log."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:226:"I've read your copies. I've never asked what it's like when one arrives."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:238:Lira laughed out loud, and a pair of sheep looked up at her in mild disapproval. Cael laughed too, more surprised by it than anything, because for as long as he had carried the notices he had thought of them as a lack, a declaration that had come out wrong. Brom had just set the two side by side as if they were simply two different kinds of record. He would think about that later. Probably in writing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:244:A hill town showed itself in pieces: slate roofs, smoke from cooking fires, and somewhere below them a mill wheel turning slowly with a deep wooden groan they heard long before they saw it. The wind carried cut grass and wet stone and, under those, bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:294:She was a woman of about forty with ink stains on two fingers of her writing hand and the patient face of someone who copied things all day. She did not get up. She ran a finger down the page in front of her, stopped, and read the names back without looking at them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-01.md:310:He walked through behind them, and the gate's shadow passed over him and was gone. On the far side the yard opened up, swept and empty, with chalk lines faint on the packed earth, and a bell rang again at a different pitch from the first. He did not know what that bell meant. He would learn, and he noticed, with something close to surprise, that he wanted to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:1:# Chapter 13 — Subject Fourteen
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:3:At the regional registry office, on the fifth day after a brown envelope left a hill town on the third-day post, a clerk with a head cold opened it, read the sheet inside, and entered one line in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:19:The folder lay shut under Coss's flat hands. He had read what was in it three times before he let himself close it: once for what it said, once for how it said it, and once more from the beginning, slowly. A monitoring assignment that turns up next to nothing, season after season, teaches a man to keep a careful distance between the words on a page and the words he would like to find there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:21:*Notice of index correspondence. Subject: Cael. Event: enrollment, chartered institution. Institution: Greyvane Academy. Category of enrollment: unclassified observer, demonstration provision. Notation entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:27:The last field was the one that always came last on this file, and he had long since stopped expecting to be able to read it. In the place where a supervisory designation belonged there was a short ruled bar, tidy as the line under a column of figures. Small type beneath it said: *Field restricted. Recipient clearance insufficient.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:41:It had cost him a long string of quarterly reports that were really one report, its three sentences dressed in fresh clothes each time. Subject remains outside any chartered institution. Subject has generated no formal complaint. Office recommends continued monitoring at the present level.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:45:He had said which, every time. Whatever else the file came to, he would stand behind that part of his record in front of anyone. He had never once swelled a report to keep the assignment breathing, and never once trimmed one to let it die. The boy had simply gone on, month after month, not crossing the threshold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:51:That evening he wrote out the request for assignment, in the flat language the form demanded. *Request for field assignment, compliance review, chartered institution. Grounds: index correspondence, enrollment of subject under monitoring.* He wrote it in one sitting, read it once, signed it and put it in the evening bag before he could begin to improve it. He had noticed over the years that the improvements he made to a request at night were seldom improvements by morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:55:He read the approval standing at the window of the district office with his coat still on, and he read it slowly, because the speed of it was information.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:59:For one minute he let himself feel about it as a private man would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:65:He thought of the ruled bar on every notice, and of his one plain question about it. Whoever had approved his request in less than a day could read what was under the bar. He still could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:67:The minute ended. He put it away in the same place as the unanswered question and sat down to the first piece of work the assignment required, which, as with every assignment, was to ask for paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:69:The records request took him two hours. It was not difficult to write; the form was a standard one, and every item on it was something a chartered academy was obliged by its charter to produce when asked. *Documentation of enrollment, complete. Citation of the provision under which the subject is enrolled. Assessment records to date, with the names of the assessors.* He wrote each item precisely.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:89:The coach was half empty and cold. He sat by the window with the file on his knees and read it again from the first page, which was his custom whenever a case was worth the travelling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:91:He did it in the hope that a file read fresh would yield some detail that a file read by habit had hidden, and it rarely did. This one yielded nothing new on the first day or the second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:99:It held the circuit summaries from Ardenmere, written by a correspondent who did not know why anyone wanted them and wrote them with the cheerful thoroughness of a man paid by the page. It held the circuit's own ledger extracts, initialled by the woman who kept the Ironyard's ledger, in an ink that changed colour twice. It held a short account, from a junior assessor, of an afternoon in a market square that Coss had read many times and never quite liked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:101:And it held, near the back, the report of a bout fought only days before the boy went up to Greyvane, in front of some six hundred people, in which the subject had beaten an Iron Rank Eight of real standing. The correspondent had described the crowd at length and the fight hardly at all. Coss had filed it under *no registered incident*, because there had been none. A fight was not an incident. A fight was only a fight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:121:He read every page. On the third night, at a waystation table with a candle that leaned and a supper he did not finish, he came to the oldest page, as he always did last, and stayed on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:123:It was not the oldest sheet in the file. The oldest sheets were Denvash's, a registration entry and a notice and a flag, as thin now as they had been on the afternoon he first read them. But it was the oldest page in his own hand: his notes from the summons in Ardenmere, the first time he had sat across a desk from the subject.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:125:Most of it was the form. Name, date of summons, the reference, the designation in brackets, the hour the subject arrived, which was early, and the hour he left. And at the bottom, in the space for the officer's remarks, a single line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:127:*Subject fourteen. Cooperative. Frightened, controlled it well.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:135:The document had been a written challenge to the summons itself, with the statute, the subsection and the appendix all cited exactly, found by a fourteen-year-old in a district archive in two days. It had been correct. Coss had read it three times and conceded it, because it was correct, and he had stamped the summons closed-pending with more force than the stamp required.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:145:He did not feel sorry for the boy. Sorrow was not his job, and he distrusted it in himself, because a sorry officer made soft reports, and a soft report did no one any good, least of all its subject.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:151:He thought, too, of the bar on the notice, and of the desk above his where somebody could read what it covered. Coss did not know who sat there. He did not know what the bar concealed. He did not, if he was honest, entirely like that he did not. He was a man who tried hard to be honest with himself at waystation tables when there was no one else awake to be honest with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:163:Then he went back to the enrollment category's gloss, which the notation copy attached to the notice carried in full, and read it again, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:165:The category was not, strictly, reserved for those who had not Kindled. The gloss said so in its second sentence, but its first sentence said something wider. It covered any candidate *without a standard classification issued by an Arbiter*. And the subject did not have a standard classification. He had a designation in brackets that sat on no schedule Coss had ever been trained on, and that designation was the whole reason the file existed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:167:So the words held. Somebody at Greyvane had read them very carefully. The words held, and only the purpose did not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:171:He found, a little to his own surprise, that he did not resent it. A case that had to be built properly was a case that would stand once it was built. And the person at Greyvane who had read those words so carefully was, whoever they were, someone he would rather argue against than someone he would rather not have to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:195:Cael read the items over the provost's knuckles. There were a good many, and the charter obliged the academy to produce every one of them on request. Not a single line was urgent. Cael could not decide whether that was a comfort. Naveth plainly thought it told him more than urgency would have.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:237:Cael went down the stair slowly, one palm on the cold wall. At the foot he stopped in the passage while the list went on reciting itself in his head, *assessment records to date, with assessors' names*, and he understood that tomorrow, for the first time, he would walk into a sitting knowing for certain that someone a long way off had already asked to read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:5:Its benches were meant for forty. By the time Cael came through the gate from the main hall, a quarter-hour before the first bell, the benches had stopped meaning anything. Students sat on them two to a space and stood on them behind the sitters. They lined the low wall along the open side three deep and sat along the top of it with their heels hanging down. A row of first-years had climbed onto the roof of the little tool shed in the corner and were being ignored by every instructor in the yard, which was a thing Cael had never seen at Greyvane before and suspected he never would again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:25:The framework, and only the framework. Wind-adjacent, the evasion he had shown at both sittings and on the circuit a hundred times, at whatever speed the fight asked of it, and nothing under it. He would not open the surface read. It was safe to show, it was on the record twice already, and he would not use it, because he did not need it, and because a fight won with one thing was a smaller fight on paper than a fight won with two. The Pressure redirect stayed banked where it had been banked for a year. The Compression fragment stayed in his chest where it belonged, and if anything Edran did woke it, he would give it no road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:29:"For the record," said Quenna.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:31:Her voice dropped into its slow, flat cadence, each word set down separately, and the yard went quiet from the front backward like a field going still in front of a wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:33:"Demonstration assessment, unclassified observer track. Third sitting, held in public, in answer to the Petition of Standing posted on this term's board. Present: the presiding assessor, the instructor's seat, the rotating faculty seat. The bout is an exhibition under standings rules, three exchanges on the instructor's count, against the petitioner." She looked up from the ledger. "The candidate takes the south mark. The petitioner takes the north."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:37:He stood on the north mark with his back to the chairs and his hands loose at his sides. Up close he looked as he did every morning in the corner by the high windows, rested and exact, with nothing in his face that Cael could use. He was not hostile. He had the look of someone about to learn the answer to a thing, who needed very badly to know which answer it would be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:59:The first strike came high, at the line of Cael's jaw. Cael read it off the shoulder before it had any speed, and the framework moved him half a pace to the left and the edge went past his ear. He heard it go. A Glass edge in motion made a small thin sound, like a wet finger round the rim of a cup, and a chill came off it as it passed, sharp as the frost on the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:115:She had been watching for that since the frost lifted, if she was honest. Not for the framework, because she had taught him the framework without knowing it and could read it in her sleep. Not for Edran, because she had watched Edran fight three times this term and knew roughly what he would do. She had been watching Cael's chest. A shell bursting against his guard was a push arriving in the bone, and the thing he had taken from Reydan on the last night in Ardenmere lived in that place, and woke for exactly that. She had seen it wake on Brom's floor, and she had read in the binder what it cost him to catch half of a slow push and seen him the morning after, holding his shoulder. If it woke in front of two hundred people and three slates and he let it go home, the whole yard would hear the knock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:121:Lira read it before Wray's hand came down, which was a thing she could do with Cael and nobody else in the world, and had been able to do since the second month in Ardenmere. She read it in how he set his feet on the south mark: a fraction narrower than in the first exchange, his weight a little forward. He was not going to give ground this time. He was going to stand closer than was sensible, inside the reach of a sequence, and stay there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:135:Not far. Half a pace, when everything about the first exchange said half a pace back. It put him inside the arc of Edran's right arm, close enough that the first strike, the left shell driven at his chest, had to come short and cramped, and the framework took him round it by a finger's width as it burst on empty air. The second came down from above, the edge on the right forearm, the other half of the ready pair. Cael slipped it too. He slipped it by less than he could have, Lira saw. He could have gone out wide, out of the sequence altogether, and the third would have cut nothing but air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:203:Today she had a different fight to read one exchange ahead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:223:The second came from above, the edge on the right, down at his collarbone. The framework read it off Edran's shoulder and moved him inside it, half a pace to the right, and the edge went down past his left shoulder close enough to feel the chill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:229:He had not decided to move when the sliver opened. He had decided six mornings ago. The framework read the shoulder that was meant to carry the third structure, read that it had nothing yet to carry, and moved him in along the line it would have taken, into the space where Edran's arm was empty. It was the same thing the framework had done with every strike since Lira taught it to him without knowing. It read where something was about to be and put him beside it. Only this time the something was not a strike. It was the absence of one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-19.md:287:The framework had already read the shoulder and moved him out of the line of the second edge, and he saw, as he went, the cost of what Edran had done. A third structure called while the second still stood was a structure called with half a mind. It was coming up on Edran's left forearm thinner than the others, paler, not quite finished. It would be there sooner. It would not be as strong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:1:# Chapter 21 — Read Back
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:31:Nobody mentioned it, not Karis, not Quenna, not him. It simply cut the section in two, his side for the work and hers for the watching, and from that afternoon on nobody crossed it, not even Quenna, who took her place at the chalk on Karis's side of it with her slate and never once stepped over. He did not ask about the line, because he thought he understood it. Clause four said he could refuse a session and clause seven said either of them could end the whole arrangement with a word, but neither clause said anything about the plain physical space of a room. The line did. It said *this much is yours*, in chalk, without anybody having to make a speech.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:33:Anyone at the rail would have expected more than there was. The work was plain. Half speed through the Wind-adjacent framework, with Karis counting the breaths between passes against a mark on her page; then working speed, and the count again. He ran Lira's circuits and the slow shoulder work for the Reydan joint and the breathing Wray had given him for the ribs, and she wrote down all of it, the ribs included, as if a boy breathing carefully were as much a capability as anything else in the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:35:The ribs showed in her numbers before they showed anywhere else. His recovery between passes at the working speed had been three counts at every session before the exhibition and was four now, and the framework's lead, the quarter of a breath she had measured in the fourth week, had not moved at all. She wrote both down side by side and read them back to him: *Recovery slower by one count. Lead unchanged. Probable cause: ribs, right side, exhibition.* "The framework doesn't seem to mind the ribs," she said. "You do." He thought that was right, and said so, and she wrote that down too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:39:Quenna had drafted it, in the end. She came in that Tuesday with a page in her hand and read it aloud in her flat sitting voice before anybody had moved: *The candidate may place his hand on the training post. The witness may lean on the post from the far side. The candidate and the witness may not touch.* Then she folded the page into her folder and nodded at Brom, who had come to the session as witness under the second clause and had not known until that moment that he was about to be useful.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:43:The Iron-adjacent read worked best at contact. It worked out to a few paces too, thinner with every pace, but at contact it was strongest, a pressure along the skin that told him where a body's weight was about to go. What none of them knew was whether it worked through something, whether the fragment needed skin on skin or only a path for the weight to travel along. So Brom leaned on the far side of the post and shifted his weight wherever and whenever he liked, and Cael stood on the near side with his palm flat on the wrapped oak and called it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:61:"I'm going to read back what I've written. Tell me if it's what you said, or if I've smoothed anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:63:She read every word he had said through each of the three cloths, in his own order, with his own phrases. *Nearly the same as skin, a voice heard through a wall, something before the shift, heavier on the left, in the cloth.* She read them exactly as he had said them, even the places where he had said them badly, in the flat careful voice she used for timings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:75:She looked at her page, drew a single stroke through *heavier* so that it could still be read, and wrote *thicker* above it, with the date in the margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:77:"Thicker. Corrected on the subject's reading."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:81:They went back to the felt for the rest of the hour. Twice more Cael caught that strange thickening before Brom's weight went, not every time and not even often, twice out of perhaps twenty, and each time Karis wrote it down and read it back. Near the end, while he was standing by the post getting his breath, she looked up from her page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:87:She stopped in the middle of the word, with her mouth still a little open, and he watched her close it and look down at her notebook, uncap her pen, write something small and quick in the margin, three or four words, and cap it again. Then she looked up at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:91:Quenna signed the strip. Cael took it and stood by the post a moment longer, looking across the chalk line at Karis, who was packing her pens, and knew what that had been. He had a page in the back of the binder that had been growing for a fortnight, a small side tally he had started without quite deciding to. In it he wrote down every time Karis's pen stopped while he was still working, and under it, on a new line, every time she began a question and did not finish it. There were six of those now, and this was the seventh. The note in the margin was the question, filed instead of asked, and the sixth clause gave him the right to read everything she wrote about him before it existed anywhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:103:*Is the lead the same for —*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:117:"You could ask me. Any of them. Clause four lets me refuse."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:119:"I know." She did not take the notebook back. "But clause four lets you refuse a session, not a question. And clause five says I don't write how. Most of those are how." She looked at the column. "If I ask, you'd have to decide whether to answer, in front of Quenna and the chalk and whoever's at the rail. If I write the half of it I've already thought and stop, nobody has to decide anything. It stays a question. Questions keep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:139:He did it in the stable after supper, at the scarred table under the bricked-up hay door, where the four of them had begun to sit without anybody arranging it. The fire was down to coals, and the kitchen had produced barley for the third night running, and Brom, who had called it honest food on the second night, was still defending it against everybody, Lira most of all. Lira had the corner of the bench, boots off and a foot folded under her, and a thick brown book of Wray's on defensive theory open on her knee. She read it the way she would have read an opponent, looking for the bill, not the lesson. Cael had the binder open in front of him and was not writing in it, and Karis sat at the end of the table with her own notebook, her pen moving in short runs and stopping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:149:She opened it, and Cael watched her turn to page forty and then fifty, and stop. In the margin beside the struck paragraph, below the younger hand's *Can't test this* and Wray's square *There is one now. Prove it. W.*, a third hand had added three lines of large square capitals, unmistakably Brom's even upside down, with a short column of numbers beneath them. Karis read them and then turned the notebook so that Cael could see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:169:Brom went back to his barley, and Cael watched Karis sit with her hands on volume six and then pick up her own notebook and close it. It was not the absent snap of someone done for the night. It was a flat, settled, deliberate closing, as if she had been carrying something, looking for the right room to set it down in, and had just found it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:185:"You promised him he'd read it first," she said slowly. "The thing your pen stopped for. At the second session. Brom told me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:193:"So the nearest you can get to *read it first* is that he hears it first." Lira considered that. "With us in the room, so he doesn't have to carry it across to us on his own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:205:"The first is the framework. I have a page on Lira's footwork, from two of her recorded bouts. I took it from the rail, for her, not for you." She did not look at Lira. "When you turn out of a strike, there's a small drop of the hip before the step. A quarter turn. Most Wind fighters don't do it. Lira does. You do it too, on the same count, to the beat. Not something like it. The same."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:209:"The second is the read. Brom told me, the week I came, that he waits on the second half of his turn longer than anybody teaches. When you read a body, through the cloth or without it, you have a wait in it of the same shape. You name one shift, and then you wait before you're sure of the next, the way he waits before he turns."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:213:"The third is the lead. I told you the framework and the read lead by the same quarter of a breath. Two different things you do, and they both come early by the same amount. And at your very first session I timed the gap between your eyes settling on where a strike would come from and your feet going. That's a quarter of a breath as well." She put one hand flat on her closed notebook. "So in both of them, the first thing that happens is that you look. Whatever comes after, the looking comes first. By the same margin, every time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:217:"So here's the hypothesis. Two capabilities, each carrying the signature of a person you've watched very closely, for a long time, at close range. Both of them started by the same act, which is watching. Whatever it is you do, the watching seems to come first. It's upstream of everything else." There was a pause. He knew what it meant: the part that mattered most was next. "And if watching is what goes in, then it's a thing you choose. You choose where to look. You choose how long. So in principle, whatever comes out of the other end ought to be directable. You ought to be able to aim it. Not only receive it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:229:Karis had never seen the binder. Cael kept coming back to that through the long quiet. She knew nothing of Feryn, or of the redirect he had kept banked and unused for a year. She knew nothing of Reydan, or of the Compression fragment that had stirred in his chest this month for a shell on his guard and been refused a road. The four names at the front of the binder, the notices copied out beneath them word for word: none of it had reached her. All she had were the marks his abilities left on the world where anyone could watch: timings and recovery counts, a read through three thicknesses of cloth, a dropped hip and a wait and a quarter of a breath. From that and nothing else, inside terms written to keep her away from every fact that would have helped, she had drawn the outline of the one question the three of them always stepped round and never said aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:231:The fear came after the relief, and it was reasonable fear. If a clever enough person could read him from outside, then one day someone he had never agreed to would read him the same way. He let it sit down beside the relief, and let them both stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-21.md:239:Lira lifted her eyes from the page at last. She looked down the bench at Karis with an expression Cael could not quite read; somewhere behind it a sum was being done. She did not announce the total. She nodded, once, and went back to the page she had not been reading. Cael had watched Lira make up her mind about people often enough that he would have bet that he had just seen it happen once more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-33.md:17:That was the first thing he wrote down, and it took him most of a page to say it properly. Ternhall did not train its students out of their tells; it put the tells *in*, on purpose, and called them correct form. The off hand went still before an ignition because a still off hand steadied the one doing the work, and Ternhall had decided it should and drilled it into a thousand students until it happened by itself. The half-breath before a sustain was there because a sustain held cleaner on a settled chest, and every commitment showed exactly as much as correct form required it to show, no more and no less. He had thought, on the first afternoon, that all this would make her easy to read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-33.md:19:It made her the hardest person he had ever read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-33.md:23:He read it back and added a line under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-33.md:63:"Five weeks. Three in four." Edran let the arm fall. "I'll take it." He looked back at the empty section. "The match won't move the board. I read the notice. It's a sitting, not a standings bout."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-33.md:91:"I've read your record. All four sittings. It's very short." He watched Karis cap her flask. "You've been at my rail every block for a week. I don't usually allow it. She asked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-33.md:143:To the untrained half of the rail it looked even, and then it looked as if Hobb was winning. He came forward and she went back, a step at a time, and twice he got close enough to put a forearm into her guard and rock her. Cael heard the tout behind him say, "Oh, now, that's interesting," in the voice of a man reconsidering his book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-33.md:199:"I'll tell you what it's like to stand near," he said. "I was on the rail at the north end, two paces from the chalk, with the read open. I couldn't help it." He frowned at the page. "It's like standing near somebody who's already made up their mind. You can feel the air's decided before she has. It leans."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-33.md:203:"I know. I've read your page." Brom tapped the channel. "So listen to me. The joins are where you'll be tempted to get clever. Don't. She's proudest of the joins, so the joins are where she's drilled the most answers. Anybody who's ever beaten a—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-33.md:253:He went over it all that evening on the wall, alone, with the binder shut on his knees because it was too dark to read and he did not need to read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-33.md:257:His framework had a seam, and he had known it since Ardenmere. When the Wind-adjacent read put a strike into his head a half-beat before it arrived, it gave him the strike and nothing else, not which way to go, so that he had to choose a direction before he could move in it. In the tiny space between choosing and moving he was nobody, neither here nor there, committed to a turn he had not finished. Against most fighters it did not matter, because most fighters gave him one strike at a time and one choice to make. Against Reydan it had mattered: Reydan had found the seam in the third exchange and leaned on it, and it had nearly been the end of everything, but the seam had been a door Reydan had to find.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:21:"Read the words for yourself before you decide anything, and notice what isn't in them. Nothing about your best. Nothing about all of it. Whoever wrote that provision knew how far a loose phrase will stretch, and left it loose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:23:"And whatever I show goes on the record."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:25:"Whatever you show goes on the record, in three hands. Think about that half harder than the first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:35:"I'd like to read the demonstration provision. The academy's copy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:39:"My assessor told me to read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:51:She went away between the shelves, and he sat and read. The provision was short; after an afternoon of arguments in a back room he had expected something longer, and it was barely half a page. It said that a candidate without a standard classification issued by an Arbiter might be enrolled for academic exposure and supervised training, that such a candidate would be assessed monthly by demonstration before a panel of three, that the candidate must demonstrate *consistent, observable practice*, and that the panel's finding should be entered in the candidate's record. That was very nearly all of it. He read it twice, slowly, paying attention to what was not there. There was no word about best, no word about all, no word about naming anything, and no word, he noticed, about how a candidate had come by whatever he practiced. The provision asked what he could do. It did not ask where it came from.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:71:That night he sat on his bed with the binder and began, as he began most decisions that mattered, by writing down everything he could show and then striking through most of it, until what was left would fit in the palm of his hand. Then he made himself do something harder, which was to imagine the record being read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:73:Not by Quenna and not by Wray. They would be in the room, and whatever they wrote would be honest. He imagined it being read a year from now, in some office he had never seen, by somebody who had never been in the room at all. He built that reader carefully, because a careless version would be useless. Not a villain; a villain would make mistakes out of spite, and spite could be planned for. He built a clerk instead, patient and thorough and correct in every procedure, with a long file of notes on him and no particular feeling about him either way, who would sit at a plain desk under a plain lamp and read the panel's entry line by line, looking not for what it said but for what it implied. How fast had the observer grown? Where was his ceiling? What could he do that a child who had not yet Kindled could not possibly do? What, in short, made it harder to call him an *unclassified observer* and keep a straight face?
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:75:He had met people like that, a man in a market square with careful questions and a careful voice, officials who had looked at him across desks and written things he never got to read. He let them blur into one patient figure under a lamp, at a desk somewhere at the end of the road the brown envelope had gone down. He sat across the desk from that figure in his imagination and went through what he could show one capability at a time, and watched what the reader did with each. He hated every minute of it. He was fairly sure it was the best use of his time all week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:83:Wind-adjacent first: Lira's framework, drilled through every bout he had fought since she had taught it to him, neither of them knowing at the time what was being taught. Its range was the length of a fight. It read a strike off an opponent's body a half-beat before the strike arrived and let him step, slip or turn into the space the strike would not occupy. Its price was fatigue over long exchanges and one structural weakness, which was that he had to choose his direction before he moved, so that a sharp opponent who saw the choice could close the door behind it. Against faster opponents it read less. He had shown it on the Ardenmere circuit a hundred times in front of crowds and judges and at least one man from the Compact, and it was not a secret anymore. It was simply skill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:87:Iron-adjacent, Brom's gift: a pressure-sense along the skin that told him where a body's weight was about to go, at contact and out to a few paces. Its range was short and speed thinned it, so that against a fast opponent at distance it gave him almost nothing. Its price was close attention, a listening with the whole body that tired the mind more than the muscles. It had been on the circuit too, more quietly, visible to anyone watching only as a boy who was very rarely surprised.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:95:Compression-adjacent: days old, a quarter of a slow push caught on a good try, paid for through the shoulder and breastbone, with a habit of draining into the nearest joint whenever he forgot to choose a road for it first. It was the youngest thing he owned and the least understood. If he showed it badly, the record would say *erratic*; if he showed it well, the record would say something far worse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:109:He had not meant to stop there. He kept doing it anyway. His line was still there in the same ink as everybody else's — *Eighth day. First bell.* — and Oona stood beside him with her slate under her arm and read it too, moving her lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:165:He read it in the corridor, and was surprised by what he felt, which was not disappointment but something much closer to satisfaction. It said *no*, and *not yet*, and *we have written down that you asked*, and he had spent most of his life in front of doors that said nothing at all; a no with a date and two sets of initials was better company than any silence. He took it up to his room and slid it into the pocket inside the binder's back cover, behind the stranger's note from Ardenmere and Vell's folded sheaf, where he kept the papers that proved things.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:171:The room was the mirror of Cael's own, narrow and whitewashed, with the one difference that Brom had already arranged everything he owned in neat rows on the shelf, as if he expected an inspection, and had set his enrollment tag in the very centre of the row, crest up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:235:He ran it, and she struck, clean and simple, from a dozen angles, and he read each strike off her body a half-beat early and stepped where it would not be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:273:"The surface read. Do the last part with your eyes closed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:277:"It's true, and it reads as restraint." Brom shrugged, carefully, minding his ribs. "You don't need your eyes for it; you never did. But they don't know that. If you shut them at the end, they'll see you aren't watching the partner's hands or his face or his feet. They'll know you're reading something they can't see you read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-06.md:287:He lay awake that night in the narrow room and ran the morning through the way he used to run fight preparation the night before a circuit bout: the framework first, four times, at panel speed, the same structure each time and only the angle varied. Then the surface read, four calls, eyes open for three and closed for the last; and nothing else, no Pressure, no Compression, nothing about where any of it had come from. Small. The same every time. Dull, with any luck. Dull would get him a second month, and a third. On the circuit he had learned to make crowds lean forward, and tomorrow the whole skill would be to make three people with slates sit back in their chairs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:13:"It was the right one to choose." She wound the cloth loosely back over it, just to keep the air off. "It was a bad idea to choose any of them. Both things." She stood. "Cold water. A great deal of it, for as long as you can bear and then longer. Then the kitchen will have something for burns; they always do. Then clean linen, and keep it loose." She looked at Lira, who had not moved from his side. "You'll see to the water."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:53:"I told Lira I was keeping it. Not you. I never told you there was anything." He opened the binder on his knee to the back pages and turned it so that only he could see it, because the first clause of the first terms still held, and she had never asked, and he was not going to make her break that now. "I'm going to read it to you. I wrote it in the tenth week. The night after you read back fourteen nulls."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:55:He read it aloud, all of it, the whole entry in the square hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:57:*Watching is necessary. It isn't enough. Every fragment came when the person I was watching was using what they had against me, for real, with something on it — my ribs, my rating, the rent, the fight. Nothing has come from watching Karis show me what she has with nothing on it, however long and however close. Whatever is in me doesn't take what it's shown. It takes what's used on me. If that's right, then the stakes aren't the weather round it. They're part of how it works.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:133:"Then have mine." She hung the coil on its nail. "Nothing at full. Not with me, not with Brom, not in front of any panel, till that thing has come clean three days in a row. Quenna may put something on the calendar. Then you go and say you need the days, or I go and say it for you." She waited until he nodded. "You found out yesterday that you can go inside yourself in the middle of a fight and take hold of something. Don't stand there this morning as if that was free. Both misses go in the binder. Dated."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:139:He wrote it at the desk by the window before the second bell, with the date and the hour and the same care he gave anything that counted. At breakfast he asked Brom for his arm, and under the table, while Brom leaned left and then right, he kept a hand on it and the read answered each time without a hitch. Afterward, in Brom's room, he tried a slow quarter push at half weight, which no reading of Lira's rule could call full. The Compression fragment caught it, he let it go, and it felt as it always had. Pressure he left alone, because he had banked it for a year and knew of no way to test it that did not need a fight, and he was not going to fight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:141:*Friday, first light. Eighteen hours or so after the match. Wind-adjacent: gone twice in the training yard, half a second each, the third sequence and the eleventh, clean for the rest of the hour. Not slow. Absent. Iron-adjacent: clean on Brom at breakfast. Compression-adjacent: slow quarter at half weight, caught and dropped, clean. Pressure-adjacent: not tested; there is no honest test without a fight. Ember-adjacent: there. Leaving it alone until the rest settle. Lira's rule: nothing at full until three clean days in a row.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:143:He read it over, and then added the line he could not stop thinking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:177:Quenna had the record waiting on her desk, a clean sheet in her own hand with the ink already dry. She said nothing when he came in. She only turned the sheet to face him and sat back, and he read it on his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:185:"It'll hold," Quenna said, watching him read. "Wray signed without a word. The rotating seat signed, and didn't put a single question to me." She let a moment pass. "Thirty years he's taught Ember. He stood up out of his chair. And not one question. That is how badly nobody in that yard wanted to be the one who asked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:203:He wanted to tell her *yes*, because yes would let her sleep, and yes would keep the record clean. But she had earned the truth, and lying to Quenna would be a worse sort of mistake than lying to nearly anyone else, so he gave her the truth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:211:That was the end of it. She turned the record back toward herself, laid it in a folder, and tied the tape. He took the sentence down the narrow stair with him, and he was still carrying it at supper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:219:It was not like the others, and he kept coming back to that without ever finding the bottom of it. The four he had carried longest lay in him like a craftsman's tools rolled in their cloth, each one separate and silent until wanted, and each so much his own by now that he could no longer feel the seam where it had been joined to him. The fifth was nothing but seam. It sat warm, not hot, doing nothing that any instrument on any panel's table would ever have registered: only a faint, even warmth, like a stone in a sunny wall still giving back the afternoon after the sun has gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:225:He had carried the oldest of the four since Ardenmere and never once noticed a person in any of them. Or he had never let himself notice. He did now. He turned toward the Wind-adjacent with great care, not reaching for it, only paying attention. It was there, and it had been there all along: a quickness that had never been his, a habit of leaving early off the back foot, a stubbornness in the turn. Lira. Lira in a freezing yard in Ardenmere, teaching a boy to stay alive because a boy who did not learn would not see the month out. Under the read lay Brom's patience. Under the redirect lay a weight he had met once across a chalk line, from a Bronze fighter who had beaten him fair. And low in his chest, under the Compression, was a ferocity that had once faced him in front of six hundred people and very nearly finished him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-37.md:241:"I watched you go through her fire with your arm down, and I counted, and you were through before I got to two and reaching for her. Then on the walk back I counted three, the way I counted six on Brom's floor, and you weren't taking any of them, and I couldn't breathe till you did." She did not look at him. "I knew what you were going to do before you did it. I saw you look at the short one. I knew you'd chosen it. I'd read it upside down off Brom's floor, the thing about choosing a burn. I stood at that rail and watched you choose it and couldn't do anything at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:15:She took the paper and unfolded it on her desk, and read it, and the records officer waited with his hands folded on his wallet and did not look at anything in particular. Cael watched her read it once fast, the way she read a slip to see what shelf it wanted, and then again slowly, with her finger moving down the margin, and on the slow reading something happened to her mouth that Cael had not seen happen before. It went thin and straight, and it stayed that way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:21:"You'll have read them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:23:"I drafted the schedule to the third. The articles are the code's, not mine." He said it mildly. "Under a challenge filed and pending, the officer of record may have inventoried the respondent institution's holdings relevant to the matter. That's the first article. The third page lists what the delegation's clerks will be taking into inventory under the general clause."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:33:"The general clause deems them relevant." He did not raise his voice, and he did not lower it either. "Two clerks come up on tomorrow's coach. They'll begin on Thursday at the front of the founding stock and catalogue as they go, and box each run as it's catalogued. Everything boxed is sealed and travels with the delegation when it goes down." He paused. "Whichever way the hearing goes, Archivist. The removal doesn't depend on the ruling. I'd sooner you heard that from me than read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:47:"You'll want to read it," she said at last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:51:"I don't want anybody to. That's not the same as not needing a second pair of eyes." She held it out at arm's length, and he came and took it, and read it standing at her desk while she looked at the shelves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:67:She did not read it out, because she knew it; she put her finger on it and said it, while the records officer followed in his own copy without a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:69:"*Upon a challenge filed and pending, the officer of record may cause to be inventoried such records of the respondent institution as are relevant to the matter challenged.*" She lifted her finger. "*Relevant to the matter challenged.* The matter challenged is the enrollment of one student under one provision of the charter. The provision was written in the lifetime of my own teacher's teacher. The waystation's traveller registers were closed and shelved before the provision existed, and before the charter existed, and before the academy existed. Nothing written in them can be relevant to a provision that was not there to be written about."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:79:Prynn did not look at the cross-index, because she knew what it said. She had shown Cael that same entry herself, in the second week of the three, as an example of a definition that had been written to close a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:81:"No," she said. "It doesn't. I'll let that one go." She moved her finger down the page to the next line. "The second sentence of the article. *And may remove under seal, for the duration of the proceedings, any record so inventoried.*" She tapped the words. "*For the duration.* The proceedings end. When they end, the seal ends. Whatever goes down that hill comes back up it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:93:She read this one aloud, slowly, because it was newer than the others and she would not trust her memory with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:109:Cael, at the far end of the table, sat quite still too. He had read that amendment himself in the second week, going through the proceedings chapter in enactment order, and had copied it into the observation notebook as one more scar and gone on, because it had nothing to do with him. He looked at it now, upside down in Prynn's volume, a short sentence, flat and clean, with nothing in it to pull on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:117:"Somebody sat where I'm sitting," she said. "Once. Some keeper somewhere, with some old shelf, and the first article open, and *relevant to the matter* under her finger. And she won. She took that word and wrung it until it gave, and the clerks went home with empty boxes. And the next year, or the year after, a man in a drafting office read the digest of what she'd done, and sat down, and wrote this." She touched the amendment, very lightly. "It's a scar. I taught a boy that at this table, a long time ago. A patch sits exactly where somebody once broke through." Her mouth moved, though nothing came of it. "I'd give a great deal to know who she was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:129:"That's the procedure as I'd read it too," he said. "I'd only not been asked." He wrote again in his margin. "It'll slow them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:133:"I'll tell them it's the article." He closed his copy and stood, and stood a moment longer with his hand flat on its cover. "I'm sorry, Archivist. I'd like that on the record, too, if there were a record of this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:145:"You read that amendment," she said, without turning round. "In the second week. I watched you copy it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:231:"The supplementary entry." She said it very evenly. "*Source: K.* On the same page as *acquisition, directed.* In my hand, in a book that isn't the binder. The second terms say nothing about how it works goes in any record anybody outside the four of us can read. That page has been a broken clause since the night I wrote it. I knew it was, and I kept it, because it was true." She looked down at the notice under her hand. "It's still true. It's also the only page anywhere outside the binder that says that word next to my name."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:243:"It's one true page." Karis's voice did not rise. "I don't burn true things. Not because somebody might read them. If I start burning what's true whenever it's inconvenient to have it lying about, I'll be a different kind of researcher by the end of the term, and I don't want to find out what kind." She put both hands flat on the table. "True things don't get burned. They get kept. By the right person, somewhere nobody else can open."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:245:She bent and unbuckled her satchel and took out the ledger, a plain brown book worn soft at the corners. She opened it at the back, past the twenty-two sessions and the ruled line and *We are missing a condition*, past the barley clause, to a page in her own small upright hand. Cael did not need to read it, because he knew it by heart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:251:Karis laid the ledger flat and ran the little blade down the page close against the binding, slowly and very straight, with the blade held almost flat, as if she had done it before and had decided then how it ought to be done. The page came away in her hand clean, and a narrow stub of paper stood where it had been, no wider than a fingernail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:253:She closed the knife and put it away, and uncapped her pen. At the top of the next page, hard against the stub, she wrote one line in her smallest hand, and then turned the ledger round so that all three of them could read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:255:*One supplementary entry removed to the subject's keeping by agreement of the four. Week twenty-one. K.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:261:"The first terms," she said. "If it ends, the notes stay with you. Every copy. And you read everything first and keep it. I'm not ending anything. I'm only using the clause the way it was built to be used." Her hand, holding it out to him, was quite steady. "The binder's the one place none of us has ever let a record reach. It can go there. Behind my consent, if you like. They'll be good company."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:265:"Clause two," said Brom slowly. He had his number sheet half out of his coat, and then he put it back. "Second terms. Nothing about how it works in any record anybody outside the four can read." He looked at the narrow stub where the page had been. "It's satisfied now. It wasn't before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:285:He put the page in the binder that night, in the back pocket, behind Karis's consent and in front of her soft-cornered paragraph. He did not read it again before he did, because he had read it on the Sunday it was written and it said what it had said then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-48.md:289:She was there, with volume five open and her pen capped beside it, and Prynn was at the high desk with her lamp, writing slowly in a book Cael had not seen her use before, a tall narrow one with a marbled cover. He sat down across from Karis and did not open anything, and she did not look up. Once she went back a page, read it again, and went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:23:"Don't tell me," said Coss. "You'd be a fool to, and you've never been one in front of me yet." He was quiet for a few paces. "But I'll tell you how this side of the table works, since you've never had anybody standing on it who'd say. The archivist keeps a lending book. Every volume that leaves a shelf, a slip, and every slip, a line in the book, with the date. I've seen it on her desk. Under the charter's procedure for a challenge filed and pending, I could have that book brought down to my room over the draper's tomorrow morning, and read your three weeks off it shelf by shelf, in the order you pulled them. I'd know by supper what you were going to say on Monday."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:39:"I've written about you every quarter for two years," he said at last, "and you've read me better than most of the people I write for." He shook his head once, slowly, the way a man shakes his head who has measured something twice and got the same figure. "But no. That's not the reason. The reason is that it would be a cheat. A legal one, and I'd be within my rights, and it would still be a cheat. The challenge is good. I built it to be good. A good case ought to be met by whatever the other side has actually got, in the room, on the day. Not by something I took off a shelf when the other side wasn't looking." He was quiet for a few paces after that. "If you'd stood behind my door with a glass to the wood for three weeks, I'd have thought the worse of you. I'd like not to give you the same reason."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:91:"Tell me how," he said. "The whole of it. As if I hadn't read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:93:"You haven't. Not the way I did." She took her hands off the pile. "I read it from the first page as if I'd never heard of you, the way I said I would. I didn't look for your word. If I'd looked for it, I'd have read with it in my head, and seen it in places it wasn't. I read every name the schedule gives and asked of each one, *what is this, and where does it live*. I wrote nothing down while I read, so I wouldn't start a list I could lean on. Then, when I'd finished every volume and every fascicle, I sat down and made my list from memory, and checked the list against the shelves afterward."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:97:"The enumeration. The provisional annex. The appendix of disputed standings. The amendment schedules, every one, with the struck categories kept in them. The record of the categories proposed and refused, which the schedule keeps as carefully as the rest." She paused with five fingers up. "And the definitions cross-index at the back of the charter, which isn't in the schedule, but which the schedule points to, so I went there too." She folded the hand. "Everything the Compact has ever put a formal name to lives in one of those six. Some in two. Nothing lives anywhere else. Your word isn't in any of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:101:"They're bound separately. In the seventh volume, at the back, after the index." She looked across the lamp at him. "It doesn't matter. It's not in the sixth either. But it means we didn't read the same way, which is the only thing that makes it worth anything that we both read it. If we'd read the same way, I'd only be you again, a week later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:117:"Thursday," said Karis, more quietly. "Tomorrow the clerks start at the front of the founding stock. I've three cases of the traveller registers left, at the back, against the wall. I'd like to read as many of them as I can before they're string and wax." She began to gather her things, the flat box first. "That isn't your search, I know. None of it's for Monday. You needn't say it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:133:The cards were pasteboard, bought from the draper in bundles of a hundred and cut to the size of her palm with the penknife. She was on her fourth bundle. There was one card for every volume she had opened, with the same things in the same places on every card, because a system that changed its places was not a system. Prynn's shelf mark went in the top left; the run in the top right, yearbook or toll book or provisioning account, weather log or traveller register; the clerk across the middle; the year at the foot, in the house's own reckoning, with her conversion under it in pencil. In the bottom right corner was a single mark: a dot if she had read the volume through, a ring if she had only carded it, and a stroke through the ring if she meant to come back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:173:She had read it on her first day on the shelves, and it had told her nothing she could use, because the first year of the house was a date in a reckoning nobody had used since the house stopped keeping books. It might have been a hundred years ago or a thousand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-50.md:209:By the seventh bell she had read four volumes of the crosshatched sevens and carded them and put a dot in each corner. The clerks had moved down a case. They were on the toll books' first shelf now, a day early, and she could hear the Blotter's generation of honest pools going into a box, and Prynn at the door saying *count*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-11.md:53:"I know why, and I'd guess the reasons behind it are good. But Cael's had a lot of close looking in his life, and every bit of it was somebody collecting. The certification office in Denvash. The men who trailed after him in Ardenmere writing reports he never saw. The registry, which had his name down before he'd finished being fourteen. Observation, they all called it. What they were doing was building a case." She held Karis's eyes. "And data is still something you'd be getting *from* him. And for a long time now I've been one of the people who stand between him and the ones who want things. So you'll forgive me for being slow to pull out a chair for somebody new."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-11.md:85:"For the record," Cael said, and all three heads turned, because it was the first thing he had said, "I'm not just allowing this. I want it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-11.md:121:"Because it isn't research," said Lira. "Not to us. Back when nobody else would tell us the truth, the three of us told it to each other, and that's what's in there. You can study what he does, if he lets you. How we got here isn't yours to read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-11.md:151:He wrote it in square capitals so large that it filled three lines where her hand would have used one: *4. He can say no to any one session without saying no to all of them. No reasons.* Then he pushed the paper back across. Nobody argued, Karis least of all. She read it and said, "Yours is better than mine would have been."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-11.md:163:Then she read the whole page out, start to finish, and then again more slowly. She signed at the foot in a firm, level hand that looked older than sixteen. Cael signed under her. Lira and Brom signed under him as witnesses, Lira in a quick leaning scrawl and Brom in his square capitals, and the four names lay together at the bottom of the sheet in four different hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-11.md:173:For a moment nobody moved. Lira was the first to reach. She passed over the top volume and drew one out of the middle of the pile, which Cael guessed was no accident, and let it fall open where it liked. She read a page in silence and turned it, then read another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-11.md:196:"It's the one I received at Iron Three," Karis said. Her voice was quite level, but she had put both hands flat on the table, and Cael saw that she was pressing them there. "It's the newest. You can't see what he has, and I won't see it. That's agreed and it's right. But it seemed wrong for only one side of this table to be read." She paused. "I've shown it to one other person before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-11.md:230:He read it in the stable by the dying fire until the eighth bell, while the tile players packed up and went and the two of them sat on at the scarred table, and when the bell rang he closed it and sat looking at the cover.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:5:She was on it already, a darker shape against the dark, heels hooked against the stone and her coat wrapped close, chin resting on her knees. She had no book and was doing nothing at all except looking out over the training yard, and she did not turn when the residence wing door banged shut behind him, though she could not have missed the sound. Either Brom had said something, or she had counted the days since Wednesday on her own and decided that this was the night. Nobody alive could read him better, and for two days she had watched him carry it without once asking him to put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:15:"Karis thinks it can be pointed. Aimed. Those mornings in Ardenmere, then. Your framework. If she's right, I didn't just happen to be standing close by when it came. I did it." He made himself go on to the end. "To you. Nobody asked you first, least of all me. I didn't know there was any asking to do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:45:"Most of what's on its way to you frightens me, if you want me to go down the whole list. The Compact, the morning it runs out of patience. That request, and whoever wrote it, and whatever they make of three people's handwriting calling you satisfactory." She pulled her knees in tighter. "It keeps coming out bigger than anyone last measured it, the thing in you, and every time there's a bill. That frightens me. I read what the half push cost you. I saw you the next morning. I can add."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:73:Then she went in, and he heard the door open and close behind her and did not turn round. He sat on in the cold and understood what she had just done, which she had done once before, long ago, under far less weight. It was the promise they had made in Ardenmere, made again now to bear a load it had never been built for, and it held, because a thing put together right the first time generally does hold when more is piled on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:75:He stayed out there long after the door had shut. The cold settled into his coat, and below him the lamps of the town winked out one by one, each of them a house totting up its day and closing the book. In the residence wing behind him Karis was very likely still awake, adding a half-question to the page she called *Not asked*. Three days west, a woman kept a ledger of every bout her floor had ever seen, so that the truth about people would be stored somewhere against hard winters. Further still, in Denvash, a man kept a workshop, and had once stood beside a boy with a hand on his shoulder on the worst morning of the boy's life. Everywhere he looked there was somebody keeping a record, and he had kept one himself for as long as he could remember. And in all that time he had never once gone over the one record that mattered most, the one written into the build of him, in a hand he was only now starting to suspect was his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:81:He read it over once by the light of the single lit window in the residence wing. Shutting the binder, he understood that what lay under that sentence had never really been fear of power. It was fear for the people around him, anyone in reach of the cost, whether he meant them to pay it or not. He thought of Lira on the frozen wall, telling him the sums came out clean. He thought of Brom with his column of numbers in his coat, still standing up for barley, and of Karis with her chalk line and her page of half-questions. He thought even of Edran, with his thin third structure, and of Hobb, who had stood in front of him twice and hit exactly as hard as he had been told to. They were all close, and he had been watching every one of them. It was their account too, every bit as much, and always had been, and the only honest payment he could make on it was to learn to read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:119:"The third sitting. I copied it into the ledger the night of the bout; the fair copy took me longer than it should have. Yours. You've read every other page of your own record before anybody else has, since Karis wrote that clause. I didn't see why the academy's should be the exception." She was already moving on up the stair. "Keep it with the others. The day somebody reads the file who was never in the yard, I'd like you to have already read every line they'll read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:121:He read it standing on the stair while students went past him on either side, Wray's twelve words and the rotating seat's long paragraph and the withdrawal entered with the hour, and found nothing in it about a fragment, nothing but a framework and a gap and a hand on a breastbone. Then he folded it into the back pocket of the binder, behind the refusal and the stranger's note and Vell's sheaf and Karis's first four pages, among the papers that proved things.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:137:*The shop is the same. The stove still smokes when the wind is east. I mended the bench you used to sit on, so it is ready for whenever.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:143:*Write when something happens. Write when nothing does. I read both kinds the same.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:147:He read it standing on the corner in the cold with the valley spread out below. It was not long; Hesk's letters were never long. But he could hear every line of it in Hesk's voice, slow and dry, said across a workbench with his hands still busy. When he came to *I mended the bench you used to sit on* he read that line again and had to look at the far hills.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:171:Lira did not say anything. She did not touch the line or point at it, or make a joke about it, or tell him what she thought the panel would make of her. She looked at it for longer than one line takes to read. Then she shifted the loaf under her arm and went on through the hall toward the residence wing with her chin level and her stride unbroken, as she had walked past the marker at the floor desk every morning since the second week. He watched her shoulders as she went. A half-second after she passed the board, they came down by about an inch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:175:Gerda came in from the yard a minute later, with her patched coat buttoned to the chin and her slip for the floor in her hand, and stopped at the calendar where Lira had stopped. She did not look at Lira's line. She looked at her own, two below it, and read it once with her lips pressed together, and then looked up at the clock above the door, as she always did, and checked it against something in her head. She saw him by the archive passage and did not come over, and then, after a moment, did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:181:Gerda considered the question as if it were a clause she had been handed to read aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:183:"I've read the procedure. All of it. Twice this week. Two bouts, a panel of three, the results entered with the assessors' initials, and a recommendation in the file." She folded her slip once along a crease that was already there. "The procedure is very clear. It doesn't care whether I'm ready. That's what's good about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:207:She was very quiet, working. She did not talk to herself as she sometimes did, or mutter at a slip that came late, or laugh when one came right. She moved and turned and moved again in the grey light. There was something in it he had seen on her only a few times in all the time he had known her, the stillness of a drawn bow, the quiet of a thing pulled back as far as it would go and held, waiting to see where it would be sent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-23.md:229:It was not long, and he had left out more than he had put in: the fragment that woke for a shell and was given no road, the binder read backward, appetite, a girl he could not look at across a stable table. Some things were for the binder. But he had written nothing in it that was untrue, and he had told Hesk about the asking, and that was nearer to the whole of it than any letter he had ever sent down a hill. He folded it, wrote the Denvash direction on the outside, and set it on the desk for the next third-day post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:9:Nobody said anything, and Karis had already opened her notebook. She turned it sideways on the desk, so that it lay between all of them, and ruled a line down a clean page, and another across the top. On the left of the first row, in her small upright hand, she wrote *L.* On the right she wrote *keeps him a fighter*, and then she looked up at Lira and waited, pen lifted, to see whether she had got it right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:17:"She'll have you," said Quenna from the window, before Karis's pen had reached the page. "Most of her life she's been waiting for someone who would read the shelves for himself, instead of standing at her desk asking what's on them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:21:Karis wrote *C. and P. — the code* on the second row, and under it, smaller, *one thread*. Then, without lifting the pen, she wrote her own initial on the third row, and only after it was written did she say what it was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:35:That left one row ruled and empty, and everybody looked at Brom, who was still leaning against the alumni books with his hands behind his back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:49:"Witnesses," he said. "That's the row. Either side can enter witnesses of fact, you said. Edran withdrew his petition on the record in his own hand; nobody will call him a friend. There'll be others who watched the sittings. I'll find out which of them will stand up and say what they saw, and not one word more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:55:Above them on the stair, Karis had stopped to write *B. — witnesses, uncoached* on the last row, and closed the notebook on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:81:"Enactment order," she said. "That's how you'll read it." She turned the first volume round so that it faced him. "Not the order it sits on the shelf in. The shelf is only where it was put away. The order it was enacted in is the order it was made, piece by piece, and whatever is weak in a thing is weak because of how it was made, not because of how somebody shelved it later." She laid her finger on the folded card. It was a concordance of the amendments, ruled by hand in faded ink, the columns narrow and dense and perfectly straight, and it was plainly older than his parents would have been. "The first amendment, then the second, then the third, all the way up. Every patch tells you somebody got frightened. Read them in order and you'll see what frightened them, and when."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:85:"Every amendment is a confession," said Prynn. "Each patch went on because somebody had found a hole, and it sits exactly where the hole was. Read them in the order they went on, enough of them, and you'll have drawn yourself every weak place that wall has ever had."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:89:"The taxonomy master said that," he said. "In the first week. He said when I was older I should read the amendments. He said every one of them was a confession."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:97:He was quiet a moment. "I read fighters like that," he said. "By what they've had to fix. Nobody practises a guard against a blow that's never landed on them. If somebody's drilled their left side until it shines, somebody once hurt them on the left."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:149:*Three weeks. Study it as I'd study a fighter: no single question till I've seen the whole of how it carries itself. Prynn says read it in the order it was frightened. L. says come out at dusk and stand on a floor. Both of those are the same rule.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:173:He read it back. There was nothing in it that a stranger could have used, and nothing in it that was not so. He wrote the Denvash direction on the outside and set it on the corner of the desk for the third-day post, and sat a while looking at it, thinking of a stove that smoked when the wind was east and a bench somebody had mended so that it would be ready whenever.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:185:He read it and moved his inkwell to the far end of the table, which he had not done, and laid his pen on its side, which he had not done either. The roof over the corner of the reading room had a brown stain spreading across the plaster, and Prynn, who had watched him read the card, looked up at the stain and then at him, and did not need to say anything about what the rules were protecting against.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:193:He read without chasing anything for the first two days, taking in how the whole thing carried itself before he looked hard at any part of it. Halfway through the first morning, with his finger on a clause and the pile tilting at his elbow, Prynn spoke from the high desk without looking up from her ledger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-44.md:221:He did not stop the other work while he read. Lira would not have let him, and he would not have let himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:5:Cael had chosen it off the schedule on purpose. It was listed as an introductory block, open to observers and first-years. He wanted to hear how Greyvane explained the whole system to someone meeting it fresh, because he had learned it as most children in the Outer District did, in scraps from adults who were busy and later from books he read standing up in other people's shops. He had never once heard it taught from the beginning by someone whose job was to get it right. He had not quite pictured what that would mean. Twenty-two children turned on their benches when he came in, and twenty-two pairs of eyes went to his height and his face and the tag on his coat and back to his face with a new question in them. He was two years older than the oldest of them and a head taller than most. He took the bench at the very back, set the binder on his knees, and tried to look like furniture.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:9:The girl from the assessment board sat in the second row with her slate. She turned around twice to look at him, the second time with open interest, and when he caught her eye she gave him a small, serious nod. He nodded back, and she seemed satisfied and faced the front.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:21:A chart hung on the wall behind the lectern, large and painted on stiff canvas, old enough that its corners had been mended twice. Across the top ran the seven tiers in their colours: Copper in dim orange, Iron in silver-grey, Bronze in warm gold, Silver bright as a coin, Gold a deep amber, Platinum white touched with blue with a small note beside it that said *historical*, and at the far end a seventh box with no colour in it at all, only the word *Void* and a question mark. Down the left side ran the Paths themselves, grouped into families and painted in neat capitals — Movement: Wind, Current, Storm; Hardening: Stone, Iron Skin, Shield, Anchor, Wall; Edge: Blade, Glass — and then Ember and Ash, Force and Pressure, Mire and Tide, and more below those, a long column of names, some of which Cael had only ever seen written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:23:"This chart is copied from the Compact's own schedule, the list the registry uses," the instructor said. "Every Path an Arbiter can assign is on it. The families are ours, for teaching; the list is theirs. If your Arbiter tells you a Path, it will be one of these. If it isn't on this chart, it isn't a Path." He tapped the top row with a pointer. "Tiers first. You all know the colours. Who can tell me how many ranks are in a tier?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:43:"And who keeps the list?" the instructor asked the room, and answered himself before anyone could guess. "The registry. Every Kindling in every district in Valdris is entered within the week, and in all its history the registry has never once taken an entry out. It is a ledger that has never closed an account." He let that sit. "The list on this canvas has been changed many times, too, by amendment. When you're older, read the amendments. Every one of them is a confession. It tells you what somebody was afraid of, the year they wrote it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:45:Cael wrote that on the left, and then on the right, after a while: *Assumes the registry is the record. Assumes that what it doesn't write down didn't happen.* He thought of Vell's ledger folded into his pack, every bout he had fought at the Ironyard and every rating change in her own hand, most of which the Compact's registry had never heard of, all of which had happened anyway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:61:"Sometimes, between people who trust each other a great deal." He set the chalk down. "Showing someone your declaration is a little like letting them read a letter you wrote to yourself. It's intimate. You'll know, when the time comes, whether a person is someone you'd do that for. Most people do it once or twice in their whole lives, if they do it at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:67:Near the end of the block the instructor invited questions about the chart, and there were a great many. Could a Path change families? (No.) Could a person have two Paths? (No.) Was it true that Storm and Wind were really the same Path with different tempers? (A long, patient no, with a diagram.) Why was Glass in the same family as Blade, when glass broke? (Because a thing that breaks well still cuts, and the instructor had a story about a broken bottle that he declined to finish.)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:73:The room did not go quiet at once. Most of the children had no idea what she meant, and they kept rustling and whispering, but the instructor's face went very still for half a moment, and in the back row Cael stopped breathing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:105:The instructor looked at him a moment longer with an expression Cael could not quite read. "Keep it," he said, and went out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:157:He set his bag against the wall, and it turned out to be a strange hour. He ran the Wind framework against an imagined count, moving through the evasion patterns at half pace and then full pace, reading strikes off a body that was not there. It was harder than it should have been, because the framework did not like empty air: it was built to read an opponent, and without one he had to supply each strike himself out of his own expectations, so that none of them ever surprised him. He did the conditioning circuits Lira had built him in Ardenmere, legs and core and the slow shoulder work that kept the Reydan joint from stiffening. He tried the surface read, and as he had known it would, it had nothing to do. Iron-adjacent needed a body within a few paces, someone whose weight was about to move, and alone it was a sense with nothing to sense.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:161:At the end of the hour she signed the record of the session, tore off the bottom strip, and gave it to him without looking up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:171:"Asking costs you nothing. Being seen to have asked, early and often, may be worth a great deal one day." She finally looked at him. "Files get read from the front page. I'd like yours to show you knocked from the start."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:173:He wrote the request that evening at the desk in his narrow room, in his most careful hand — *The undersigned, enrolled under the demonstration provision, requests that a training partner be assigned for supervised sessions, in order that practice may be conducted against a live opponent* — read it over, signed it, and left it at the assessors' desk before the eighth bell. He did not expect it to be granted, and he did not mind. A refusal on paper would at least carry a date and a signature, and most of the doors he had ever stood in front of had offered neither.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:179:He had come into the training hall early to look at the open-floor roster, and she came in a minute behind him, and he saw her before she saw him. There were two lines at the floor desk. The standard line ran from the near end, where a clerk sat with the big roster and a stamp. The other began at the far end of the same desk, past a small wooden marker, with its own sheet and its own clerk, who did nothing but wait for the few names that belonged there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:183:There was one other person at the far end of the desk, a woman of perhaps twenty, tall and narrow-shouldered, with ash-pale hair cut short and a training coat patched at both elbows. She signed in after Lira and said something as Lira passed her, and Lira stopped. Cael could not hear them from the roster, but he could read the shape of the minute that followed: the older woman pointing at the slip in Lira's hand, Lira looking down at it and back up, a short dry exchange, Lira almost laughing. When she came over to him she was still half smiling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:199:At the second bell he went to hear Wray teach the ceiling. He had found the block on the lecture schedule the night before, after Brom's argument about ceilings over the barley — *Declaration Efficiency, Hardening Family, Second Years. Instructor Wray.* — and since an observer could attend any lecture, he went, though he had not quite believed until he walked into the room that Brom would be there too. Brom was in the back row, sitting very straight with his notebook open and his ribs held carefully to one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:245:"Before you say anything," she said without turning, "I know it's a clerk's mark. I know it only means somebody checks my results before they count. I've read the rule. Twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:249:"You were going to say something kind and sensible, and I'd have had to agree with it." She kept her eyes on the dot. "Here's the trouble. For two years I've told anyone who'd stand still that Fenmark got me wrong. Then I walked in here and put my name to a page that says Fenmark's record stands, and I did it so I could train, and now there's a mark by my name every morning to remind everyone that my wins need a second signature." She breathed out through her nose. "I was right about Fenmark. I also signed. Both of those are true, and I can't make one of them sit down and let the other have the chair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:3:Quenna read the terms standing up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:5:She read them in the small room off the main hall that the assessors used between sittings, with its one window, its two hard chairs and its long table stacked with slates, and she did not sit down to do it, though both chairs were empty. Karis stood on one side of the table and Cael on the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:7:Quenna held the page at reading distance and went through it from the first clause to the four signatures at the bottom, and then turned back to the top and went through it again, and the second reading took longer than the first. Cael began, somewhere around the fourth clause, to wonder whether she was looking for a reason to refuse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:11:"Where do the notes live?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:13:"In my room, in a box with a lock on it," said Karis. "His copies live in his."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:15:"Not in the archive. Not in any academy record. Not attached to anything with a seal on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:19:"Good." Quenna turned the page a quarter-turn on the table, so that it faced Cael. "And you've read the sixth clause properly. You understand that it gives you every word before anyone else sees one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:23:"Then I have one condition, and it's mine, not theirs." She laid a finger on the margin. "The sessions happen inside your supervised hours, in section four, with me at the chalk. Not on the open floor, and not in somebody's room after the eighth bell. Every hour she watches you is an hour the record already shows you were supervised. If anyone ever asks what an observer was doing with a Ternhall transfer and a notebook, the answer is already written down, in my hand, with a date on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:27:"There's one more thing," Quenna said. "I won't read her notes, and I don't want to be offered them. If I've read them, I can be asked what they say." She said it without any weight at all, and then she picked the page up once more and looked at the bottom of it, at the seventh clause in Karis's small upright hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:49:It was the evasion framework, the Wind-adjacent pattern Lira had put into him in Ardenmere when neither of them knew what she was teaching or what he was learning. By now it lived somewhere underneath thought. He ran it three times through.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:59:There was something close to rest in being watched like that. No one was marking him. Whether his feet were pretty, whether the pattern belonged on any Path's chart, whether he ought to be allowed to stay, what word went in which box: none of it seemed to concern her at all. She was taking all of him down, every piece of it worth her trouble.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:63:He had come out of a turn a shade squarer than the turn needed, and kept his shoulders set on their line a beat too long, so that the shape would read cleanly from where she sat. It was not the circuit itch to make a crowd lean in. It took him the rest of the pass to find what it was instead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:79:In all the hundreds of times Lira had watched the framework, she had never wondered aloud what it was like to be inside it. Brom read him through the skin better than anyone alive, and even Brom had never come at it from that side, never suggested that a movement might carry a memory of its owner, or that doing it might feel like wearing someone else's coat. Quenna's questions, for all her years of studying what he was, always had answers a person could watch. This one had no outside at all. Only one person could ever stand where its answer was. Karis had walked straight up to that door on her very first afternoon and tapped on it, politely.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:89:Karis nodded. If she was disappointed, it did not show; she looked like a person who had predicted this result and was logging it. She wrote nothing. There was nothing to write. She got up, folded the borrowed stool under her arm, thanked Quenna, gave Lira a nod at the rail on her way past, and went out, and the question stayed behind in section four like a seed she was content to leave in the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:91:Quenna made a single mark on her slate, signed the record, tore the strip off and held it out to him without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:119:"The session," she said. "Everything I wrote, put in order. The copy is yours to keep. I'll wait, if you want to ask anything, or go, if you'd rather read it alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:123:She sat across the table and opened her own notebook and did not watch him read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:125:He had never in his life read a page about himself before anyone else had read it. He understood that only as he unfolded the first sheet, and it made his hands slow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:127:Every page anyone had ever written about him had been written somewhere else and read somewhere else, and had reached him, when it reached him at all, long afterward, in pieces, through someone who had seen it first. The registry's word. The certification clerk's form. Whatever the patient official had been writing all this time. He had always been the last to know what he was on paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:137:He read all four sheets twice. On the second reading he found it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:145:Karis studied the line. Then she opened her own notebook, found the page from the session, and ran her finger down a column of timings he could not read upside down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:149:She uncapped her pen. She did not rub out the line or scrape it away. She drew a single straight stroke through it, so that it could still be read, and wrote beside it in the margin, small and exact: *Error, observer's. Pass 3 differed from pass 1 at the second turn; subject reports deliberate change. Corrected on the subject's reading.* Then she wrote the date, and did the same on his copy, and pushed it back across the table to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:163:He folded his copy and put it in the back of the binder, with the refusal and the stranger's note and Vell's sheaf, among the papers that proved things. It was the first page in that pocket written about him that he had read before anyone else in the world.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:223:Later, alone in the narrow room with the lamp turned low, he did something he had not done since the road. He opened the binder at the front, where the four sections began, and read only their first lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:225:*Wind-adjacent. Lira.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:233:Four fragments, four notices copied out word for word as they had arrived, and beside each one a name. He had written the names on the day each notice came, because it had seemed only honest to record where a thing had been when it arrived, as a man records the field where he found a stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-12.md:241:He thought about the read, the pressure along the skin that told him where a body's weight was going, and it had Brom's patience in it, the long listening wait before anything happened. He thought about the Compression, the hot bar across his collarbones, and it had nothing of anyone in it at all that he could find, only the cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:9:The clerk had it in her inky fingers and was turning it over. She looked at it much longer than she looked at anything that came through her booth, and Cael watched her look and knew what the look was. It was the look of somebody checking a thing against her memory and finding nowhere in her memory to put it. She said something. The older man answered, patiently, and took a second paper from a document case under his arm, folded once: a careful traveller's spare proof. The clerk read it, and put her finger on her list, and found something on the list, and the gate opened as it opened for anybody the list expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:27:He had gone there because he could not think of anywhere else to go, and because Prynn had been in the middle of explaining to him, the evening before, the way she numbered the slips she left in place of a pulled volume, and he had asked her to finish. He had asked only so that his hands would have something to do. She had seen that at once, and finished anyway, in more detail than the subject strictly needed, and he had been grateful.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:37:The walk took four minutes, and he spent every one of them counting, because counting was easier than looking at the one thing he was not ready to look at. Fourteen students crossing the yard. Three crows along the kitchen ridge. His boots loud on the flags and then quiet on the trodden earth, and the wind cold on one cheek. He had rehearsed this day since Denvash in more versions than he could number, and in every one of them it had come as a knock: a courier on Hesk's front step on the second morning, one knock, a polite wait, a satchel. In none of them had it come as a patient man in road-coloured clothes holding up a seal at a gate, and the gate swinging open for him because nothing about him was wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:59:So it was that kind of meeting, small and on the record.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:69:"Readily," said Coss. "When a subject hears a thing at second hand, the second hand gets it wrong, and then somebody appeals, and we all do it twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:99:"In this room, now, on the record." A third paper came out of the case, already signed. "It compels a public adjudication, with a magistrate of the Compact presiding. It sits on the academy's own ground, and the record goes into the permanent registry. None of this pleases me. I'd ask you to believe that. And I'd ask you to believe as well that my being pleased or not was never part of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:107:"I've read the alumni ledger," said Coss. "I mean this: it does the academy credit." There was no heat in his voice at all, and somehow that was worse. "But it doesn't answer me. Every one of those students was what the provision was written for while it held them. None of them was a registered classification. Nobody had ever thought to ask it to cover one." He paused, and chose the next word. "Nobody had been *inventive* enough. This won't be a hearing about forty years. It'll be a hearing about a single student."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:125:"Here's how it will go," she said, "so we don't waste a day of it pretending. It'll be the assembly room, on the ground floor of the lecture wing. The record's public, every word. Our counsel gets to see their documents on a timetable, and they get to see ours. Either side can enter witnesses of fact, by name." She counted the points off on her fingers, as Lira counted rumors. "Counsel will fight the timetable and the scope and the order of documents, and every one of those fights buys us days. None of them buys us the result. He went straight for the wording that sheltered you. That means he read our file as carefully as I wrote it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:129:"Five sittings," she said. "An exhibition. A match. Three signatures on every page." She said it like a sum she had checked twice and still did not like the answer to. "That was the whole plan, you know: be on the record so thickly that nobody would bother looking. He bothered. He's read every page I wrote, and he's decided the category weighs more than all of them." Her voice did not change, but something in her face did; it was not fear, it was nearer a debt being owned aloud. "It was my name that put you in that chair across from him. So the next three weeks can have whatever they want of me, and I mean that as a promise and not a comfort."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:133:"After the first sitting you said our job was to get on the record before somebody hostile read it," he said. "Somebody has. What's the job now?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:145:He was perhaps nineteen, with a new coat and ink on his cuff, and he plainly had not been told whether he was allowed to speak to the subject. He decided, visibly, that nobody had told him he wasn't.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:147:"He read your file on the coach," he said. "Both days. Every page, and then from the front again. He does that with all of them, they told me at the office, but I thought you'd —" He stopped, and went red to the ears. "I don't know what I thought. He's fair. That's all. Everybody at the office says he's the fairest man on the floor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:163:She said nothing, but he watched her face go back two years, to a girl being handed nineteen words from Fenmark that had already been used against her before anyone let her read them. It was the face of someone who had learned early how far the machine reached above any office with a door you could knock on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:191:"I've done some counting as well," he said. "Three hundred people in the formal yard, and a panel of three, and not a single one of them could name what you did, and the record held regardless. And today one man with one card climbs the hill to tell us the paperwork can't hold you." He lifted the whole stack in both hands, as though weighing the argument itself. "If you ask me, it's the paperwork that should be worried."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:213:"I've sat across a desk from it." He watched the small yellow square. "He read my paper three times, in Ardenmere, before he conceded it. I watched him do it. He didn't skip a line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:217:"Then you'd better read his," she said. "Three times. Without skipping a line." She stood up off the wall and shook out her coat. "And then you'd better read the thing he never read at all, because if there's anything in that whole building he hasn't read from the first page, that's where you go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:9:"Mine," she said. "Not theirs. If you're going to read the code to a magistrate you'll read it off a copy that's been kept properly." She looked at him over her spectacles. "I've counted them. Every fascicle. Don't drop anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:23:Quenna came in by the side door and did not come to the table. She went to the row of chairs set a little back from it, where the academy's counsel and Naveth already sat. She took the chair at the end nearest the table and put her slate on her knee. She did not look at him. Cael felt her there, as she had said he would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:31:Yorlan had the thin book under his arm. The grey-bearded assessor had his pad. And Ilsev, coming last, carried in her two arms a set of dark-spined volumes of her own, tied with a strap, which she had not brought the day before. She set them down on the bench in front of her chair and unbuckled the strap and laid them out in a row, and opened the first of them at a place near its beginning, and put one finger on the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:33:Cael saw Coss see it. He saw the Warden's eyes go to the row of volumes in front of the senior evaluation seat, and stay there for a breath, and come away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:37:"This tribunal sits under the charter, in public, and on the record. The matter is named in the notice. The parties are named in the notice. The panel will hear the challenge first, and the answer after. The panel will hear what is put to it and no more. The panel will not hear argument for which it has not been shown the ground. The respondent will state his name."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:43:"The panel notes it." Yorlan did not tell him to sit. He turned a page in the thin book, and read from it in the same voice. "At the close of yesterday's sitting the respondent put a question to this panel about the reach of its own hand. A tribunal may always be asked how far its hand goes. It may never decline to look." He turned another page. "The panel has looked. The question is before it. The respondent will be heard standing, and while he is heard nobody will break in on him, from either table or from this bench, except the panel for order." He looked up. "The floor is the respondent's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:67:"A thing given on this record," said Yorlan, "is given for good. Nobody takes it back afterward, at any sitting, for any reason. From this moment the panel will hold every part of the challenge as proved. The respondent will say that he understands it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:81:"I read the code the way I'd read anybody I had to fight," he said. "Not for what each part can do to me. For what each part is standing on." He held his left hand out flat, palm down, at the height of his chest, and set his right hand flat beneath it. "The Compact's powers over a practitioner are all in the enforcement chapters: every sanction, every order, every removal. But the enforcement chapters can only make you answer for an obligation, and the obligations only fall on you once you've been classified. So the powers rest on the obligations, and the obligations rest on classification." He lowered the left hand onto the right, and then moved both down to the table. "And classification rests on one sentence. One. In the whole of the code there's only one place that says what the word means, and it's the first article of the charter. If you want to know how far the Compact can reach, there's no point starting with the enforcement chapters, where the reaching is. You start at the foot of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:85:He read it slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:89:He did not look up. He read it again, at the same pace, so that the roll and the room would both have it whole, and on the second reading he stopped after *shall mean* and let the two words stand on their own in the high quiet hall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:97:"So the panel has a fact to find, and it's a small one," said Cael. "Anybody with the books can check it. It's strange that it seems never to have been checked on any record I could find. The registry calls me shattered. The Warden's papers call me that on every page he showed you yesterday, and his challenge rests its whole weight there, on my status. So the panel only needs to know whether that designation is written down where the charter says a classification has to be." He laid his hand on the first volume of the schedule, closed, at the end of the row. "I'll ask the panel for its help. Help me find shattered in the schedule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:99:He opened the first volume, and began to read it aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:105:Havel had recorded a great many things since the summer. He had recorded depositions and petitions and once, at a district sitting on the coast, a dispute between two fishing guilds over a boundary stone, which had run for three days and turned on the meaning of the word *seaward*. He had never before recorded a man reading a schedule. Nobody read a schedule aloud. People cited it, by section and entry, in a breath; they did not open its first volume and begin, as the boy at the respondent's table had just begun, at the beginning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:107:"*The primary enumeration,*" read the boy. "*The Path families, in the schedule's order.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:111:He wrote it in the service's short hand, the little hooks and bars that let a recorder keep up with the fastest witness, and within a quarter of a page he hardly needed them. The boy did not read fast. He read at the pace of somebody who meant every word to be written down, clearly, a little slower than speech, and he stopped at the end of every entry for exactly as long as it took a pen to finish it. Havel noticed that by the fourth entry. By the tenth he understood that the boy had noticed the pen first, and had set his pace by it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:113:Every Path family had its name, and under it its ladder of tiers, Copper to Gold and the rank within each, and under that the notes of its admission: the year it was entered, the amendment that had entered it, any condition that had been attached. The boy read all of it. He read Stone with its four old notes and its one new one. He read Wind. He read Iron Skin, and Havel heard the stocky boy on the front bench shift his weight at the name and go still again. He read Glass and Blade and Ash and Anchor. He read the compound designations, which Havel had not known there were so many of, and the lineage variants, each with the district that had argued for it and the year it had been allowed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:119:A recorder learned to read a room by its coughs. He had been taught that by the old recorder who trained him, a woman who had sat at desks for forty years and claimed she could tell the hour of a sitting with her eyes shut by how often somebody cleared his throat. This room coughed often. A boy on the third bench had his chin on his fist. Two people at the back were whispering, and the clerk at the rope had turned his head toward them twice. The woman in the dark blue coat at the end of the bench had put her pen away. In the middle of the front bench the old archivist sat with her arms folded and her eyes shut, and Havel could not have said whether she was listening or asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:131:"*Contested-Standing,*" he read. "*Entered under the fourteenth amendment, with terms of review.*" He read the terms of review. "*Interim-Assessed.*" He read its conditions. "*Dual-Registered.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:133:Every category the Compact had ever tried before it made up its mind was in the annex, with the amendment that had let it in and the conditions on which it was let. The boy read every one, and every one had a name, and Havel wrote each name; he had written perhaps four hundred since the first word, and his hand had stopped asking him whether it could go on. They lay along his roll in a long dark ladder, every Path and every tier and every provisional standing the Compact had ever put its hand to. It came to him somewhere in the annex, quite suddenly, with the pen still moving, what it was that he was making.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:137:It was in the subject's file, in the field where the file's flag was entered. It was in the challenge, on its first page. Havel had seen it in the routing papers of the autumn request on Wednesday afternoon, under the line Ilsev had read three times. It had gone down the hill on Monday morning in the senior evaluation seat's referral, in the delegation's bag, on the first courier of the day. That had been while the magistrate was reading the notice into the record and the boy was saying his name. And it was not on Havel's roll. It would not be. Every name the schedule held was going down on Havel's roll in his own hand, and not one of them was that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:157:"And the panel declines the stipulation," said Yorlan. "A stipulated absence is an agreement between parties. The respondent appears to be offering the panel a demonstrated one. They are not the same object, and the panel prefers the better one." He did not look at the Warden. "The respondent will proceed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:171:After the annex the boy read the appendix of disputed standings. Every category in it had been forced on the Compact by one of its member guilds. The Compact had let each one in, and then set down beside it, in the guild's own words, that it had objected. The schedule kept even its own reluctance on the page, as if it could not bear to forget that it had once minded something. Then the boy read the categories the Compact had been asked to enter and had refused, which were bound at the back of the seventh volume, past the index. He turned back to the charter and read the cross-index at its foot, where the schedule sent anybody who could not find a thing. He had kept the amendment schedules for the end, one fascicle for every revision, because that was where the schedule ended. In them, struck through but left standing in their places, were the entries the Compact had once made and later taken back. The boy read the struck entries as carefully as the standing ones. He said *struck* before each, and the year of the striking, and then the name, and Havel wrote *struck* before each on the roll and the name after it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:183:The stocky boy on the front bench had both hands flat on his knees. The girl beside him with the wind-burned face had her chin up and her eyes shut. The girl beside her, the one with ink on her fingers, had her pen in her hand over an open notebook and had not written anything for half an hour; Havel had seen her stop. And the old archivist at the end of the bench had opened her eyes. She was watching the boy turn the pages of her books with an expression Havel did not have a word for, and did not try to find one. Beside her, on the empty place, the folded slip lay where she had put it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-57.md:189:The boy reached the last of the fascicles. He untied its faded tape, and opened it, and read the last amendment the Compact had ever made to its schedule, and the last designation in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:5:It was a low old room built from the same grey stone as the wall, with ceiling beams close enough that Brom ducked under the first one out of caution and then looked faintly embarrassed when he saw he hadn't needed to. Four other arrivals sat on a bench along the back wall with their bags at their feet, and a window let the afternoon in so that the light lay across the floorboards in a long pale square. Each of the three desks had its own clerk and its own line, and above each line hung a small painted board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:21:Brom went first, because his line was empty. His clerk was a brisk young woman with her sleeves rolled to the elbow. She took his papers, scanned them, and turned them face up on the desk in one smooth motion: a Copper-tier certification from a registered Arbiter station, Iron Skin Path, and a clean record of two seasons' documented circuit work with Vell's stamp at the bottom of every page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:73:"I'm required to read this aloud, each clause, and you'll confirm you understand it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:75:"I can read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:81:From where he stood, Cael could read the heading whether he wanted to or not. *Acknowledgment of Prior Standing.* The clerk read it in a flat, even voice: that Fenmark Academy had ended her enrollment; that the record of that ending was to stand as written; that her candidacy for re-certification at Greyvane was granted on the understanding that she accepted that record as accurate and did not dispute it as a condition of entry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:87:"There is a separate process for a formal dispute, should you wish to pursue one later. It is not connected to your enrollment here and would go through channels outside this academy." He tapped a small unmarked box at the bottom of the sheet. "You may note an intention to dispute. It doesn't affect your candidacy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:91:He turned the sheet toward her a little further and laid a pen beside it, and she read it herself, slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:127:She did not hand him to a clerk. She led him to the third desk, under the blank board, and sat on the far side of it herself. He registered that it could be read two ways — as a courtesy, because she had brought him here and meant to see him through it, or as a warning, because he was the kind of case that could not be trusted to a clerk. After a moment he decided it was both, and that both were reasonable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:135:The afternoon went in pieces. Papers came to the desk in twos and threes: a letter of acceptance; a copy of his circuit record with Vell's stamp; a form that asked for his Path and tier and rank, which Quenna read, frowned at and set aside; another that asked the same questions in different words; and a third, older, with a line reading *registry document attached*, which led to a long, quiet search through her folder. His registry document was in there. She had obtained a copy from somewhere, and he saw the corner of it as she leafed past, and the word in brackets, before she covered it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:139:A boy from the kitchen brought tea on a tray. Cael thanked him, held the cup in both hands, and forgot to drink it. The patch of sun from the window crept down the back wall, crossed the floorboards toward the desk legs, and went thin. The other clerks finished their days; the man with the half-moon spectacles nodded to Quenna on his way out, and the young woman who had enrolled Brom gave Cael a look he read easily as *better you than me*. The bench emptied. Somebody, somewhere, lit a lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:149:He read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:151:Beneath the category, in smaller type, a printed note explained what the category was. It was reserved for any prospective practitioner who *has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment*. Such students were usually children whose families had asked for early schooling before their certification appointments. A Path, a tier, a rank, any record from an Arbiter: the category conferred none of those. What it did confer was a seat in lectures, training under supervision, and academic standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:153:He read the category line again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:159:He read the category line a third time, and the gloss under it. *Has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:163:"I underwent it." His voice came out quieter than he meant it to. "I was fourteen. Denvash. There's a record."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:169:"There's a record, and the classification on it is one the Compact's own schedule never defines." She said it evenly, without softening it; she had never softened anything she'd said to him. "This category exists for anyone without a standard classification issued by an Arbiter. A child who hasn't Kindled has no standard classification. Neither do you. Yours isn't standard. The schedule has no line for what yours is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:177:"Every word on that page is used correctly, and it will bear any reading anyone cares to give it. That matters; that's what protects you." She leaned forward slightly. "What holds up less well is the purpose. The people who drafted the provision were thinking of twelve-year-olds whose parents wanted them to sit in on lectures. They were not thinking of you. Someday someone will read this form more carefully than a clerk does, and they will ask about the purpose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:179:"So someone will come and read it properly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:187:"Then we need to be established fact before they get here." She laid one finger on the seal. "A student with a monthly assessment record. Public, dated, signed by the people who watched. Each month you spend here, passing, in ink, makes the case against you a little harder to make."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:205:"Does anyone read them?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:267:Lira read it over his shoulder without asking, as she always had, and said nothing, only bumped his arm with hers once. And then the seventh bell rang, and the three of them got up and went to find out what an academy fed people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:285:Brom stood in front of his own door a moment before opening it, read his name on the board, and went in and shut the door softly behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:3:The felt was still on the post when he came into section four on Tuesday, grey and thick and wound three times round the old oak. Nobody had touched it since Karis wound it on a week ago, as if the post had been waiting all that time to be asked something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:13:"The stair isn't in my notebook." She wrote anyway, a short line, and he was nearly sure it said *stair* somewhere in it. "The lead is unchanged. A quarter of a breath. It hasn't moved since the first session, through the exhibition and the ribs and the week after. Whatever else changes in you, that doesn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:21:It came three times in twenty that afternoon, once more than last week. Every time, Karis wrote it down and read it back to him in his own words, and every time he listened to her read it and changed nothing, because she had heard him exactly as he had said it. When the hour was done Quenna signed the strip and tore it off and handed it to him, and went out with her slate under her arm. Brom went with her as far as the door and then stopped there, because Karis had not stood up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:39:What she was offering was not small, and he would not let himself pretend otherwise. He knew what it was from the other end, from the end where he had always stood without knowing he was standing there. Every name at the front of the binder had been there when something came to him, and not one of them had been told, beforehand or after, by anybody, that it could happen. Lira had not been asked. Brom had not been asked; he had held out his arm to be read because Cael had asked him to stop being careful, and that was not the same thing at all. Karis was offering to walk up to the front of that line on purpose, knowing what she had already worked out stood at the far end of it, with her notebook open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:71:He had stood at the residence wing door for a long minute before he came out. He had promised himself that he would carry nothing of his own across to her this week, and here he was, two days into it, carrying the largest thing anyone had ever handed him. He had very nearly gone back up the stairs. Then he remembered the rule they had made in Ardenmere and renewed on this same wall four nights ago: tell her when he knew a thing, and tell her when he wasn't sure. The only thing that would ever cost him with her, she had said, was knowing and keeping it. He came out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:135:"An observer may watch anything the academy lets anyone watch," she said at last, "and anything a student of full standing invites him to watch. That's what the category is. It's the one thing it gives you freely." She picked her pen back up. "You don't need my leave for it. It isn't one of my sessions, and it won't be in my record. I'd prefer to keep it that way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:145:"You've seen two things I do," he said. "The framework and the read. There are more than two. I won't say how many, or what, and none of it is for paper." He made himself hold her eyes. "Each of them came after I'd watched somebody for a long time. That much you'd already worked out, from outside. I'm telling you it from inside, so that you know what you're offering to stand in front of."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:153:They built it in under an hour, because they had done it once before and knew now where the joints were. Lira wanted the first clause and got it, and Karis wrote it in her small upright hand: *1. Consent for each session is given aloud at its start, and may be taken back with one word at any moment, without reason.* Brom wanted the second, because it had been his kind of clause the first time, and he wrote it himself in his large square capitals, three lines where anybody else would have taken one: *2. NOTHING ABOUT HOW IT WORKS GOES IN ANY RECORD THAT ANYBODY OUTSIDE THESE FOUR CAN READ.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:161:The third clause was Lira's again: that she and Brom would know the general shape of what was happening, and could come to any session as witnesses under the same rule as before, without notice. The fourth was Cael's, and Karis wrote it as he said it, word for word: her ledger for these sessions would record the conditions and the result and nothing else. And the old sixth clause still held, the one from the first terms that let him read every word she wrote about him before it existed anywhere else, and keep a copy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:167:Then she read the four clauses aloud, twice, as she had read the first terms, and set the pen down, and picked it up again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:177:Cael read it. Then he read it again, and then a third time, and somewhere in the third reading he understood why he could not stop. Nobody had ever before written a rule down to keep someone safe from him. Every rule he had ever met had been written to protect the world from what he was, or to protect him from the world, or to protect some office from having to decide anything about him at all. This one was written to protect a girl of sixteen from a boy across a table, by the girl herself, in a hand that had not shaken once. He was glad it existed. The gladness was not at all comfortable to hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:195:He had watched her Ember a dozen times from the edge of a section and twice in a formal yard, and he still had to make himself breathe when she worked close. She did not throw heat about as circuit Ember did, in sheets and gouts that cleared a ring. She set it. A point bloomed in the air at the height of her shoulder, a dim warm light the size of a coin, like a coal glowing through paper. It stayed exactly where she had put it, steady as a lamp, until she let it go on her count. Then it was gone, with that faint third of a breath of shimmer after it, like heat over a summer road. Then two at once, at the same height, a pace apart. Then two again, laid on the whitewash of the end wall, and this time she joined them. A thin line of heat ran along the wall between them with a sound like a kettle a moment before it sings, and held while both points held, and the smell of warm stone came across the room to him, and the hair on his forearms stood up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:197:*Drawn Channel*, he thought. *Iron Rank Three.* He had read the receipt once, on a card on the stable table, and he was watching it be spent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:201:He did not know what reaching was. He discovered that in the first minute, standing in a whitewashed room with Lira by the door and a line of heat singing on the wall between two coins of light. Every time, he had written down what happened after a fragment came: the sense of something settling into a place he had not known was empty, and then the notice, like a line somebody else had already written. He had never once made any of it begin. Wanting a thing turned out to be one skill and making it happen another, and he had only ever had the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:231:"Of course." Karis wrote one more line and turned the ledger round, as the fourth clause said she must, and he went down to her end of the room and read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:233:*Session one. Source: K., Ember Path, Iron Rank Three. Intensity: demonstration. Consent given aloud. Distance nine paces. Duration one hour. Declarations: single ignition points; paired points; one Drawn Channel, laid on the end wall; one sustained point, count thirty. Subject's reach: attention, invitation, want, rebuilt stillness, in that order. Fragment notice: none. Result: null. No change in source.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-15.md:79:"There's a file about me at Fenmark," she said finally. "I've never read it. But I know one word in it, because they read it aloud to me on the day they threw me out. *Insubordination.* And the man who read it said, so that I'd be sure to understand, that it meant I had no control of myself." She set the flask on the rail. "So when I take a hit now, I take it where everyone can see I chose to. The ones I slip, nobody remembers. The ones I take on purpose, they remember I didn't have to." She looked at Karis directly for the first time. "That isn't for your notebook."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-15.md:139:She went back across the ring in short, neat steps, her weight even, her eyes on his chest. Twice, when he came close enough to touch, she laid a single point directly against his leading shell, a small hot bloom the size of a coin, and both times the shell took it and burst in a spray of light and the point died with it, and a new shell was already coming. Cael watched her off hand. It went still a quarter of a breath before each of the two points, just as it had on the training floor, the free hand stopping wherever it was as if it were listening. Then the point came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-15.md:157:She laid six in the second exchange, one for each step she gave, and to the benches every one of those steps looked like retreat. Edran pressed into the space she left. He was good, and he was strong, and twice in the exchange he got close enough to strike, and twice she took the strike on her forearm and turned with it and went back another step and left another coin behind her. By the end of the exchange the south half of the ring held six small warm points in an uneven crescent, and Edran was standing in the middle of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-15.md:193:He stood where he was, on the one small island of unlined earth at his feet, and looked at the floor. Cael watched him read it, line by line, as he would have read an opponent's guard: the channels, the two open places, the line he would have to cross to reach the second place, and the line beyond that he would have to cross to reach her. Cael saw him weigh the cost. A channel was hotter than a point; it held while both its points held, and every one of her points was still holding. He could cross. He could burst a shell on the first line and another on the second, and he might reach her with whatever shell he had left, or he might not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-15.md:209:He had written a great deal. He looked at the page now, while the yard emptied round him, and read it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-15.md:225:Beside him, Lira had finished her apple and was looking at the ring with an expression he could not quite read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:53:"Who they were hiding from," he said. "It was never us. They didn't care whether I knew. All that care about not being seen was for other people, in case somebody asked what the Compact wanted with a boy in a market." He looked at the man by the well, who was looking at nothing. "Nobody needs to ask that now. It's on a public record, with a ruling on the bottom. They're not watching a boy. They're watching a hole in their own schedule, and anybody who wants to can read why." He took a bite of the apple; it was sour and very cold. "Openness is cheaper," he said, "once the watching is defensible."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:81:"It's aggressively boring," she said, handing it to him. "I read it three times looking for anything interesting in it and there isn't a single word. It's exactly the right register. That paper must never be interesting again, as long as it lives."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:89:Letters came up the hill on every coach, every one of them addressed to the provost. The provost's clerk had taken to calling them *adjudication-adjacent*, in the hearing of half the stable. Registry offices wrote asking for the transcript. Any of them could have sent for it through its own channel without troubling Greyvane at all; they wanted Greyvane to know they had it open on a desk. Two academies wrote, one from the coast and one Cael had never heard of, asking with great care and no apparent interest at all how the demonstration sittings were run. A guild's legal office wrote a letter so wrapped round in qualifications that Karis, reading the fair copy the clerk let her see, said it was the first letter she had ever read that cancelled itself out by the last line. It asked whether the academy expected to be heard again on the matter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:105:Karis read the ruling at the long table in the archive on the Saturday evening, with the record spread out in front of her, and she did not let anybody talk to her until she had finished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:107:It was a fair copy of the whole of it, both sittings, every exhibit, the deposition and the ruling, and it had come up from the delegation's recorder by way of the provost's office, because the record was public and the academy was a party. Prynn had certified it by ear in an afternoon. Karis had it weighted open at both ends with two of her own notebooks. Beyond the pillar the back of the archive was still bare, all nine cases of the founding stock empty to the old wall, and she did not look toward it once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:117:She turned the record round on the table so that it faced him, and put her finger on a line near the end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:119:"It's jurisdictional," she said. "Read it again. The panel didn't find your enrollment good. It found the Compact had failed to show its right. That isn't the same finding, and I'd like you never to let anybody tell you it is." She took her finger off the line. "A hole like this lasts exactly as long as nobody with the power to draft bothers to fill it. Somebody will. One amendment to the schedule. One line, entering your word, or entering some new word that means the same, and every sentence of your argument stops being true the morning it's posted. You won because the text said nothing. A text can be made to say something by anybody with a pen and the standing to use it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:127:"And the observer track," said Karis, "is finished. Whatever the ruling says. Naveth has re-papered you very well, and he can do it again as often as he likes, and it'll hold for a while. That provision got you through a gate because nobody looked at it hard, and on Monday two hundred people looked at it as hard as anybody ever will. It died in the assembly room on Monday, in the Warden's drafting records, whatever happened on Tuesday." She laid her palm flat on the record. "You're standing on a gap now. Their authority isn't there, and that's real. A gap is real. It just isn't anything you can put a house on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:131:"Something that's there. Not the lack of their right. A right of your own." She pulled her oldest notebook toward her from under the record and opened it at a page near the back, dense with her small hand in an ink that had gone brown. "I kept finding it on the founding shelves. Not the thing itself; its shape. The third clerk wrote down what the assessor said about a traveller in sentences. *Shown thus, and well. Shown thus, and not well.* Before the schedule had a name for everybody, the only test anybody had was whether you could do in front of them what you said you could. Judged by demonstration. Entered by what was shown." She touched the brown ink. "That's in every margin of everything old enough. If it was ever written as a rule, a real rule, with an article and a clause, kept somewhere well enough to stand being read by a magistrate, then that's sturdier than any provision written for travelling children. That's a basis. Something like that could carry you somewhere bigger than this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:137:Lira had been looking at the four of them from the bench by the door. She looked at the record and the notebooks and the bare cases at the back, with the party going on faintly up in the residence wing without them. Her face had gone soft, which it did for nobody but this table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:165:"May I read the Power Log?" she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:167:She said it formally, the way she had asked his leave in the archive on the first day, with nothing in her voice that would make it any harder for him to refuse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:183:"I'm always right," said Lira. "Read your log."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:187:She did not read it all that night. She read it at the table, slowly, from the front, with the lamp pulled close and her hand flat beside the page and never on it. The three of them sat with her and did not talk. She read the first pages from Denvash. She read Lira's section and Brom's, and stopped at Brom's, and Brom looked at the fire. Then she came to the pocket at the back of the binder. She drew out what was hers in it, one sheet at a time, from among the papers that proved things, and laid each one on the table in the order it came. There was her own consent in her own hand with Quenna's initials in the margin. There was her paragraph with its scraped-out ghost of a sentence, and the page she had cut out of her own ledger with the penknife. Last came a loose sheet dated the Sunday before the hearing: the second copy of her finding about him, two sentences in her small closed hand, which she had pushed across the archive table that afternoon and told him to put somewhere she would never know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:235:"Three," said Karis, and counted, and at ninety-four the warmth came back. Then she stopped counting and stood very still at the end wall, looking at the three marks on the grey oak in a row, the seed and the thumbnail and the coin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:241:"Mine costs by the size and the holding," said Karis slowly. "A bigger point costs more. A point held longer costs more, and the bill comes at the end, when I let go. Yours doesn't. A seed costs you what a coin costs you. Exactly what. The count comes back at the same place every time, near enough." She came forward and knelt by the post and bent close over the three marks. "So you're not paying for the fire. The fire's the wood's; I said that the first day. And you're not paying for the size. You're paying for something that's the same every time, whatever size you choose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:257:He wrote it kneeling on the scrubbed boards, with the binder on his knee, and read it aloud as he wrote, so that Karis would hear what went into the only record there was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-60.md:263:He read the line back, and then put one more under it, smaller, in the square hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:9:His coat hung on the back of the chair, where it had hung all night. He put it on and buttoned it, and as he did up the second button his hand went flat against the inside pocket without his telling it to, and stayed there. Two folded papers lay against his chest, one a little stiffer than the other. He did not take them out. He knew what they said, and he knew that if he read them now he would be reading them for luck, which was not what either of them had been given to him for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:61:Seven people had come up a hill in midwinter to watch a hearing at an academy most of them could not have found on a map a month ago. The record was public, and word of it had travelled faster than three weeks ought to have let it. He took that in, and set it down where he kept the things he would think about later, and reached the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:67:The lecturer's dais at the end had been raised on the platform from the assembly stage and turned into a bench, with three high-backed chairs behind it and a long green cloth over it. Above it the roof beams of the old waystation ran across the whole width of the hall, black with age, so close together that the plaster between them looked like light coming through a fence. Below the bench, on its own small platform, sat the recording desk. There were two tables on the open floor. The delegation's stood on the left, squared with document cases, and the respondent's stood on the right, bare except for a slate and a stick of chalk that were not his. A little behind and to one side was a third, where the academy's counsel sat with a satchel of papers and Naveth beside him, very straight, with his long hands folded on the wood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:77:Across the floor Coss had come in through the side door with his aide behind him. He was laying out his cases on the delegation's table in an order that had plainly been decided long before he reached the room. He did not look at the respondent's table. He looked at the cases, and then at the bench, and then he sat down with his hands flat on the closest case and waited. He was as still as he had been at a borrowed desk in Ardenmere, with the same patience that had always made him so hard to read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:81:The recording officer came in by the door below the bench, carrying the sloped wooden case. He set it on the desk and opened it, wound the roll a little on its spindles, and stood the brass glass upright at the top corner. He uncapped his ink and laid out two pens side by side, and then a third. Cael watched him do it and thought that whatever else happened today, that young man was going to write down every word of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:87:On the corner of the bench, at Ilsev's elbow, somebody had set a stack of dark-spined volumes, closed. Cael knew them without being able to read their spines from where he stood. He had held every one of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:95:"This tribunal sits under the charter, in public, and on the record. The matter is named in the notice. The parties are named in the notice. The panel will hear the challenge first, and the answer after. The panel will hear what is put to it and no more. The panel will not hear argument for which it has not been shown the ground. The respondent will state his name."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:105:He sat. Yorlan read the notice into the record: the matter and the parties, the challenge filed by the Compact's field service, the academy's counsel for the academy, the respondent for himself. He read the challenge itself in the same voice, every clause of it as level as a list of goods on a carter's bill. The sentences that had taken the whole of Cael's winter went past in the room no louder than the scrape of a bench. At the recording desk the young man's pen moved without stopping. Then Yorlan closed the thin book on his finger and looked, for the first time, at the room in front of him, not at any one person in it but at all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:107:"The panel will hear the challenge," he said. "The officer of record."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:113:"Magistrate. Assessors." Coss spoke as he always spoke, evenly, with no paper in his hand. "The Compact will put three things before the panel. First, what the respondent's provision was written to do, and for whom: its text says so, and so do its drafting records, and the Compact will produce both. Second, that the respondent is not one of the people it was written for: that is in the registry, and in his own words on deposition. Third, that whenever a chartered academy has stretched this provision over a person it was not written for, the enrollment has been voided, every time, and the Compact will produce each instance." He paused, not for effect, only because the next sentence was a separate thing and he gave it a separate place. "What the respondent is, the Compact leaves alone. That question is not before you, and the Compact will not put it there. The Compact asks the panel to read a paper and say what it says. The paper is plain. The Compact begins with its text."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:119:Coss read the provision first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:121:He read it in full, from the certified copy with Prynn's pencil at its foot, every sentence of it from *a candidate without a standard classification issued by an Arbiter* to *the panel's finding shall be entered in the candidate's record*. He read it slowly, and when he reached the end Yorlan said, "Again, for the roll." He read it again at exactly the same pace, with the same small pauses in the same places, so that the second reading sounded like the first laid over itself. Then he took the enrollment form, the certified copy of the one Cael had signed on his first afternoon. He laid it beside the provision and read the category line from it. *Unclassified observer.* Then he read the gloss beneath it. *Has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment.* He read that twice as well, without being asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:139:The first was a memorandum of the committee that had written the provision, long ago. It stated what the provision was for: academic access for the untested children of member families, travelling on a guild's licence, until they could be brought to a station and assessed. Coss read it plainly, and laid it down. The second was the first draft itself, the one Cael had read in the archive in its leaning, tired-looking hand, and Coss read the first two sentences of it and laid that down too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:143:It was a later page, from the same committee in the same years, proposing that the provision should also cover *candidates whose capabilities present irregularly and cannot be assessed under the standard schedule*. Below the proposal, in a different ink, somebody had recorded the committee's vote. The proposal had been struck. And below the vote, in a third hand, somebody had written down the reason in two plain sentences. The provision was a kindness to children awaiting assessment, and was not to be made a door for anybody the schedule could not hold. If such persons were ever to be provided for, it should be by the schedule and not by this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:145:There were signatures under it. Coss read them out, one by one, as names, without comment.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:159:And his own argument hoped for the opposite. It hoped that every page the Compact had ever written was still where it had been put. Tomorrow he meant to stand up and ask the room to read all of them, and to find that none of them said what everybody had always taken for granted they said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-54.md:163:Beside him, without turning her head, Quenna drew the slate toward herself and wrote on it in small square letters. She turned it a quarter toward him so that he could read it, and then turned it back and wiped it with the side of her hand before anybody else could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:7:Wind-adjacent answered first, as it always did, before he had finished turning toward it, light and quick and rested after two days without Lira's chalk. Pressure-adjacent sat where it had sat for a year, banked and unspent, a weight he could feel the edges of. Iron-adjacent was steady, a readiness along the skin of his forearms and the backs of his hands, and Compression-adjacent lay low in his chest, quiet as a coal under ash. Each one answered the moment he turned toward it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:33:At the fourth bell he went up to his room and wrapped his forearms in guard-cloth, both arms, wrist to elbow, wound firm and tied off with his teeth as he had done it a hundred times in Ardenmere. He flexed the hands. He put the binder in his coat and took it out again, because a binder was no use in a ring. He held it, and then went down the corridor and knocked on Lira's door and gave it to her. She took it without a word and put it inside her own coat, against her ribs, on the side where she kept Fenmark's four paragraphs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:55:At the front of the rail on the open side, near the south mark, stood Lira and Brom. Lira leaned forward on both forearms on the wood, as she did to read a floor, and Brom stood beside her with his feet planted and his hands behind his back, as solid as a wall somebody meant to keep. Neither of them waved. Everything they meant to say had been said over porridge, and it had come to an extra roll and two words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:65:"For the record," said Quenna.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:69:"Demonstration assessment, unclassified observer track. Fifth sitting. A supervised training match under standings rules, three exchanges on the instructor's count, with the faculty's discretion to halt. Present: the presiding assessor, the instructor's seat, the rotating faculty seat." She did not look up. "The opponent consents in writing. The outcome will be logged under the provision. The candidate takes the south mark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:73:He heard the conditions read and kept none of them, because his eyes were on Karis and hers on him. Between the two of them a conversation was going on that the panel could not hear, because it was not made of words. On each side of the chalk stood six weeks of looking: her notes knew his recovery to the count, and his knew her ignition to the quarter-second. Somewhere under all of it, in the back of a binder against Lira's ribs, sat the sentence neither of them would say in front of a panel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:99:He felt it before he saw it, a hiss and a sudden heat that climbed the back of his calf like a hand laid there. A thin bright line joined the point behind him to one beside him along the frozen earth, close enough that he smelled scorched wool. The yard made a sound. It was low, and nobody meant to make it; it was the sound of three hundred people beginning to wonder whether this was still a demonstration.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:123:The framework had been spent hard and was answering slower, with perhaps two more full-speed slips in it before the speed stopped being real. His read on her was gone, worthless, and he would have to build a new one at the speed of the fight. The floor was hers before it had started. Her tells were real but cut to slivers, her lag did not exist, and seven points stood between him and her already. He could not win the drawing; nobody could win her drawing, and Edran had said so, with a number.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:127:He stopped trying to win the geometry. He set his feet, and opened the read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:129:It came up along his skin as it always did, the readiness along his forearms and the backs of his hands and the front of his body. It was the weight-read he had taken from Brom and taught to find a body's lean through linen and felt and an oak post. In the quiet room, at three paces, it had begun to do something more: a thickening in the air at the place where her next point would bloom, a breath before it bloomed. Three times in twelve. Twice in the last session. Never more. But in the quiet room every point had been a demonstration, laid for a ledger with nothing behind it, and he had never once had the read open inside a real fight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:137:Karis came forward, laying as she came, and a breath before her first new point bloomed the air at the place it would bloom went heavy on his skin. It was faint and sure, a density, a lean, exactly where the light was about to be. At full economy every point she laid was a whole commitment, thrown down close and fast with everything she had behind it. A committed point leaned on the air as hard as a fighter's weight leans before a strike; the read had been made for exactly that. Each of her points reached him as a weight on his skin before it was a light. It was half a breath, no more, and against the precision of her floor half a breath was the difference between answering and only surviving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:143:It was a single small flicker across her face, there and gone, the researcher in her noting a reading that did not fit, at the speed of a fight. She did not stop or hesitate. She changed. The next three points did not go down near him at all; they went down far off, at the edges of the ring, four and five and six paces from him, well beyond where any read could feel the air lean. She had worked out from the way he moved that he could feel her points coming when they were close, and in the space of two steps she had moved her whole floor out to where he could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:147:A channel ran across the ring from one far point to another, seven paces long, singing, and swept the floor between them in half, and another lit beside it. The far points needed no reading once they were lit; he could see them as well as anyone on the benches. They were boxing the middle of the ring into narrower and narrower lanes. Inside the lanes her near points still bloomed close, and the read still gave him those half a breath early, but the long lines were coming from where he could not feel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:205:He made her pay for the early ground. It was the only coin he had left, and he paid it out honestly. The read gave him her near points half a breath early, and Lira's arithmetic moved his feet, *pick faster, the correctness comes later*. Three times in the first part of the exchange the floor closed round him in a way that should have been the end, and three times he was already gone before it closed. He did not choose any of the three. He went, and trusted the floor to have been read already, and it had. For a quarter of the exchange, perhaps, he stood only where her geometry had not yet been finished, exactly as Brom had said, never in one place long enough to be drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:211:Now she laid in pairs, and the pairs came faster than the read could price them. A pair went down at his left and joined as they landed, and the channel between them walled off the whole left side of the ring: one line, short, low and bright, that he could have crossed in a step. He could not afford to cross it. His arm knew it before his head did. She had seen him cross once and seen what it cost him, and she laid that wall on his left because she knew he would not pay twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:215:Then a channel lit ahead of him, across the one lane he had left. The framework read it and slipped him out of its line, slowly, a redirect that cost him everything it had left in that moment, and he came out of it into what should have been open floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:219:He knew it the instant he landed. The channel ahead of him had been laid to be escaped from, and while he was escaping it, spending his last slow slip, she had used the time the slip cost him to lay the true thing behind him. He felt it bloom at his back on the read, point after point, too many and too fast, the real assembly closing behind him while he was busy getting out of a trap built to be got out of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:231:Six weeks he had spent learning her. Not writing her down, though he had done that too, on more pages than he could count. Learning her, as he learned everybody, as he had learned Lira in a cold yard in Ardenmere before he knew he was learning anything. He knew the shape of her points and the rhythm of her joins and the exact gap between one commitment and the next. He knew the stillness in her off hand, and he could feel it now, through the read, gathering a tenth of a second ahead of the fourth channel's last point. His own handwriting was not more familiar to him. Until this half-breath he had never once had cause to reach for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:255:He felt it happen round him. The channels that had been about to light did not light. The count she had been holding everything on was gone, and her grip on the lit channels went with it, so that they faltered and went dark one after another as she lost them, a run of dying hisses along the earth. The whole careful structure of the exchange came apart from the inside, and what was left was sixteen separate small fires standing on the frozen ground of the formal yard with nothing to say to each other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:259:Her fourth channel lay ruined round her feet, her off hand still lifted a little at her side, and Cael, kneeling on the earth with his palm over a crooked coin of light, watched her understand what had just happened to her. She understood it a long moment before anybody else in the yard did. He watched it arrive in her face in stages, each one plain to him, because six weeks had taught him to read that face. First the fighter, at a lattice broken from inside by nothing she had prepared for. Then the researcher, quick as a struck light, running through every other explanation there could be and throwing each one away. And then the third thing, the thing under both of them, coming up through her face like heat coming up through a channel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:285:The lines came after, plain and flat, visible to nobody in the yard but him. He read them as they came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:297:Four times he read that last line, and four times it said the same thing. He had never seen it. Four fragments had come to him, and a notice with each, and every one of them had ended at the note, so that he had never once suspected there was room under it for anything more. Yet here it stood, in the same voice as the rest, as though it had always had its place. Wherever the notices were kept, that line had been ruled from the beginning. It had waited through Lira, through Feryn, through Brom and Reydan, empty each time, because nothing he had done had ever belonged in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:301:Four readings had not been enough, so he gave it a fifth. It had never been weather, then. Not once. He had sat under every fragment as if it dropped out of a sky, when the truth was that in all that time he had never put out his hand for the handle. Every record anyone had kept on him had got him wrong except this one, which had kept a space for exactly that difference and left it standing blank through four notices, and today the line was not blank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:313:Several things crossed her face together, and none of them stayed long enough to be named: awe, and the plain glee of a researcher whose result has come in, and under both of those something he could not read at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:325:Behind them the panel was breaking up. Quenna had gone back to the chairs with the ledger held against her, and Cael could already see the official language assembling itself behind her eyes. The yard was pouring its noise out through the gates. By supper there would be six stories, each arguing with the other five, and the true one would be the one nobody believed. There would be questions, and a record written slowly and with great care, and a conversation about what three people had seen this afternoon and what each of them would be willing to say they had seen; after that would come consequences he could not yet measure, because three hundred people had watched, and a thing three hundred people have seen does not go back to being unseen. All of that was on its way. All of it was real. None of it was allowed in before this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:13:"For the record," said Quenna. "Supplementary recorded bout, elected by the candidate under the reconsideration clause of the re-certification provision. The candidate: Gerda, Ash Path. Recorded tier, Copper. The bout will be considered together with the candidate's formal review, and advancement assessed against the originating record. The candidate takes the south mark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:35:She laid the haze again and came out of it again. This time when the fast hand came she took it on the forearm and turned from it, and the turn put her in under his arm, close, closer than a Blade fighter ever let anybody get. She put her shoulder into his side with the whole of her weight and the whole of her height, the way she had put it into Hobb on the fourth evening. A Blade fourth-year was not a Stone fourth-year. He went three steps sideways and nearly off his feet, and her two fingers were on his breastbone before he had them back under him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:49:"Supplementary recorded bout. Won by the candidate, two touches to one. The panel considers it together with the formal review." She read off the ledger in the slow flat cadence of the record. "The candidate came out of her own declaration under live opposition and committed fully, at cost, twice. The recommendation of reconsideration is withdrawn. Continued track."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:55:Gerda walked to the table and signed, carefully, every letter separate. Then she stood and read the line Quenna had written, as Lira had stood and read hers, and Cael saw her lips move once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:77:Lira sat on the floor by the door with her knees up and Gerda's laugh still visible somewhere at the corners of her mouth. Brom sat beside her with a heel of bread he did not take out of his coat. Karis stood at the far end of the whitewashed room with the ledger closed on the boards at her feet. Cael stood three paces from her, as close as the room had ever brought them except for the one morning at a single pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:81:She had told him at breakfast what the last session would be. It would not be a variable; it would be all of them, everything the twenty-one before it had tried, put into one hour at the nearest distance and the longest duration she could sustain without her points going dirty, and she would run it fresh. He would reach every way he had ever reached, in turn and then all at once, with the read open on his skin and every channel of attention he owned turned toward her like lamps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:83:"If it doesn't close in that," she had said, "it doesn't close in this room. And then we'll know that, which is the finding."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:87:He knew her now. That was what he understood, standing three paces from her in the last hour of the series while the first point bloomed at the height of her shoulder and held. He knew her better than he had known anybody he had ever watched, better than he had known Reydan at the top of the bout and better, in this one narrow way, than he knew Lira. He knew where she would lay the next point before she laid it. Twice in the hour, with the read open, he felt the air at that place grow faintly heavier before the light came, as it had on the long afternoon. He could have shut his eyes and rebuilt any one of twenty-two sessions from the sounds alone, the small hiss of a point, the kettle-song of a channel, the click of her pen. He had learned her past memory, down into the place below memory where Lira's footwork lived in his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:97:"Null." She wrote it, in the same careful hand, at the same size, as she had written the first. *Session twenty-two. All conditions of sessions one to twenty-one, combined. Result: null.* Then she did not close the ledger. She looked at the page, and drew a line across it under the last session, straight from one side to the other, as a person rules off a column of figures before writing the total.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:99:Under the line she wrote one more sentence. She did not read it aloud. She turned the ledger round and held it out across the three paces so that he could read it under the fourth clause.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:119:"I know I don't." Karis did not look away from Cael. "I've decided it anyway. I'm only telling you, so that it's on the record somewhere honest, which is this room, with the three of you in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:131:Karis looked at him, and then at the ledger. Then she uncapped her pen and wrote, under the line, under the sentence about the missing condition, in her small upright hand, and turned it round so that Brom could read it from the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:135:Brom read it twice. He sat back against the wall with his bread in his hand and an expression of such deep and peaceful satisfaction that Lira, beside him, laughed until she had to put her head down on her knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:139:After supper he sat down at the desk by the narrow window and took out a clean sheet of paper. The third-day post went down the hill in the morning. Hesk had written that he read both kinds of letter the same, and this week had been mostly the second kind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:151:*Brom found out from his teacher that there's a gap in the way he turns a strike that only somebody very fast could use, and nobody here is fast enough to teach him to close it. He took it the way he takes everything, by eating more barley.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:205:He read it standing in the doorway with one hand on the latch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:217:*Session nine at Ardenmere is kept, marked real, unexplained, keep. Compression-adjacent: the whole slow push once, a quarter of a fast strike once. Wind-adjacent lead held, narrowly.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-30.md:3:Lira came to Brom's room for the fast catch on the Wednesday night of the eleventh week. She had read the page about the whole push. She had told him at breakfast, cutting his porridge into squares, that the next time he did something new to his chest she was going to be in the room for it with a cold cloth and a very bad temper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-30.md:37:He came back slowly. The cloth was on his breastbone, cold and wet through his shirt. Lira was kneeling over him with one hand flat on top of it, pressing, not hard, and her face was very close and very white. Brom was on his other side holding his left wrist in both hands, chafing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-30.md:67:*Compression-adjacent. Brom's room, L. present. First fast catch: a quarter of a quick palm strike, caught and returned, on the eighth try. Done by waking the fragment before the strike, held half-ready with the road laid open. Waiting for the force, the way I do with a push, doesn't work on a strike; it's gone before the fragment wakes. Cost: all of it at once, no delay. A white flash, legs gone, on the floor. Couldn't breathe for long enough that Lira used my name the way she did on the road. Left hand numb, then pins, for most of an hour. Integration: partial. A quarter of a strike, once.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-30.md:69:He read it back, and then he added the line Lira had asked for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-30.md:73:Lira read it over his shoulder, upside down, from where she sat on the floor against the bed. She did not say anything. She took the binder out of his hands, closed it, and put it on Brom's shelf in the neat row beside Brom's crest-up tag. Then she put her hand on his forehead for a moment, and took it away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-30.md:89:He had known she would. His recovery between passes at the working speed had gone from three counts to five. The framework, which had not minded the ribs, minded this. The quarter of a breath of lead that Karis had measured since the first week held, but only just, and twice in the hour he felt it thin. Karis timed it and wrote it down without comment and read it back: *Recovery slower by two counts. Lead held, narrowly. Cause not stated by subject.* He said nothing to that. The cause was a strike in Brom's room, and it belonged to neither of the two arrangements and to nobody's ledger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-30.md:121:They did not leave. They sat on the floor of the quiet room with their backs against opposite walls and the two notebooks open on their knees, her ledger of the nulls and his own pages of session notes, all of her Path and none of his. She would read a line from the ledger and he would find the same session in his pages, to see whether what she had seen from outside matched what he had felt from in. It had become a ritual since the third week without either of them deciding it would: two notebooks open and two pens moving, in a whitewashed room with the high window going grey.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-30.md:141:"Months ago. Before Greyvane, before the road. A session with Brom, in his sparring circle in Ardenmere. The read was open; we were working on it the way we always worked on it. Nothing about that afternoon was different from a hundred others." He could still see the circle, the scuffed chalk, Brom's forearm under his hand. He did not reach for anything else. "And for less than a second, through the read, there was something like water. Water moving, somewhere there wasn't any water. Then it was gone, before I could even turn toward it. Nothing came after it. No notice. I've never had it again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-30.md:169:She opened the ledger again, but she did not turn to the nulls. She turned past them, past the plan she had drawn for the last sessions, to a page near the very back that he had never seen. There was a single short line on it in her small upright hand. She must have written it in the few minutes he had been talking. He could read it from across the room only because she turned the ledger toward him. *Session nine, Ardenmere, with B. One reading, Tide-like, under a second. Never repeated.* Beside it she made a small mark with her pen. It was not a letter or a number, nor any symbol he knew, only a short precise shape, made slowly and deliberately, as a person signs a thing they mean to stand behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:5:Quenna had told her first, on the stair the week before, in the voice she used for the record. Gerda had told her second, at supper, with the exact words of the procedure: a member of the gallery who disturbed the sitting might be removed by the clerk, and a cough counted, if the clerk decided it counted. Cael had told her third, at dusk in the swept yard, by asking her to write it down, which was worse than being told. So she sat on the second bench from the front at the left, by the aisle, directly behind Oona's straight back and polished tag, with her arms folded so that her hands would have somewhere to be, and she kept the list in her head instead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:31:"The officer of record may put questions," said the magistrate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:33:The Warden stood. He did not come round his table. He stood behind it with his hands at his sides and looked at Edran across the open boards, and Lira thought he looked at him as if he had already read everything Edran was going to say and was only checking the copy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:55:"I don't know what it was," he said, to the bench, before the clerk could lift the rope for him. "What he did. I'd like that on the record as well. I'm not saying it to help him. It's only true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:73:"The officer of record may put questions," said the magistrate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:91:He did it so quietly that half the gallery did not notice the case had changed direction. He took the certified copy of the enrollment form from the first case, where it had lain since the morning, and set it on the green cloth's edge in front of the bench. Beside it he laid a single registry sheet, which Lira could not read from where she sat but could guess at. Then he read the gloss under the category line once more, slowly. *Has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment.* Then he read from the registry sheet the name of a station in Denvash, and a date, and the word *assessed*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:97:Then he read the deposition into the record.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:101:He read the respondent's name and number as the registry held them. He read the question about the enrollment form and the signature, and the answers, which were *yes*, and the date. He read the question about how many times the deponent had read the category line before he signed it, and the answer. "*Three.*" Somewhere behind Lira a girl drew in her breath. He read the questions about the circuit at Ardenmere, and the answer that the circuit's ledger said so. He read the question about what the deponent's architecture was, in his own words, and the answer, "*That's a classification question.*" He read the question after it, and the answer, "*I understand that everything may be noted. That's what a record is.*" At that one a ripple went along the front bench that was not quite anything. Lira could not tell whether it was approval or the start of a laugh, and neither, she thought, could the people making it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:103:He read the question about the sixth sitting. *Before that sitting, was that capability entered in any record of this academy?* "*No.*" *Why not?* "*Because I hadn't elected to show it. The provision lets the candidate elect.*" *Had you possessed it for some time?* "*Four weeks.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:105:He read that without any weight on it, and went on to the next page, and Lira saw the panel's three heads all go down at once to their papers and come up again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:107:Then he read the question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:113:"The record marks a silence of nine counts after the answer," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:117:"*Then why did you sign it?*" read the Warden. "*Because it was the door the provision had, and I wanted to go to school.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:121:Lira had heard all of it before. She had heard it at the scarred table on the Wednesday, in Cael's own voice, with his bread in front of him and his hands turning it over. She had torn that bread in half and given him both halves, because she had not known what else to do with her hands. She had heard it then as a true thing told among friends. Now she was hearing it in a stranger's patient voice, read off a stranger's page to two hundred people and three officers of the Compact and seven who had come up a hill to listen, and it was the same words and not the same thing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:125:And the Warden stood beside his cases and let it lie, as he had let everything lie, and Lira understood for the first time why Cael had stopped being angry at him. The man had not twisted it. He had not needed to. He had read the boy's own words exactly as they were said, the nine counts included. The words were true, and they were the best thing in his whole case, and he had set them down without once pretending they were anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:135:The yard had filled the moment the doors opened, all of it in knots, all of it talking low, and the four of them came out into it and every knot turned to look. Brom did not say anything. He walked them across the yard, round the end of the lecture wing, to the narrow angle where its end wall met the old wall of the yard, out of the wind and out of the way, with nothing in it but a broken barrow. He stood in the mouth of the angle with his back to the yard and folded his arms, and the yard, after a moment, found something else to look at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:181:He gave each its district and its year and its name. The first was Sarnholt. A chartered academy there had enrolled under the provision a youth who was past the age of Kindling and had refused to be tested, on the reasoning that a person who would not be assessed had not yet been assessed. The enrollment had been voided inside a single sitting. The second was Wexley, where a registry clerk had made an honest error that nobody caught for three years, so that a girl who had Kindled and been classified was entered on an academy's rolls as untested. When it came to light the academy had defended the enrollment on the ground of the girl's good standing, and lost, and had spent two years under review of its own charter for having tried. The third had no name read out, only its district and its year, because it had never come to a hearing at all. A disputed assessment, an enrollment under the provision, a challenge filed; and the academy had withdrawn the enrollment before the magistrate arrived.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:191:He read Wray's line from the first, the seven words she had chosen and counted and would stand by. "*Unorthodox architecture. Consistent execution. No safety concerns.*" At the side wall Cael saw her not move. He read the second's and the third's. He read Quenna's from the fifth, the match that had been halted, word for word, in the voice he used for everything. "*Atypical adaptive response, consistent with previously documented irregular architecture. No safety violation noted. Assessment satisfied under demonstration provision.*" And then he took the last sheet in the folder, which was the sixth, and held it a moment before he read it, as if to be sure of the date.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:193:"*The candidate demonstrated, at his own election, an ignition-point capability, contact-adjacent, single channel. Three times, at a size stated in advance, with a rest between. Consistent, observable practice. No safety violation noted. Assessment satisfied.*" He laid it down. "Entered as of its own date. The Compact offers the record of the sittings as the academy's own account of what the respondent can do. The Compact does not dispute a word of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-55.md:197:It held. That was what he noticed most, sitting there. The dullest record in the room had been read aloud by the man who most wanted to make something of it, and there had been nothing in it to make anything of. It was a thing with a size. It did what it was said to do, three times, with a rest between. It held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:9:Gerda came through the gate as the bell finished, with her ash-pale hair cut close and her patched coat folded over her arm and her hands wrapped. On her way to the ring she stopped, and looked up at the clock above the main hall door, and checked it against something in her head. Then she went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:11:"For the record," said Quenna. "Re-certification review, formal. The candidate: Gerda, Ash Path, re-certification track. Two recorded bouts before the panel, under standings rules. The originating academy's record of finding has been received and read. The candidate's recorded tier is Copper, Rank Nine at the date of the originating record. The finding is termination of enrollment for failure to meet the standing requirement." She turned a page. "The panel will assess advancement against the recorded tier. First bout. The candidate takes the south mark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:19:Cael had seen it twice from the rail and once in the near corner of the open floor with a first-year walking slowly through it, and it was still strange to watch at close range. It came off her low and grey and spread along the swept earth like smoke that had decided to lie down, ankle-deep and then knee-deep, two paces out from her in every direction. Anything that moved inside it moved as if through cold honey. A strike thrown into it arrived late, and a foot set down in it came down slow. She stood in the middle of it with her hands loose and waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:35:The second bout went to her, and it was worse to watch than the first. The opponent was the second-year Current Path girl whose footwork never stopped, the one Cael had watched in his first fortnight. She came into the haze without a moment's hesitation, because she did not know any better, and the haze took her feet. Inside it her endless footwork became slow and pale and easy to read, and Gerda could have touched her four times in the first exchange. Cael saw her see it. He saw her not take it. She waited until the girl had slowed almost to a stop in the grey, and then she reached out and touched her breastbone with two careful fingers, as if she were laying a cup down on a table she did not trust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:45:"Re-certification review, formal. Two recorded bouts. The first lost by the candidate, the second won. The panel's notation." Her voice did not change. "Performance consistent with recorded tier. No advancement demonstrated against the originating record. Recommend reconsideration of candidacy at the term's end."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:49:Gerda walked to the table and signed. She did it carefully, every letter separate. Then she straightened and looked up at the clock above the main hall door, and Cael saw her lips move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:57:Nothing had gone wrong. That was the whole of it. Nobody had cheated her, or lied, or made a sentence up out loud at the end of a table. The procedure had worked precisely as its writers meant it to. Three people had read her originating record and watched her fight twice on a cold morning and written down what they saw. What they saw was a careful Copper fighter who did not move out of her own haze unless she had to and never once took a risk she could avoid, which was the tier and rank the record said she was, and so the record and the floor agreed. Yesterday a floor had disagreed with a file, and the disagreement had been written down and would stand. This morning the floor had agreed with one, and the agreement had been written down too, and it would stand just as long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:101:"The three bouts were entered as forfeits," Gerda said. "Correctly. I wasn't there. A forfeit is what you call a bout where one fighter isn't there. Three forfeits in a term means the standing requirement isn't met, and that means a review, and the review looked at the record and found three forfeits, which were there." Her hands did not move on the table. "I filed a petition. I said I'd had leave. It was posted. It was answered. There was no record of leave. The forfeits stood."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:105:"Correctly." Gerda looked at her bowl. "That's the thing nobody understands, when I say it. Everyone wants somebody to have been cruel. Nobody was. Everybody did their job. The instructor meant to write it and didn't, and the clerk entered what happened, and the review read what the clerk entered, and the petition was answered by people reading the same page I was reading. If any one of them had done anything wrong I could have stood up and said so. They didn't. That was the worst of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:147:At the end, when Cael had said nothing for the third time in his life and she had written the result, she turned the ledger round so he could read it under the fourth clause, and he read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:149:*Session two. Source: K., Iron Rank Three, demonstration intensity, consent given aloud. Distance nine paces. Duration one quarter-hour. Declarations: single points only, eight. Subject's reach: attention alone, held throughout. Witness: B. Witness eating. Result: null.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:161:The third was that evening, after supper, at five paces. Karis worked in pairs, two points at a time a pace apart, held to a count of twenty and let go together. Cael tried invitation, with his eyes shut between each pair and open for the bloom, the space held open in him beside the four like a hand held out across a room. Nothing came of it. *Session three,* the ledger said. *Source: Iron Rank Three, Ember, demonstration intensity. Distance five paces. Declarations: paired ignition points, held to count twenty. Subject's reach: invitation, eyes closed between declarations. Notice: none. Result: null.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:163:The fourth was on Sunday morning, before the first bell, with nobody else in the quiet room at all. Karis had asked whether he wanted to try it close, and he had said yes, and they had argued a little about how close. She settled it by walking up to a pace from him and saying, "Here. If it's too near, I'll know before you do." From one pace he could feel the heat of her points on his face. Each one bloomed a hand's width from his cheek, steady as a coin of lamplight, and the warmth came across his skin like standing near a hearth with his eyes shut. He wanted, plainly and with his whole chest, as hard as he had ever wanted anything. Nothing happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:167:"Null." She wrote it. She looked up at him from a pace away, close enough that he could see the tiredness at the edges of her eyes that he had not known was there. "Was it too near?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:213:On Monday morning of the tenth week, in the archive, Gerda read the reconsideration clause aloud to Prynn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:221:"Sit," she said to Gerda. "You'll read it to me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:223:Gerda sat. She read it, one phrase at a time, waiting at each comma while Prynn's pen caught up, in her flat exact procedure voice. *A candidate whose review recommends reconsideration.* The pen scratched. *May elect, before the term's end.* Prynn's pen stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:231:"You read *may*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:235:"It does." Prynn dipped her pen. "Most people who have been bitten by a procedure read *may* as *may not*. They hear it as a door somebody might shut. It isn't. It's a door you hold." She wrote. "Go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:237:Gerda went on. When she reached the end Prynn read her copy back against the book, silently, with one finger moving down each, and signed the certification at the bottom and blotted it, and handed it to Gerda without a word. Gerda took it in both hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:239:At the assessors' room at the third bell, Lira stood beside the desk while Gerda wrote the request beneath the certified clause in her careful square hand, every letter separate. The candidate elected to fight one supplementary recorded bout before the term's end, as the provision allowed. At the bottom she wrote the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:243:Quenna took the sheet, read it, initialed it in the margin, and wrote something in the calendar book on her desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:245:"The last day of the eleventh week," she said. "The first bell. The same panel." She looked at Gerda over the desk, and something passed across her face that Cael, standing in the passage outside the door, could not read. "Filed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-40.md:5:"One," she said. "Contact only. Your hand on the thing, not near it, not pointed at it, not waved at it from across a room because you've decided you're clever. Two. One point. Not two, not a row, not a pattern. One, and then you stop and we look at it." She held up a third finger. "Three. On something that isn't you and isn't any of us. Wood. Stone if there's no wood. Not a floor anybody has to walk on afterward and not a wall anybody has to sleep against."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-40.md:37:He went to his supervised session in section four at the fifth bell with the warmth sitting low in his chest. He ran the framework for Quenna at the chalk and read the post through felt with Brom leaning on the far side, and nothing in him moved toward the new one. It sat where it had sat since Thursday, faintly and steadily warm, like a stone in a wall that has been in the sun all afternoon, and it did nothing at all, and he did not ask it to. Quenna signed the strip and handed it to him and did not mention the match, and he did not mention it either. Her question lay between them on the chalk like a coin neither of them would pick up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-40.md:47:He had not meant to use the arm. The burn under the loose linen had stopped beating and begun, over the last two days, to itch, which Wray had said was the good sign and Lira had said was the worst part. He kept it close against his side and knelt by the bucket so that the top of the post was level with his chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-40.md:105:Brom lifted the scoop. Karis brushed the grey crust of sand away with two fingers and bent over the ring of char, close, with the absolute attention she gave a timing that did not agree with anything she had written before. She did not touch the black. She held her hand flat above it, a finger's width off, and then laid one fingertip on the oak beside the ring, and then on the ring's brown edge, and took it away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-40.md:149:"Contact," said Karis softly. "Or close enough to it that the difference wouldn't show on any instrument I know of. Not a finger's width. A hair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-40.md:165:He put the binder on his knee on the scrubbed floor and wrote it, and because Karis had asked to write nothing, he read it aloud as he wrote, so that she would hear what had gone into the only record there was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-40.md:167:*Tuesday, week seventeen. Quiet room, sixth bell. L., B., K. present, K. writing nothing. The new one, first deliberate use. Contact only, right palm, an oak post in sand. It doesn't gather and it doesn't glow. One instant, and then the wood is burning, an ordinary small fire, which keeps going until it's put out. Size: the first came bigger than I asked. Choose the size first; the second came most of the way. A finger's width off: nothing. A hair off: yes. Cost, every time, after: a cold drop under the breastbone, cold down the arm into the hand, fingertips grey, and the warmth gone until it comes back, about a hundred count. Watchers can't see it. L. watched my face and saw nothing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-40.md:179:He came to understand, over those two afternoons, that this was going to be the whole of the work for a long while. It was the same work he had done with every fragment he had ever been handed. Finding out what it could do was the easy part, and could be done in an hour. The work was finding out what he could make it do on purpose, every time, with nobody holding his hand. The Wind framework had taken him the better part of a year in Ardenmere before it would stay at the speed he asked for. Compression had taken him the whole of the autumn to get from a quarter to a slow push. He did not expect this one to be quicker because it was newer, and it was not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-40.md:189:He did three on the Thursday. All three came a thumbnail across, near enough that Karis, kneeling by the post with her face a hand's width from the oak, could not choose between them. They sat in a row on the grey wood, three small black rings with brown edges, each one crusted with sand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-40.md:197:He had been at the Thursday session in section four an hour before, as on every Thursday, with Quenna at the chalk and Brom leaning on the post, and the warmth had sat in him through all of it like a coin in a pocket, and he had not spent it. Quenna had watched him read the post and had asked him nothing. But on the way out of the section she had stopped him at the door, with her slate under her arm, and said only, "You're pale. Sleep tonight," and looked at his hands, and then at him, and gone. She had not asked why. She had been not asking why since the first week of the term, and it had never once sounded like not caring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:19:He ran it at panel speed. Hobb struck and he read the strike off Hobb's hips and shoulders and stepped into the space beside it, and the next, and the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:21:Wray did not break the count this time. Instead, on the third pass, she raised two fingers instead of one, and Hobb came in from the left twice in succession, the second strike following the first so closely that the read for it began before the first had finished. Cael felt the framework stretch to cover it, a half-beat of reading laid over another half-beat, and he moved at the pace he had chosen and not a finger faster, and nothing landed. He finished the pass and came back to his mark, breathing evenly, and three pens moved at the end of the hall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:23:The surface read went as it had before. Four calls, the last with his eyes closed, every one of them right. When he named the fourth, Hobb's weight going forward onto the ball of his left foot, Hobb did not huff this time. He only nodded very slightly, and waited for his next instruction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:31:Cael stood in the ring and counted the words without meaning to. Last month there had been seven. This month there were six. Wray had looked at a second sitting and found nothing in it that needed a new word, and he understood, standing there, that this was the highest compliment her dialect could offer. Nothing new to write was the best thing a record could say about you, when the person reading it was looking for something new.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:83:"I've got a page on it." She angled her notebook toward him. At the top of a sheet, in her small upright hand, he read *Handwriting, size of, under load*, and under it a column of dates. "Clause six. You can read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:85:He read it. It was exact and dry and held no theory, only measurements: how big his letters had been the day of the table, the day of the first session, and every day since the request. Each of the last three was a little smaller than the one before. At the foot she had written *Cause unknown. Probable.* He laughed out loud, which surprised him, and slid the notebook back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:123:The building was small, two storeys of the same grey stone as everything else in the town, with a narrow door and a single window and nothing over the door but a plain board. He knew what it was before he read the board. *Registered Arbiter Station. Certification and Evaluation.* The orientation instructor had told the new students, with his pointer tapping on the floor, that this was where they would come down the hill to be measured, and where the observer children would come down the hill to be Kindled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:143:Halfway up, he looked back once. The family had gone inside. The door was shut. Oona would come down this road in winter, with her own hands clasped, and he would be at the top of it, waiting. He decided, on the steep part of the road with the wind in his face, that whatever else he was doing in winter, he would be there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:149:Prynn read the slip at her high desk by lamplight, all of it, including the provost's signature, which she held closer to the lamp as if checking that it had not been forged. Then she climbed down from her stool, took the bound statute book from its shelf, laid it on the long table, and opened it without looking to the right page, as she had on the first day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:151:"Sit," she said. "You'll read it to me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:153:"Read it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:155:"Aloud. Slowly. While I write." She set a sheet of heavy paper square to the table's edge, uncapped a pen, and dipped it. "A copy that's certified has to be exact. Every word, every comma, nothing added and nothing lost. My eyes are seventy-odd years old and they've read this half page so often that they'd happily tell me what it says without looking. Ears are more honest. Go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:157:So he read it to her, the provision he had now read five times on his own, one phrase at a time, waiting at each comma while her pen caught up. *A candidate without a standard classification issued by an Arbiter.* The pen scratched. *May be enrolled for academic exposure and supervised training.* The pen scratched. She wrote in a small, exact, unhurried hand, and the only other sounds in the archive were the lamp and the cold settling of old paper on the shelves behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:159:Halfway through he read *the panel's finding shall be entered*, and she stopped him with one raised finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:163:He looked at the book. "The panel's finding shall be entered in the candidate's record."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:165:Her eyes came up over the spectacles and stayed on him. "You read *shall*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:169:"It does. Most people who read this to me say *may*, because they expect a provision to be polite. It isn't. It's a command." She dipped her pen again. "You read what's written, not what you expect. That's rarer than you'd think. Go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:171:He went on. When he reached the end she read her copy back against the book herself, silently, word by word, with one finger moving down each. Then she signed the certification at the bottom in the same small hand and blotted it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:191:Prynn's copy of the provision lay on top, in her small exact hand, and Naveth had read the pencilled note on its cover with great satisfaction before rubbing it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:197:"Present."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:201:"Present, certified by the archive."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:205:"Two sittings. Present, certified by all three seats."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:207:When the last item had been read and checked, Naveth wrapped the bundle in oiled cloth, tied it, and pressed the academy's seal into the wax at the knot while Quenna watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:215:He gave Cael the file copy of the transmittal on the way back up the stair, because Cael asked for it, and because he read everything that touched his own name. Cael read it on the landing by the light of the window, and again, slowly, in his room, and the second time something in it snagged and would not come loose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:227:She held the transmittal out at the full length of her arm, which was how she held anything she meant to read properly, and she looked at the routing box for a good deal longer than Naveth had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:245:By now the bundle would be on the road in the courier's bag, wrapped in its oiled cloth. In a day or two it would come to rest on a desk. A knife would go through the string. Someone would read the six words Wray had given the second sitting and the two Quenna had given both, and the rotating seat's long, careful paragraph about an anticipation that grew thinner as opponents grew quicker.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:267:Cael laughed with them, and then sat between them in the dusk without talking. Before the light went he opened the binder and wrote, slowly, in his squarest hand. A thing that would stay written ought to be written exactly, even when being exact did not make it any easier to read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-14.md:271:He shut the binder. Down in the town the lamps were coming on, one after another, along the one street worth walking. Somewhere past the last of them a courier's horse was carrying three certified signatures and two sittings' worth of record down a road whose end Cael would never see, toward a desk where he would never sit, for a reader who had asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:21:"*The challenge rests on documents*," Oona read aloud, slowly. "What does that mean?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:37:Hobb came down the passage from the training hall with his hands in his pockets and his feet a shoulder-width apart even when he walked, and the first-years flattened themselves against the wall to let him by. He stopped behind the crowd and read the board over all their heads, without hurry, and then he looked at Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:49:"He said four words," she said. "In a row. I counted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:55:Edran came last, from the stair of the lecture wing, with his arms folded and his flat honest face giving nothing away. He read the board once, and did not stop to read it twice, but came along the edge of the crowd until he was beside Cael and stood there with his eyes on his own name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:73:Cael stood a moment longer in front of the board, with Oona at his elbow and the first-years pretending not to look at either of them. *Witnesses of fact entered for the challenge: none.* Coss had come up the hill with a case he did not need a single living person to stand up for. Everything he had would be read out of a document case, in a patient voice, and every word of it would be so. And everybody who would stand up on Monday and open their mouths would be somebody who had stood across a chalk line from him, or sat on a bench and watched, and had decided by themselves to say so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:87:He stopped and looked at them with the lamp in his hand. They were plain flat boxes of the kind a draper packs cloth in, stacked very square, the way Prynn stacked anything she had been made to let go of, with a slip of paper tucked under the string on the top of each one. He bent and read the nearest without touching it. *Founding stock, case one, shelf two. Yearbooks, eleven volumes.* Then the marks, eleven of them, in a clerk's careful hand that had plainly been asked to go slower than it liked. Then a signature, and under it, in Prynn's small dry hand, *Counted at the door.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:97:He did not open the notebook, because he had nothing to open in it. He sat with his hands on his knees and watched her read, as he had for three nights at the long table, and then stopped watching her and only sat, and listened to the archive.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:113:It was not that she stopped moving, for she stopped often, when a line wanted reading twice; it was what kind of stopped it was. Karis at work was never quite still. There was always the pen, or the paper knife, or the small movement of her lips over a number, or her eyes going back a line and forward again. This was not that. Her hand lay open on the page, fingers spread, pinning it against a wind that was not there, and her eyes and lips and breath had all stopped together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:121:"Come and read this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:125:It was a register entry, near the foot of a left-hand page, in the crosshatched hand, small and level. The traveller's name at the head of it had faded into a brown smudge that might once have been two words or three, and whence and whither were there, but he did not read them. The line under them was longer than the lines around it, because the assessor had said something and the clerk had written all of it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:129:He read it once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:131:He read it again, and on the second reading his body understood it before he did. *Multiple signature families.* He knew what a signature family was, because he had spent a term sitting under a chart of them in a lecture room, with the families laid out in boxes and joined by lines, and his own word on none of them. *No single designation applicable.* Somebody had stood in front of an assessor, in this house, and shown things that crossed the lines between the boxes. Perhaps it had been at the gate in the wall he could feel at his back, or in the hall that was now an archive. And the assessor had not reached for a flag, or written *removal* or *voidance* or anything of the kind. He had written that he could not decide, and had given it a word for the while, *pending further study*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:143:*SHATTERED*, in the same capitals, on the same page, set down by a pen that had come along after the crosshatched clerk, and read what the clerk wrote, and written over it what it was to be called from now on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:165:She did not say anything at first. She bent close, and read his line, and read the register's, and read his again. Then she did what she did with everything: she checked it. She checked the spelling, letter by letter, with the tip of the paper knife held just off the paper. She checked the capitals, his and the clerk's. She checked the form of the word, whether it stood alone or had anything joined to it. She looked at the word *terminology* in his line and the word *custom* in the clerk's, and he could see her deciding that they were not the same word and did not have to be. He watched her run out of things to check, one after another, and come to the end of them, and keep looking anyway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:177:"Two sources," she said. "That never touched. Yours came out of a fighting ledger in a city I've only seen from a coach. Mine's been sitting on this shelf since before the school existed, and nobody's read it in Prynn's lifetime, she told me so. Neither one can have been copied from the other. Neither one can have been written by somebody who'd read the other." She stopped. "And they say the same word. The same word, in the same capitals, and nothing on this hill or in that city that could have told either of them to say it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:199:"Now read it," said Karis. "Out loud. From the register."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:203:"All of it. Every word, every capital, where every line breaks." She had her first notebook open on her knee and her finger on the first word. "I'll follow in mine. Then you read it again and I'll follow in the second. Then I'll read the register and you follow in yours." She did not look up. "You're a second source now. Act like one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:205:So he read it aloud, low, in the cold, from the register, with the lamp between them. He read the crosshatched clerk's entry and the later hand's line, word by word, and stopped at every line's end and said *break*, and she followed in her own copy with her finger and said *yes* at every break. When he had done it once he did it again, and she followed in the second notebook, and at one place she said *stop*. She had written *signature family* where the register said *signature families*, and struck it through with a single line and wrote it again above, and said *go on*. Then she read it from the register while he followed in the observation notebook, and he found nothing wrong in his. Then she checked his anyway, letter by letter, with the paper knife.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:209:"Three copies," she said. "Two in mine, one in yours. Each read against the register out loud by somebody who wasn't the one who wrote it." She closed the second notebook and laid her palm on its cover. "If anybody ever asks whether this page existed, there's that much."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:211:She wrote a card for the volume, and put a dot in its corner, the mark for read through. Then she closed the register itself, gently, with the paper knife's flat under the cover so that the old leaves would not crease. She carried it back to the shelf it had come from and set it in its place in the run, square with its neighbours, its spine exactly in line with theirs, as if putting it back right might matter to whatever came next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:249:"That's correct." She looked at the notebooks. "I hate it. I want you to know that I hate it, so it's on the record somewhere. It's correct, and I hate it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:251:"It's on the record," said Cael. "Mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:267:He wrote it at the scarred table before they went up, with Karis watching and not reading, because she had asked once in the second week never to read his pages over his shoulder and had kept that as she kept everything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-51.md:271:*Two hands that never met. One in the margin of Vell's oldest ledger in Ardenmere. One in a road-house register on this hill, the third clerk, with a later hand over it. The same word. K. made two copies, I made one. Read aloud, all three, against the page.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:15:"Because of your record. What there is of it." She did not sit. "For eight months I've been putting your circuit record back together out of other people's stories. Men who stood at a rail once. My mother's cousin, who trades wool through Ardenmere. A girl who saw one bout and told it to three people three different ways. They agree on the outline, and on almost nothing else." She had told herself this often enough that the drama had been rubbed off it, like the shine off a coin carried in a pocket. "I'd like to study you. Formally, as research. You'd set the terms. Nothing happens without your permission. Every line I write, you read before another soul does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:39:"Dull." She answered at once, and from the speed of it he guessed she had built the day long before she came through the door. "Sessions when you choose to have them, and never otherwise. I watch and I write. I don't touch you, and I don't test you. I never ask you to show anything you haven't already decided to show. Afterwards I might ask questions, and you can refuse one, or all, and owe me no reason. You read what I wrote before anybody else does, and you keep a copy of every page. Then if a page ever turns up somewhere you didn't send it, the proof is sitting in your own room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:65:He had been looked at for as long as he could remember. An Arbiter had looked at him at fourteen and handed down a word in brackets. A clerk had looked at the word, said she was sorry, and stamped it. A man in a market square had looked at him through a long list of careful questions, and an official whose name Cael knew had gone on looking afterwards, in reports Cael would never be allowed to read. Even the people who loved him had looked hard, Hesk across a workbench and Lira across a sparring circle, close enough to see what didn't fit before he had any words for it; and yet not one of them had stopped at the edge of the looking and asked whether he minded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:81:The sensible answer was no, or not yet, and he knew where that answer came from. People who wished to study him had seldom wanted only what they said they wanted, and between the purpose a person stated and the use the work was put to later there lay a stretch of ground where he had always been hurt. She would write; she had said so without a flicker. But paper travelled. Three weeks ago a brown envelope with his name in it had gone down this hill, and a grey card had come back up, and somewhere between the two a person he would never meet had read a line about him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:89:Trustworthy was a separate question, and an open one. What it did make her was new. Nobody who had come wanting to know what he was had ever gone about it like this, and when a thing was that new, he liked to look at it from every side before he touched it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:105:The Current girl moved through them as she was told to, one drill after another. Karis placed a point where the girl's left foot was about to land and let it go a breath before the foot came down. She placed three in a row along the line the girl would take to reach her and let them go one by one, a breath apart, so that the girl ran down a little corridor of fading warmth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:127:It wasn't a performance; the next two settled that. No one keeps that kind of evenness up for four days in a row just to impress a boy standing at the edge of the chalk. On the fourth afternoon she did not look at him at all, and he understood that she had simply stopped needing to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:135:He worked through the framework against the empty air and through Lira's circuits and the slow shoulder work, and for most of the hour he did not mention it. He had not decided whether to. Then, near the end, while he was stretching the right shoulder against the wall and Quenna was signing the record, he heard himself say it anyway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:143:"I read her transfer papers. I read every transfer's papers." She handed him the strip. "Ternhall sent three pages after her, asking us to reconsider admitting her on the grounds that she was making a decision she would regret. She sent one page of her own, asking us to disregard theirs." The corner of Quenna's mouth moved very slightly. "Hers was better written."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:155:"Most people who want to write about you will never show you a page of what they write," she said. "If she means to, that's worth something. If she doesn't mean to, you'll find out quickly, and that's worth something too." She tucked the slate under her arm. "If you decide to agree, bring me the terms before the first session, not after. Whatever you sign, I'd like to read it while it can still be changed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:181:"I know a bit about being written down." Her voice dropped. "Fenmark kept a file on me. It's sitting in a box at the intake desk right now, and I've never once opened it. I know who wrote it, though. And I know that not one of them ever asked me a single thing first. They watched me for a year and wrote down what they'd decided they saw, and then a clerk stood in a room and read it to me, and that was who I was." She let out a breath. "So somebody says *I'd like to study you*, and up go my shoulders before I can stop them. That isn't me arguing with her. It's a scar, and I know a scar from an argument. I'm pointing at it so you know it's there. Later on, when my face does something about her, you'll know where it's coming from."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:189:"Sit with it," she said, and knocked her shoulder against his, which was as close as Lira ever came to a blessing. "You're good at that. Keeping hold of a thing till you find your own opinion of it, instead of the one you've been handed. Go and be good at it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:215:He read it over. Verdicts came down on people; they arrived sealed, and all you could do about one was outlast it. A question was different, because you could walk around inside a question, and you could answer it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:7:Cael had run the framework, four passes at panel speed, with Wray breaking the count on the third as she always broke it. He had given the read, four calls at two paces, the last with his eyes shut. Then Quenna had asked whether he elected to show anything further, and he had said no. Whatever he owed the record was in it already, at its own date, in the dullest words Quenna owned. Everything else he banked, as he had banked it since the first morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:11:"As previously recorded," she said, for the record. "No safety concerns."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:27:*Wind-adjacent. Lira. The framework. Still the first thing my feet do when anything comes at me. In the second month Karis asked whether it felt borrowed, and I told her I didn't know. For this one I know now. It isn't borrowed. Lira and I made it between us, in a yard behind a grain exchange, and it lives in me. She's read this page. She agrees, and says Wray is right about the second figure.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:31:*Iron-adjacent. Brom. The surface read, from contact out to a few paces, thinner the faster they come. It read through linen and quilting and felt; it gave me Karis's points as weight before they were light. For most of a year I tried to make it be something else. It's better at being what it is.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:71:That was the whole of it. Hesk had not even put his initial at the foot. It read as if he had begun to write before he sat down, and stood up again the moment it was finished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:73:Cael read it three times. The second time through, something happened behind his eyes that he did not let go any further, and Lira, beside him on the wall, looked out at the town with great attention and did not see it. He did not write that in the binder afterward. There were things the binder was for, and there were things he was allowed simply to keep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:77:He gave her the letter, and she read it, and gave it back without a word, and left her hand on his arm a moment longer than she needed to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:91:It took him until the lamp burned low, because Vell would read it as she read her ledger, every word, in order, and he crossed out more than he kept. What he kept was short.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:97:*There was a hearing. A man from the Compact came up the hill with correct papers and asked a magistrate to take me off the rolls here. He was right about everything he said. I let him be right, all the first day, and on the second day I stood up and read their own schedule to them, from the first page to the last, out loud, because my word isn't in it. It never has been. They ruled that they couldn't show they had the right. The ruling is below, word for word. You'll want the words.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:99:*I read the code the way you taught me to read a ledger. Every line, in order, and none of them softer than what happened.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:119:The carrier set each box down at her knees. She looked at the slip under its string, which was her own; then she broke the wax and cut the string and counted, and read the marks against her list, and ticked them. Then she carried the volumes down the long room to their cases herself, in her arms, as many as she could hold, and shelved them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:129:It was no longer than it takes to breathe in and let the breath out again. Then they went on. She took the volumes out one at a time and laid each across her knee and read its spine against her list and ticked it. When the box was empty she sat with her pencil over the list. She read down the column for that box, and then reached for the delegation's return at her elbow and read down that to the line for box seven, where it said in the officer's neat hand *nine*. Then she read her own list again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:137:It was the width of one volume, among the crosshatched sevens, in the place where a register had stood with a faded brown smudge on one of its left-hand pages. She did not put anything into it. She did not slide the books on either side along to close it, which would have taken her one breath and left the shelf looking whole for good. She stood in front of it for a moment with her hands at her sides. Then she came back up the long room to the high desk and opened the marbled restoration ledger, in which she had written one line for every box. She wrote this box's number and its case and the date, as she had written the others, and closed the ledger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:141:He did not say anything about it either. He thought of a notebook buttoned inside a coat on the far side of a door in the residence wing. He thought of the box drawn round two lines near the front of his own notebook: one old assessor's word for somebody like him, once in his own younger hand out of Vell's ledger, and once copied from the very register that had not come back. And he thought of the register itself, wherever it was now, in somebody else's keeping, read by somebody who had wanted it enough to take it. There would come a time to talk about that, and it would take a great deal of talking. It was not now. Prynn knew it was not now as well as he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:163:"Next term," said Edran, without any greeting. "Same terms. Full sanction, faculty supervised, on the record."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:175:"I'm glad it stood up," he said. "Your reading. I put my name to that petition because I was sure what the rules said, and you were standing outside them." He let the structure go again. "Then I sat in the gallery and heard somebody read the rules out from the first page, and found out I'd never once gone and looked. Everybody had only said." He built the third again, whole. "I'll look, next time. Before I file anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:189:She looked at him a moment longer, and something she kept very straight for the record went a little less straight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:199:"When you were reading the schedule," she said, "somewhere near the end of the first hour, I watched the recorder. He stopped between two entries and took his inkwell off the desk and held it against his chest in his fist, to warm it. Recorders do that in winter. I've sat at hearings for twenty years, and I've never once seen a recorder fail to notice he was cold until the ink told him." She turned toward the old building. "Nobody in that room knew how long they'd been sitting there. I've never seen better done on a hearing floor, and I've seen a great deal done on them. So, yes. Worth it." She took one hand out of her pocket and touched his sleeve, briefly, with two fingers. "Go and find the others. Close out your term."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:203:They found the wall at dusk, the four of them, as they had found walls in more towns than he had kept count of: the low one by the residence wing door where Lira sat on evenings when there was nothing to do. They sat on it in a row with their coats pulled round them and looked east off the hill toward the country they had walked in from in the autumn, and past it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:219:"I have five things that aren't a Path," said Cael, after a while, to none of them in particular. He had not planned to say it. It had simply become true enough, sitting there, to be said aloud. "An argument on the public record. And three people who know all of it." Then he turned it over once more, the way he turned a sentence before he let it stand in the binder. "This might actually be enough."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:229:Under them the hill was cold and old and took their weight, as it had taken the weight of everybody who had ever stopped on it on the way to somewhere else. Far down the long roads, in rooms none of them would ever stand in, a record with his name across its first page was already being copied and read, and would go on being read, and could not be made small again. And at the foot of the hill the road they had come in by ran on east into the dark, pale under the first stars, with nobody on it yet. It would wait for them. It had waited before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:7:It was all there, in the order it had grown. The provision had been revised three times, and each revision sat in its fascicle with its date and its reasons attached in the flat language of the drafting clerks. He read the newest first, out of habit, and then made himself go back and read them in order, as Prynn had told him to. When he reached the bottom of the oldest box he found the first draft, written in a hand so old that the letters leaned against each other like tired men.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:15:He walked the road for eleven hours. He traced every revision and every enrollment the digests recorded under it, every late Kindler and disputed child and clerical error the provision had ever sheltered. He was looking for one single case, anywhere, of somebody already Kindled and classified who had been carried by it and allowed to stay. There was none, and the road went nowhere. Read it as kindly as you liked, it was written for the untested. He had been tested. Nothing in its history could change what its words said, or what its first drafter had meant them to say. Coss had walked this same road weeks ago, he was certain of it. Coss had found the box in the basement, or wherever the regional office kept its copy, and had read the leaning hand, and had walked all the way to the end. Coss would be standing there waiting for him, in the assembly room, with the first draft in his document case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:29:"I've known for forty years. I've certified forty copies of it, and read every one of them aloud." She looked at the crossed-out pages. "I'd have told you if you'd asked. You didn't ask. You wanted to walk it." She went back to her stool. "Most people have to. It's a good road. It's only the wrong one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:43:It was in the first article after the preamble, the definitions clause, and he read past it twice. He had been reading for something clever and hidden, and this was neither; it was so plain that his eyes slid off it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:47:He read it a third time, aloud and very quietly, because Prynn had taught him that ears were more honest than eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:49:*Shall mean.* Not *shall include*. Not *shall be taken broadly as*, or any of the soft wide words the middle centuries had loved. The charter was not describing classification but defining it, shutting it, and leaving the schedule as the one way in. No category could reach the Compact's authority except through the schedule; the Compact's authority could reach nobody the schedule did not name. *For all purposes.* Every *over classified practitioners* he had read in two nights by lamplight ran back through this one sentence to the list. There was nowhere else for it to run.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:57:He had spent two years reading things that did not want to be read, and he read this harder than any of them. Every Path family. Every tier. All the provisional categories, too, that the Compact had tried on before it settled: Dual-Registered, Contested-Standing, Interim-Assessed, and half a dozen more that came and went as the Compact's idea of a Path kept changing under it, slowly and clumsily. He took the amendments in their order, as Prynn had taught him, and watched categories arrive in one generation's confidence and get struck out in another's embarrassment. The struck ones stayed on the page, ruled through but readable, because the code kept its mistakes as carefully as its rules. Everything it had ever recognized had a name, and the name was written in the schedule, in the flat clerk's language that turns a real thing into a lawful one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:65:On the sixth night he read the whole schedule a third time, by lamplight, from the first page to the last, with Prynn at the high desk silently refilling his cup at an hour when no duty on earth required her to still be in the building. The third reading said what the second had. Nowhere in the schedule, in all its generations of piled-up text, did the word appear. Not once. No footnote, no entry struck out and kept in the record of its own repeal, and not as something once proposed and voted down. The schedule kept its refusals as well. He had come across three: categories somebody had argued for and lost, each refusal written up with the same clerical care as everything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:83:She did not pour, but put the pot down on the far corner of the table, well away from the bindings, and read the page upside down while he watched her read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:91:"Don't tell me," said Prynn. "Let me read what you wrote. If it needs you standing next to it to make sense, it won't stand up in that room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:93:So he said nothing, and she read it to him off the page, slowly, in the voice she used to certify a copy, with one finger moving down his lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:99:"Sixty years I've kept these shelves," said Prynn. "You're the first person to read the code the way it's written instead of the way it's enforced."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:109:"Because nobody's ever asked me," he said. "Not about anything. A station in Denvash decided what I was. A courier came with a notice. A man in a borrowed office in Ardenmere, and somebody above him I've never seen, signing things. Every word anybody's ever said about what I am was said over my head." He looked at the circled word. "If I hire somebody to stand up and say this, then even the argument that nobody may speak for me is made by somebody else. I've read it all now. So I get to say it myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:141:"Anybody like you." Karis picked up the bread. "Not yet. I've read four hundred entries and every one of them is somebody the old words could hold. Ash and Stone and Current. People nobody had any trouble naming." She tore the bread in half. "That isn't a finding. It's the shape of a shelf I haven't finished. There are a great many shelves."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:155:"Wray's building something," he said, when Cael came to the door. "This afternoon. The sixth bell, on the defensive floor." He looked at the floor of the passage. "I'd like you to watch it. You read my wall before anybody but her did. I'd like somebody at the rail who can see where it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:167:"It isn't a queue," said Wray. "That's the difference. Last time each of them came at you, and you turned it, and then the next one came. Today the next one starts before you've finished with the last. Hobb comes first, with his weight, from the front. You take it and you redirect it, and your redirect has to go somewhere. It goes into Edran's line, because Edran's already coming from the west by the time you're turning Hobb, and he's bursting a shell on your guard as he arrives. Then Karis's channel is under your feet where you have to step, because you'll have to step to turn Edran, and it'll shove up at your shins as you do. And Lira finishes it, from whatever side you've left open." She looked at the four of them and then at him. "No one of them is faster than you can read. All four of them together arrive faster than you can turn. That's what Silver is, from the inside. Too much arriving, too close together, from too many places to finish one before the next."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:185:The third round was where Cael began to see it, and he saw it because he had been watching Brom's wait since the night of the null sessions. It was the small held instant between the read and the turn, the place Wray had shown with her own hand on the seventh strike. At the long intervals it did not matter, because the wait finished before anything else arrived. Now there was no room for it. Hobb's weight came, and Brom read it, and waited his instant, and turned it. In the instant while he waited, Edran was already a pace nearer than he had been the time before, and so the turn into Edran's line came a shade too late, and the burst came a shade too close, and the step onto Karis's channel came a shade too soon. Every lateness piled on the last, until by the time Lira came in, the whole of Brom's body was still busy finishing something else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:197:The others had seen it too, and they were learning. Cael watched them learn, because watching people learn was the oldest thing he knew how to do. On the fourth round Karis laid her channel half a step further along the line Brom would have to take, so that its shove came up at the very moment Hobb's weight was still on his guard, two forces from two directions at once. Edran built his shell and burst it a beat early, before Brom's redirect had finished arriving, so that the burst met the turn halfway. And Lira did not wait for Brom to finish anything at all. She had found his instant on the third round, and on the fourth she came in at the start of his wait instead of at its end, so that her palm arrived in the exact space he left open while he held what he had read and before he sent it anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:209:"No," said Wray. "Not closed. I can make you see it at the speed it matters. You can see it now better than anybody I've taught in twenty years. I can't make you fast enough to close it." She did not soften the next part, because she never softened anything, and Cael understood that this was its own kind of respect. "Nobody on this hill can. I can build you an imitation of Silver for four rounds out of four students who'd rather be eating their supper. To close it you'd need somebody who's Silver all day long, every day, for a year. That isn't here." She tucked the slate under her arm. "I'll keep looking. I said I would. I don't say that twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:71:Brom had written it down the way he wrote down a fetch drill, every count in its place. He gave where each of them had stood when Vell called it. He wrote that Cael had come in with no speed left, because the second exchange had taken his legs, and with nothing left to throw, and how he had fought small, short angles, weight kept over his feet. He wrote what his own read had been doing: tracking, as it always tracked, the pressure of a body in front of him, the way a hand in the dark knows where a wall is. And then he wrote the place where the read had stopped tracking. *For less than a second there was nobody in front of me. I don't mean he was quick. I mean my read went to where he was and found the floor.* He wrote that he had not known what to do, and that he had done nothing, which was wrong, and that he had won the exchange anyway on feel, and that it had taken him three times as long as either of the first two. His own errors were in it too, set down with no more and no less care than anything else. He wrote the fourth exchange in a single line: *Then I put him down, because his legs were gone and I knew it, and that's how I won the bout.* Under that, in the square capitals again, he had copied Vell's line from the ledger as she said it over them on the floor: *ATYPICAL MOVEMENT, THIRD EXCHANGE.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:117:He read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:121:"That's the one that's true." She watched him read it again. "I came over that hill to study something nobody could explain. I had a stack of notebooks full of wrong explanations and a column of things I'd killed, and I wanted to stand next to the thing when it finally made sense." She put her hand flat on the stack. "And somewhere in the last three weeks I noticed what I'd been writing down. That he reads every letter from Denvash twice before he answers it. That he gives back the larger half of the bread when he thinks nobody's counting. That a man asked him on the record whether he knew the form was wrong, and he said *No* and then nothing, and walked out of the room lighter. That a boy who beat him once wrote down the one exchange he couldn't beat." She glanced at Cael's coat, at the place over his chest, and he understood that Brom had told her, or that she had seen Brom go to the rail with his hand on his coat. "A phenomenon doesn't do any of those things. A person does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:125:"I've done nothing of the kind." She said it plainly. "I've changed what I'm studying. That's not the same as stopping. It's the opposite of stopping, if anything." She took the volume back, gently, and closed it, and laid it on top of the stack. "I wrote it twice." She took a loose sheet from inside the back cover and laid it on the table in front of him. "One page is in that book. This one is yours, because clause six says you read it first and keep it. Put it with the others. I'd rather not know where that is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:163:"You said it on this wall," said Lira. "The night I'd read the four paragraphs and decided they didn't get to tell me who I was. You'd had it off Vell, and you turned it round and gave it to me. I wrote it down that night so I'd have it the way we'd made it, not the way I'd have made it alone." She looked at the slip in his hand. "I've had it behind Fenmark ever since. Every time I took the four paragraphs out to read them again, which I did more than I'd ever tell you, that came out first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:167:"I don't need it tomorrow. You do." She put the envelope back in her coat and buttoned the coat over it. "Tomorrow the Compact writes a record with one of us standing up in the middle of it, talking. Not a line about us, written in a room we weren't in. One of us, talking. So you keep that in your pocket. And you make the record say something worth keeping."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:179:They did not fight, exactly. There was nobody to throw anything and nobody to count, and the frozen ruts were uneven under his boots where the broom had not reached. They went through the oldest patterns there were, the first-year forms, his evasion framework against her Wind at the speed of somebody walking home. Then they went a little faster, and then, without either of them saying so, at the speed they had used on the circuit, when there had been a ledger and a crowd and a purse. His feet found the ruts and stopped minding them. On the fourth turn the pivot that had been a hair late all week came exactly on time, and he felt it come, and she saw him feel it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:185:"Tomorrow they'll spend the whole morning telling you what you are," she said, between one exchange and the next. Her breath was going up white and quick. "In their words. Coss will. He'll stand there for an hour and read you out of his case, and every word will be so, and none of it will be you." She turned out of his reach. "Let him. When it's your turn, don't start from his words. Don't start from theirs at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:217:*I thought I'd be angry, by now. I'm not. Everything I've read for three weeks says the same thing underneath. The flag, the courier who walked me out of Denvash, the file that followed me here: not one of them ever had a line of the code under it that held me. They did all of it anyway. That's the part I'd have wanted to hear at fourteen. Tomorrow I'll say it to the Compact instead.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:219:He read it over and did not cross anything out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:227:Down the hill the town's lamps were going out one by one, as they did every night. The last one burning was in an upper window at the back of the draper's shop, a small square of steady yellow, where a man read every case from the first page and would be reading this one now, again, from the beginning. Cael watched it. He did not hate it. He could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:7:Edran stood in the middle of the ring and breathed. He was not breathing as hard as Cael was, because Glass did not tire the lungs as Wind did; it tired something further in. But his hands had a fine tremor in them at his sides, and his face, which had shown nothing all morning, showed something now that was not anger and was not quite anything Cael had a page for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:11:"For the record," said Edran.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:27:It was not friendship, and Cael, still blowing hard in the cold, knew that very clearly. It was a narrower thing. In its own way it was worth as much. A student of full standing, Iron Rank Two, first on the standings for most of three years, had stood across a chalk ring from him in front of the whole school. Then he had said aloud, for the record, that the thing the observer category had no word for was something real. Nobody had told him to. He had filed the question himself, and now he had answered it himself, in the same daylight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:33:"The record," said Quenna.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:41:"Evasion framework under live opposition. Consistent with prior record. No safety concerns."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:43:Cael counted the words without meaning to, as he had at the second sitting. Twelve, more than last month, and he understood that the new words were *under live opposition*: Wray had written down, in the plainest language her dialect allowed, that this time somebody had been trying to hit him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:103:"You chose a small fight." It was the first thing she had ever said to him that was neither an instruction nor a record. "You kept it the size you chose all the way through, with two hundred people and that boy asking you to make it bigger. That's harder than winning. Edran will be on my floor tomorrow morning at the first bell, working on the third structure. You might come and watch. He won't mind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:113:He did not get to the kitchen for nearly half an hour, because the yard would not let him go. People did not crowd him, because Greyvane was not that sort of place. But they came up one or two at a time while he stood at the rail with Lira and Brom, said things, and went away again, and every time he thought it had finished someone else arrived. The Storm Path fourth-year who had asked whether he meant to fight close or at range came up, said, "Close, then," nodded, and left. A Copper first-year from the movement floor asked whether the cut hurt. Two girls from the hardening family, who had wished him good morning on the stair in the first week for entirely the wrong reasons, said good morning again now, and he thought they might have meant it this time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:121:"Three to one." The boy held up the crusts like a trophy. "Nobody on the Edge floor will speak to me for a week. It's wonderful." He looked at Cael with something close to reverence. "You never told me a thing. Not one word. That's how I knew."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:155:"I know. I heard you, in the third row."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:173:"Because tonight Quenna will copy the record of this morning into the ledger in her best hand. And some day, in some office I'll never visit, somebody will read it. The third audience." He turned his long weathered face from the window and looked at Cael. "They'll read that a candidate under the provision was assessed in public against a ranked student of full standing. That he showed the same evasion framework already recorded at two sittings, and nothing else. That he took one touch and made two, and the petitioner withdrew." He paused. "It's the dullest paragraph I've ever wanted to read. I've never in my life been so glad of a dull paragraph."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:189:The withdrawal went up on the board in the main hall that afternoon, before the fifth bell, as the rule said it must. It was a single sheet in Edran's own hand, the same clear hand that did not shake, pinned with one brass tack beside the three pages of the petition and Quenna's answer. Cael went to read it at the change of bells, when the hall was busiest, because he had decided on the way down from the kitchen that he would not wait for it to be quiet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:195:Cael read it through. He noticed the word *framework*, and he noticed that Edran had written *a form he accepts* and not *a form that satisfies him*, and he thought he understood the difference. Edran was not satisfied, and was not sure he ever would be, but he had asked whether the standings could still tell, and they had told, and he had put his name to that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:213:"That's why it worked." She held up a third finger and then put it down again. "Third, there isn't a third. It's gone. Nobody's saying you're a Compact spy anymore, and nobody's saying you read poetry to the panel." She sounded faintly mournful. "The poetry one was my favourite."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:225:*Third sitting, public, the formal yard. Assessment satisfied. Wray: evasion framework under live opposition, consistent with prior record, no safety concerns. Function shown: the Wind-adjacent framework and nothing else. The Compression fragment woke for a shell on my guard in the first exchange. Gave it no road. Third time I've chosen that, counting Brom's floor. It's getting quieter to do.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:229:*Edran withdrew. He did it in the ring, in front of everyone, in words of his own, and they're in the record now. Whatever you are, it's real. Not friendship. A witness. I don't know yet what that's worth.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:233:*He believes the board ought to be able to tell. He would rather lose to it than win around it. When he found a gap in himself, in front of everybody, he asked for another exchange so he could close it while it still cost him. He narrowed it by half in the time it takes to walk to a mark.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:245:She did not need to. He had said so on the wall, and she had agreed, and they both knew the exhibition was not a session and the clauses did not reach it. She brought it anyway. She set four sheets beside his elbow at Prynn's long table, written out fair in her small upright hand with a fifth folded inside them that was a copy, and sat down across from him with her own notebook and did not watch him read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:247:He read it as he had read her first pages, slowly, with his hands slow on the paper. It was as dry as the others, with no adjectives in it anywhere. It gave the four exchanges in order, with timings in the margin that she must have counted in her head, because he had not seen her look at the clock. It noted the shell that burst on his guard in the first exchange, and the line beside it said only *subject's breathing held two counts after, released*, and nothing about why. She had seen something happen in his chest and had written down exactly what she could see from outside, which was a held breath, and not one word more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:249:The second exchange took most of a page. He read the line about the graze, and then the line under it, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:251:*At contact: subject's eyes on petitioner's right forearm, bare, in the interval between second and third structures; not on the strike. Pen stopped.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-20.md:271:"Quenna said I'd asked her something." She set her hands flat on the closed notebook. "I don't want you to wonder what. I asked her a week ago whether the sessions could ever include anything more than watching. Any live condition at all, inside the rules." She paused. "She said not under the provision as it's written. Partnered practice isn't authorized. You have a refusal in your binder that says so, with her initials on it. I asked her to keep the question anyway, for whenever it might be answerable." She looked at him levelly. "That's all I asked. I should have told you before she did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:23:It was the worst hour of practice he had ever had, and it was not close.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:25:She came at him from the first breath. She did not strike, not really, but she moved as if she would, short sharp feints off the front foot that made the framework wake and read and want to slip. Every time it slipped him she said *point* and bent and drew a circle on the earth where he had been about to go. Within a few minutes there were six, and the ring that had been eight paces across was full of small chalk mouths. Every step he took had to find the space between them, and every slip the framework offered him had to be checked against the floor before he could take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:47:Karis timed him on the Thursday in section four, as she had timed him since the first session, and when she read back his recovery between passes she frowned at her own page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:53:"Then something else is making you tired." She held her pen above the page, the sixth clause sitting between them in the cold air of the section: he read everything she wrote about him before it existed anywhere else. "Cause?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:59:Karis's pen did not move for a moment. Then it did, and she turned the notebook round so that he could read what she had written, as the clause said she must.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:61:*Recovery slower by one count. Cause stated by subject: Lira.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:67:They did the post work after that, with the felt on and Brom leaning on the far side, and the read gave him four in twenty, his best yet, which Karis wrote down and read back in his own words. When the hour was done she folded her stool and stood with it under her arm, and did a thing she had not done since the terms: she hesitated.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:73:"No. The clause makes you read it. I keep coming because I said I would, and I keep writing because it's true. It would be stranger to stop." She looked at him. "And you're reading every line I write about your recovery. That's the most even thing about this whole arrangement. I know exactly how tired you are, and you know exactly what I know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:85:The new idea turned out to be Hobb, and a Storm second-year Cael did not know, and Edran, one after another. Wray stood at the edge of the chalk with her slate and called them in at Brom in turn, each faster than the last, Hobb slow and heavy, the Storm boy quicker, and Edran the quickest of all with his ready pair built at his mark. Brom took every strike and gave and waited and turned. Cael watched the wait. He could see it now every time, the small held instant between the read and the turn that Wray had shown him on the seventh strike, and Edran came closest to it, and Edran never got in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:115:"You're watching me," she said, before the first point. "I saw it all last night. You watch my feet and you watch my hands and you trust Brom to throw where you can see it. That's wrong." She drew the first circle. "She isn't going to be in one place. Her floor's going to be everywhere. So you're going to watch the floor, and let me be something you read out of the side of your eye."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:135:Six touched him on the third night. He stood at the end of it with his hands on his knees in the middle of a ring that had nineteen circles in it, having done what he had not believed possible on legs that had stopped being his own somewhere around the fortieth minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:151:"I know. I've read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:227:He did it twice more, because one was luck and two was the floor, as Wray had told Brom. The second was the same, one breath and the strap. The third he woke too late, so that the strike landed before he had the road open and the fragment found nothing to close on, and he took it the ordinary way, and nothing went into a joint.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:237:*Compression-adjacent. Brom's room, L. present, told before. The drop, fast: a quarter of a quick palm strike caught and let go, unsent, with the fragment woken before the strike. Two of three. Cost: one breath, a tightness across the breastbone. The cost lives in the sending, not the catching. It takes the shove. It does not take heat. Nothing I have takes heat. If I ever used it to cross something hot, the burn would be a thing I chose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:239:He read it back, and Lira read it upside down from the bed, and neither of them said anything more about it that night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:245:He had thought it would go on falling. Eleven, nine, six: the arithmetic seemed to promise a night when no bag touched him at all. Lira's shoulder was rested and she was fast again, and Brom threw as he always threw, and Cael watched the floor and read Lira out of the side of his eye and picked and picked and picked, faster than the night before, and four bags touched him. The next session, after a rest, it was four again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:247:He knew why before Lira said it, because he could feel it in his own legs in the instant before each slip. The framework read the threat and offered him a way out of it, as it always had. Then, in the half-beat while he was still taking it, something in him checked the floor and the floor said *not that way*, and he stopped and began a second way, and the second way was always just too late, and a bag that would have missed him found him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:259:"With Vell it was always one opponent. One rhythm, one body, one strike to read. You got very good at it." Lira leaned back on her hands. "This isn't one rhythm. It's twenty, all coming due at once, and she's the only one who knows the order. You can't read twenty rhythms the way you read one. You've got to stop asking whether it's right and just go, and trust the floor to have been read already."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-04.md:87:The crowd let its breath out all together, which was not a cheer but was close to one. Wray did not make a sound at all. She wrote on her slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-04.md:93:"Somebody taught you to live through being underestimated. Maybe you taught yourself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-04.md:163:She had spent the afternoon in the hill town between her own scheduled blocks. She reported on it quickly and without wasted words: it was even smaller up close than it looked from the hill, with one street worth walking and one worth avoiding, a mill, a smithy, a post office in the back of a draper's, and a baker at the bottom of the hill who opened before dawn and was rude to everyone equally, which Lira considered a mark of integrity.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-04.md:173:"Re-certification. I saw the roster for the review room. My name, and three others. Only a handful. But a handful means somebody did this before me and somebody will do it after me, and there's a desk and a procedure and a clerk who knows how to read the clauses aloud." She was looking at the cup. "It's a *track*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:131:She nodded, once, as she did when a sum came out. Then she turned the slate over in her arms and looked at it and did not write anything on it, which he had never seen her do before. She was holding a small card in the same hand as the slate, pressed against its frame, just as he had once held a card of his own that said nothing anybody could read, and she did not show it to him and he did not look at it. It was hers. It was the first thing she had ever owned that nobody else would ever be allowed to read, and she held it as if she already knew that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:169:Lira looked at her from the wall, with an expression Cael had seen on her face once before, on the morning she had read four paragraphs from Fenmark and decided they did not get to say who she was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:173:Oona looked at her with something close to alarm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:181:On the Tuesday of the eighteenth week Karis asked if she might try two things, and asked it at breakfast, formally, before the porridge, so that everybody at the scarred table could refuse it in front of witnesses if they wanted to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:187:The first thing she asked was whether he could light two at once. She did not mean two in a row with the count between; she meant two together, his palm and his fingertips, one ring under the heel of his hand and another under the pads of his fingers, at the same instant. He chose both sizes, and held both in his mind, and turned inward, and asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:207:Karis bent close and looked at the shadow on the oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:231:That night the line went into the long index with all the others and was run against every standing file, as every line always was, and it matched nothing. But it was copied, the next morning, onto the short daily sheet that went to the officer who had asked to see anything the registry received about that academy, because he had asked, a fortnight before, for exactly that. It reached Coss at his borrowed desk on the second floor at a little after the third bell, between a bound volume of enforcement digests and a sheaf of drafting records tied with faded tape, and he read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:235:The briefing had taken one afternoon. Everything after it had taken the season, and he had known that it would, and had not minded. A challenge to a chartered academy's enrollment was not a request for papers. It went before a magistrate in public and into the permanent registry, and every line of it would be read aloud by people who wanted it to be wrong. So every line had to stand on something that could be produced in a room and handed across a table. He had spent the season finding those somethings, one at a time, from offices that kept their papers in the order that suited them and not in the order that suited him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:239:The drafting records had been in three places, none of them where the index said. He had found them at last in a basement room under the regional chamber, in a box marked for a different provision entirely, and he had read them in an afternoon by a window that let in more cold than light. The demonstration provision had been written long ago, and it had been written as a kindness. The guilds in those days had sent their families travelling, carters and drovers and pedlars with a guild licence, and their children came to fourteen on the road, a month from any station, and arrived at the gates of the academies untested and unplaced. A chartered academy could not take an untested child. The provision let it, under watching, until the child could be brought to a station and found out. That was all it was for. It had been amended three times since, and every amendment had widened who it covered by a little and narrowed what it was for by not at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:243:Then he had found the precedents, which had been easier, because the enforcement digests were indexed by people who expected to be asked. Three times in other districts, across the long life of the provision, somebody had tried to stretch it over a person it had not been written for. Once it had been a youth past the age of Kindling who had refused to be tested. Once it had been a disputed assessment the family would not accept. Once it had been a clerk's honest mistake, never caught until it was challenged. Three academies, three enrollments, three challenges. Each enrollment had been voided. None had survived the hearing, and one had not survived to a hearing at all. He had read each digest twice and copied the citations onto a clean sheet in his small plain hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:247:*Candidate formerly enrolled under demonstration provision.* A child, then, a real one, of the kind the drafters had meant. A child who had come to Greyvane untested and sat under watching and gone down to the station at fourteen and been found to be something ordinary on every chart there was, and moved out of the category by the plainest procedure there was, as the provision had been built to do. Coss read the line a second time and thought that if he had been asked to invent an exhibit to show a tribunal what the provision was *for*, he could not have invented a better one. The academy had sent it to him itself, on the third-day post, because the procedure said it must.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:261:"The standard procedure is service by the regional channel. A sealed packet to the provost by courier, a copy to the magistrate's office, the date set from there." The senior officer looked at the packet on the desk, which was now as thick as a hand and tied in two directions. "It's a good packet. I've read it. It will stand up by itself. You don't need to walk it up a hill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:265:He did not try to explain the rest, because the rest did not fit on a form, and the senior officer had been in the service long enough to know that a man who said *I don't need to* in that voice was not finished. A packet sent by courier was read first by a provost alone at his desk, and then explained to its subject secondhand, by people who loved him and were frightened for him and would explain it in their own words. Secondhand bred errors. Errors bred appeals. And under that, where he did not need to look at it for long, there was a borrowed office in Ardenmere and a boy with his hands pressed white on his knees saying *Thank you*. Coss had stamped a summons closed-pending that afternoon with more force than a stamp required. He did not intend to do this one at the end of a courier's arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:267:"The subject attends a proceeding on his own status by right," he said instead, which was true and fitted on a form. "I'd prefer he hear it from the officer who drafted it, in the room, with the provost present. It saves a step later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:273:He put it in that night, in the flat language the form demanded, and it came back approved by the middle of the next morning. That was faster than the senior officer could have signed it and sent it up and had it back, and so it had not gone through the senior officer at all. It had gone to the same desk as before, somewhere above, where somebody had been waiting. The registry returned the challenge packet to him stamped for service. On its cover sheet the last field before the seal was the one he had stopped expecting to read: the short ruled bar, as neat as a line drawn under a sum, and the small type beneath it. *Field restricted. Recipient clearance insufficient.* He tied the packet up again in both directions and left it tied.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:279:An aide was assigned him the same afternoon, a young man two years out of the regional school who could carry cases and copy fair and did not yet know when to stop talking. Coss liked him well enough. He told him they would leave before dawn on the Saturday and reach the academy on the Monday, two days by the coach to the hill town and half a morning on the road up from there. The aide was to bring the bound digests and the drafting records and a great deal of clean paper. He would not need anything to read on the coach, Coss told him, because there would be the file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:285:He sealed it and put it in the evening bag. Then he went back to the borrowed desk and untied the challenge packet after all, and read it again from the first page, slowly, as if he had never seen the subject's name before.

