  ('chapter-03.md', 159, "Cael wrote that down twice, in two different places, because it seemed important twice. Every other academy he had ever read about sorted its students by tier. Tier was what institutions ranked people by, the number on a file that decided which doors a person could go through. Sorting by family meant that this place cared more about what a student could come to understand than about where he currently stood on the ladder. He thought it was Naveth's ledger again, written into a building.")
  ('chapter-04.md', 75, "It was the first full strike in a long while, and it came in hard, the whole weight of a fourth-year Stone Path behind it. It landed on Brom's right ribs before he had finished turning to meet it. The sound was a flat, heavy thud. Brom's breath went out of him in a grunt, and although his Iron Skin had come up in time — Cael saw the sheen of it along his side — Iron Skin did not make a person weightless. Brom slid a full pace back across the chalk and dropped to one knee.")
  ('chapter-05.md', 243, "He found Lira again at the notice board that afternoon, arms crossed before the roster for the training floor, reading it for longer than reading it could possibly take. He knew what she was looking at before he reached her. Her name was on the roster, and beside it, in the clerk's small hand, sat the same raised dot that was stamped on her tag, the provisional marker, which appeared beside her name and three others, Gerda's among them, and beside no one else on the whole board.")
  ('chapter-05.md', 253, '"Maybe." She uncrossed her arms and crossed them again. "I come and look at it every day, as if, if I stare at it long enough, it\'ll stop being able to sting."')
  ('chapter-06.md', 51, "She went away between the shelves, and he sat and read. The provision was short; after an afternoon of arguments in a back room he had expected something longer, and it was barely half a page. It said that a candidate without a standard classification issued by an Arbiter might be enrolled for academic exposure and supervised training, that such a candidate would be assessed monthly by demonstration before a panel of three, that the candidate must demonstrate *consistent, observable practice*, and that the panel's finding should be entered in the candidate's record. That was very nearly all of it. He read it twice, slowly, paying attention to what was not there. There was no word about best, no word about all, no word about naming anything, and no word, he noticed, about how a candidate had come by whatever he practiced. The provision asked what he could do. It did not ask where it came from.")
  ('chapter-09.md', 19, 'Brom considered the matter seriously while he ate. "Ternhall doesn\'t lose people. People leave other schools to go to Ternhall."')
  ('chapter-10.md', 45, 'Nobody had ever offered to protect him for the sake of keeping their numbers clean. He turned the idea over with the statute book under his hand, and believed it more than he would have believed kindness. Kindness could change its mind on a bad morning. A method had to announce it first.')
  ('chapter-12.md', 55, 'He could hear how she worked even with his back to her. Her pen ran in long unbroken strokes while he moved, and the instant he stopped, it stopped too, and lay quiet on the paper until he went again. She was not describing him. Anyone with eyes could have described him. She was following something that only ran while the framework ran, and when the framework halted there was nothing left for the pen to follow.')
  ('chapter-12.md', 247, "A month ago, *I don't know* had been a hole in the wall that anybody could look through. Tonight it was an answer he had given out loud to someone who wrote down only what she could see, and who had not written down a theory after it. It was a question left open on purpose, waiting for evidence. That was a different thing entirely. He was not sure yet whether it was a better thing. He suspected it might be.")
  ('chapter-13.md', 21, '*Notice of index correspondence. Subject: Cael. Event: enrollment, chartered institution. Institution: Greyvane Academy. Category of enrollment: unclassified observer, demonstration provision. Notation entered.*')
  ('chapter-13.md', 83, 'He left before dawn. Nothing in the assignment required it; it was his own habit. Setting out after sunrise had never, in all his years, made a journey any better. He had found many times that the first hours of a road were the only quiet ones it had.')
  ('chapter-13.md', 169, 'He wrote *purpose* in the margin, looked at it, and drew a line through it. A tribunal did not rule on purpose by being told about it. It would want the drafting records of the provision, and precedent, and a clear statement of what the people who wrote those words had meant them to cover. All of that would take weeks to gather from offices that were in no hurry to be gathered from.')
  ('chapter-13.md', 213, '"Then it comes down to whose patience gives out first, and I won\'t pretend I know. The Compact as a whole can wait longer than any one office in it. But offices are people. People get posted somewhere else. They get bored. They retire. They get promoted to a desk where the file stops mattering to them." He touched the request with one finger. "Suppose a person is carrying this at their end, and not just a procedure. I\'d count that good news. Procedures don\'t tire. People do."')
  ('chapter-14.md', 85, 'He read it. It was exact and dry and held no theory, only measurements: how big his letters had been the day of the table, the day of the first session, and every day since the request. Each of the last three was a little smaller than the one before. At the foot she had written *Cause unknown. Probable.* He laughed out loud, which surprised him, and slid the notebook back.')
  ('chapter-14.md', 183, 'Then she took a pencil from behind her ear, wrote something small on the cover of the folded copy, and handed the whole thing to him to carry back to Naveth without telling him what it said. He looked when he was halfway across the yard, because he had never once in his life carried a piece of paper about himself without reading it. It said, in pencil, *Correct. Do not hurry me again.*')
  ('chapter-17.md', 81, '"I can\'t dismiss it. It\'s clean. Every clause is in the right place and every date is correct, and the young man who wrote it has three years of standing behind him and the right to file. If I set it aside because it\'s inconvenient, I tell every student in this building that a petition is a thing the provost answers when he likes the question." He paused. "A good many of the students in this building came here because some other place answered their questions that way. A petition that gets posted and then answered is the only protection some of them have ever had. I won\'t spend it on you. You wouldn\'t thank me if I did."')
  ('chapter-17.md', 175, 'That was all of it. It was shorter than any one page of the petition.')
  ('chapter-17.md', 273, '"I mean it. Don\'t tell me." Edran wiped his palms down the front of his training shirt, slowly, one and then the other. "If you tell me now, I\'ll have your word for it. And I\'ll probably believe you. You don\'t seem to lie much, and I\'ve watched you a while." He looked at the floor between them, at the chalk and the grey light. "Then I\'ll go into that yard knowing something nobody earned in front of anybody. One more thing on the board that didn\'t come from the board."')
  ('chapter-17.md', 309, '"No. Before, he was asking what you are. That\'s the one he had wrong." She looked at the answer on the board, Quenna\'s short square paragraph. "Now he\'s asking if the board can tell. He\'s allowed to ask that. That\'s his. And you can\'t answer that one with words anyway. It\'s not a words one."')
  ('chapter-18.md', 201, "He understood it on the walk back across the yard, with the binder held flat against his chest in the cold. The first two were the ready pair, built at leisure with the whole of Edran's attention before the fight asked anything of him, and they cost him nothing to spend. The third had nothing behind it. It had to be built from nothing in the middle of everything, and building a full structure took the whole of a Glass fighter's mind, which was not free until the last of the ready pair had gone. Every Glass sequence of three or more had to pass through that one moment when the ready structures were spent and the next was not yet made.")
  ('chapter-18.md', 293, '"It\'s a good seam. I read it. It\'s real."')
  ('chapter-20.md', 59, "Quenna glanced along the rail on the open side, and he followed her eyes. Karis was standing where she had stood all morning, three places down from where Lira and Brom had been, with her notebook still open in her hands and her pen resting on the page. She was not writing. She was watching him with an expression he had not seen on her before. It was not satisfaction, though there was some of that in it. It was closer to the look on a person's face when a sum she had not dared write down has just come out right on somebody else's slate.")
  ('chapter-20.md', 81, '"He earned it. That isn\'t the same thing. You paid him with all your attention, every scrap of it, and tonight when he goes back over the bout he\'ll know that. That was the right coin. Exactly the right one." Her eyes went to the north gate, where Edran had gone. "And he withdrew, standing in the ring with the whole school watching, in words he chose himself, every one of them written down. Losing cost him less than that did. Remember it about him."')
  ('chapter-20.md', 91, '"He moved the third to the other arm, between your third and your fourth, in about the time it takes to walk back to a mark. I\'ve known people train for years and never once change a thing like that during a bout." Brom looked at Cael steadily. "You beat a very good one today. When you write it up, write that down as well. Not just the seam you found in him. Him."')
  ('chapter-20.md', 117, '"I put a crust on you. You remember. In the line. I said I would."')
  ('chapter-21.md', 169, 'Brom went back to his barley, and Cael watched Karis sit with her hands on volume six and then pick up her own notebook and close it. It was not the absent snap of someone done for the night. It was a flat, settled, deliberate closing, as if she had been carrying something, looking for the right room to set it down in, and had just found it.')
  ('chapter-22.md', 169, '"You watched me like a man doing accounts. Ardenmere, that first month. I knew it. Anyone who\'s ever faced you in a circle knows it. It\'s the worst-kept secret you\'ve got." He moved his arms on the rail. "And because she put a name to it on Wednesday, you think it\'s turned into a different thing?"')
  ('chapter-22.md', 181, '"She stands two paces off and watches me for an hour. Nobody in my life has watched me closer. Closer than you did that first month. She knows where my weight\'s going before I\'ve finished sending it there. Then she says *there*, and down I go, and I get up, and I\'m better than I was." His gaze stayed on the floor. "From outside, it\'s what you do. The same thing exactly. One person watching another so hard he ends up knowing the other\'s body better than its owner does."')
  ('chapter-22.md', 205, "Of everyone in the world, this was hers first. It was hers before it was Brom's, because her name was the first one at the front of the binder. It was hers before it was Karis's, because Karis had only ever seen the outside of it. It was perhaps hers even before it was his. From turning toward hard things instead of away from them, again and again, he had learned that a load carried alone only gets heavier for whoever happens to be standing there when it is finally set down.")
  ('chapter-23.md', 27, '"I\'m not done." Now she did turn, and in the starlight her face was very plain. "Say the thing in you was aimed at me somehow, somewhere neither of us could see or feel it. It doesn\'t change one word of what I just said. Keep on as you have been, and when you find something out, tell me." She looked back at the yard. "You can\'t be forgiven for something you never knew you were doing, Cael. It isn\'t the kind of thing forgiving is for. All that\'s left is what you do now you know."')
  ('chapter-24.md', 21, '"Him. He can stand by the door and keep his mouth shut, which he\'s good at." She tore the heel of the loaf in two and pushed half of it across to Brom, which was an apology in advance. "Not you. You\'d help."')
  ('chapter-25.md', 135, '"An observer may watch anything the academy lets anyone watch," she said at last, "and anything a student of full standing invites him to watch. That\'s what the category is. It\'s the one thing it gives you freely." She picked her pen back up. "You don\'t need my leave for it. It isn\'t one of my sessions, and it won\'t be in my record. I\'d prefer to keep it that way."')
  ('chapter-25.md', 187, "The quiet room was at the far end of the training hall, behind a narrow door Cael had walked past fifty times without ever reading the board beside it. It was one of two small rooms the academy kept for students practising a new declaration in private, because a new declaration was nobody's business but the person it had been given to, and the board said only that. A slate hung on a nail by the door, and on the slate, in Karis's hand, it said *K. — declaration practice, sixth bell*, which was true.")
  ('chapter-26.md', 135, 'It was not fast, the first one. It was a flat hard shove off her forearm, and as it came a faint pale plane went along the outside of her arm from wrist to elbow, like a pane of glass seen edge-on, so that the shove arrived as a wall arrives, all at once along its whole width. Brom took it on the forearm and gave, the inch and then the second inch, and turned it. It went back round on her along a line she plainly had not braced for, and she took it on her planted feet without moving at all, as if she had expected it to the last ounce.')
  ('chapter-26.md', 153, "It was the thing Karis had asked him about in the first week, the second half that he held longer than anybody taught. Cael had written it on Brom's page as the best thing about him, the patience that let a strike commit itself fully before Brom took it away, because if he turned too soon the other fellow felt it and kept his weight back. It was the whole of what made his redirect work against people who meant it. And at this speed, for the first time, Cael could see the shape of it as a shape. Between the instant Brom's body had read the strike and the instant his body began to turn it, there was a gap. It was very small, small enough to live inside a breath with room to spare. At Iron speed it was invisible, and at Copper speed it was a gift.")
  ('chapter-26.md', 175, '"A wall," said Wray. She turned to Hobb. "Thank you. You were here in case I needed something slow and heavy to show him the difference, and I didn\'t. Go and eat." Hobb nodded and went, and passing Brom he stopped, and put one broad hand on Brom\'s shoulder, and took it away, and went on without a word.')
  ('chapter-28.md', 57, "He had been thinking about the full push since the day the half came. The half had come not from reaching harder but from choosing a wider road before the force arrived, a channel cut for twice the water. A whole slow push would need the whole road, as broad as Brom's arm, laid out back along the line the push would come in and held there, steady, the whole time the force was arriving. If he reached for more than he had chosen, or chose the whole and then flinched from the size of it, it would go into a joint. The size had to be decided first and then not changed, and that was the entire difficulty, because nothing in him wanted to stand still while a thing that large came in.")
  ('chapter-28.md', 65, '"Elbow." He flexed it. It moved, unhappily. "I changed my mind in the middle. It went where I hadn\'t decided."')
  ('chapter-28.md', 81, '"Whole," said Brom, from the floor. He was holding his own wrist. "That was the whole of it. It came back whole."')
  ('chapter-29.md', 87, "She took it. Cael saw her decide to, a half-beat before the hand landed, the same decision he had watched Lira make on the circuit a hundred times and in a formal yard the week before last. She let Hobb's palm hit her collarbone with the weight Wray would have told him to put into it. It rocked her back a step, and she did not take the second step back. She planted on it. She turned from it the way Lira turned from a hit, so that the step back became a step round, and then, with the whole of her weight and the whole of her height, she put her shoulder into Hobb's side, under his arm, where he had not set himself, and Hobb moved.")
  ('chapter-29.md', 185, "Edran came in. The edge came first, fast, a hard bright sweep at Brom's shoulder, and Brom took it on the forearm, and gave, and waited, and turned. The edge burst in its spray of glitter. The shell came next, driven at Brom's chest, and while it was still whole on Edran's left forearm the third had already begun on the free right arm, the way he had taught himself in the fourth exchange of the exhibition. Brom took the shell, and gave, and waited, and turned, and it burst against nothing, and the third was there behind it. It came up thin and pale and not quite finished, but less thin than it had been in the yard, and less pale. Cael could see three weeks of mornings in it.")
  ('chapter-30.md', 5, "She sat on Brom's bed with her back against the wall and her boots on Brom's blanket, which Brom permitted from nobody else, and a folded cloth from the kitchen pump in her lap, dripping slightly. She did not speak. She had said she would not, and Lira kept her word about speaking less often than she kept it about anything else, so that when she did keep it, it meant something.")
  ('chapter-30.md', 45, '"Not long. Long enough." She did not take her hand off the cloth. "Long enough that I\'ve got something to say to you, and I\'m going to say it tomorrow."')
  ('chapter-30.md', 141, '"Months ago. Before Greyvane, before the road. A session with Brom, in his sparring circle in Ardenmere. The read was open; we were working on it the way we always worked on it. Nothing about that afternoon was different from a hundred others." He could still see the circle, the scuffed chalk, Brom\'s forearm under his hand. He did not reach for anything else. "And for less than a second, through the read, there was something like water. Water moving, somewhere there wasn\'t any water. Then it was gone, before I could even turn toward it. Nothing came after it. No notice. I\'ve never had it again."')
  ('chapter-30.md', 185, "*Told Karis, in the third week of the nulls, because she asked properly whether the shape she could see was the whole shape. She took me through it three ways. No Tide anywhere near. Nobody watched. Nothing on it. It shouldn't exist. It exists. Her mark on it is real, unexplained, keep. Nothing else in her notebook carries that.*")
  ('chapter-30.md', 221, 'Cael went up to the residence wing and left them there. Halfway up the stair it came to him that the part of him he had written down a week ago had been listening too. *Part of me is waiting for it.* None of the nulls since had moved it. It had heard Gerda say she would come out of the haze and take what was put on her where the chairs could see, and it had leaned toward the words, because somewhere ahead there was still a floor that was his. He did not like it any better than he had at the desk. He went on up. At the turn of the stair he looked back through the doorway once and saw them still sitting side by side in the one circle of lamplight at the far end of the empty hall, two girls with dots on their tags and their backs to a rail. Neither of them was looking at the clock.')
  ('chapter-32.md', 37, "Quenna's room was up a narrow stair at the back of the main hall, over the assessors' room, and he had never been in it. It was smaller than he had imagined and much tidier: a desk under a window that looked down onto the formal yard, three chairs, a shelf of folders tied with tape, and a cold grate with a kettle on it that had plainly not been used since the autumn. There was nothing on the walls. It looked like the room of somebody who did her real work somewhere else and came here only to write it down.")
  ('chapter-33.md', 103, '"That\'s Ternhall. Every Ember school in the country teaches the same declarations; the Arbiter gives what it gives. What a school teaches is the order you use them in." He nodded at the section. "Ternhall teaches orders the way a grammar teaches sentences. Points first, always. Joins last, always. Never join what you can still move. A Ternhall Ember child can lay you a dozen different drawings by the time she\'s fifteen, all from the same two declarations, like a clever child saying a dozen different things with the same forty words." He sniffed. "Ternhall calls it a lattice. Most schools haven\'t got a word for it at all, because most schools can\'t do it. It isn\'t a declaration. It\'s a habit of putting them in the right order."')
  ('chapter-33.md', 235, '"Wanted to know," he said. "Four years, people tell me where to stand. Stand there, hit this hard, don\'t win." He considered the boots. "Nobody told me yesterday. So I wanted to know what it did."')
  ('chapter-34.md', 17, '"The bags are channels." She tossed one to Brom, who caught it without looking. "Brom throws them. Flat, along the ground, from one circle toward another, whenever he likes and without telling anybody. If a bag touches you, you\'ve crossed a channel, and I count it out loud, and I don\'t feel sorry." She looked at him. "And I\'m also me. I\'ll be pressing you the whole time. Making you turn. Because she won\'t just stand there laying coins and waiting for you to trip. She\'ll be making you choose."')
  ('chapter-34.md', 21, '"She didn\'t need to touch Hobb. Hobb was walking forward. You won\'t be." Lira set her feet. "Framework. Panel speed is for panels. Go."')
  ('chapter-34.md', 145, '"You\'re picking." Lira got up, slowly, using the pump. "Last week you\'d have stood there deciding until the bag made the decision for you. That\'s better. Better\'s just less standing there." She went to collect the bags. "Tomorrow we find out if it\'s real, or if it\'s only my shoulder making me slow."')
  ('chapter-34.md', 251, '"You\'re still deciding," said Lira, "whether to trust the slip or throw it away. And you\'re doing it in the same half-beat you\'re meant to be doing one or the other. You can\'t do both in there. There isn\'t room."')
  ('chapter-34.md', 277, "*Lira's lattice. Eleven, nine, six, four, four. Stopped at four. I'm still deciding in the half-beat where I should be moving. Lira: pick faster. Speed the decision. The correctness comes later.*")
  ('chapter-35.md', 189, '"Tomorrow, don\'t fight the experiment." There was none of her usual care in how she said it; it came out flat and plain. "Fight me. Whatever happens or doesn\'t happen in that yard, let it happen to somebody who was busy. Don\'t stand at your mark with half of you reaching and half of you writing it down. You can\'t steer anything while you\'re watching your own hands on the wheel. You\'ll only see your hands."')
  ('chapter-36.md', 93, "He saw every one, and that was the worst of it. He named them as they came, *point, point, point*, and the naming was clean and true and no use at all. To see a promise made was one coin, and to be quick enough to stop it being kept was another, and she was paying out faster than he could reply. Every slip he took cost him a choice, every choice cost him a half-beat, and every half-beat she spent on another point, so that the floor got smaller round him a coin at a time, like Lira's ring filling with chalk.")
  ('chapter-36.md', 99, 'He felt it before he saw it, a hiss and a sudden heat that climbed the back of his calf like a hand laid there. A thin bright line joined the point behind him to one beside him along the frozen earth, close enough that he smelled scorched wool. The yard made a sound. It was low, and nobody meant to make it; it was the sound of three hundred people beginning to wonder whether this was still a demonstration.')
  ('chapter-36.md', 183, "He walked back to the south mark holding his left arm a little away from his side. He did not look at it. It was beating with a slow heavy pulse of its own, like a second heart worn on the outside of the arm. He would not look at it, because looking would make it a thing to think about and he had nothing to spare for thinking. The drop's bill came due on the walk, and it was bigger than any it had ever sent him, because he had let go of more than ever before. A strap pulled tight across his breastbone for three breaths instead of one, and then it eased.")
  ('chapter-37.md', 225, "He had carried the oldest of the four since Ardenmere and never once noticed a person in any of them. Or he had never let himself notice. He did now. He turned toward the Wind-adjacent with great care, not reaching for it, only paying attention. It was there, and it had been there all along: a quickness that had never been his, a habit of leaving early off the back foot, a stubbornness in the turn. Lira. Lira in a freezing yard in Ardenmere, teaching a boy to stay alive because a boy who did not learn would not see the month out. Under the read lay Brom's patience. Under the redirect lay a weight he had met once across a chalk line, from a Bronze fighter who had beaten him fair. And low in his chest, under the Compression, was a ferocity that had once faced him in front of six hundred people and very nearly finished him.")
  ('chapter-37.md', 257, '"You didn\'t." Lira looked back at the yard. "Nobody made her anything. She walked out there and made herself into it, in writing, with a line under it. And you went in to fight her. I watched you. You weren\'t looking for anything in the first two exchanges. You were losing, and you were fighting, and you crossed fire to stand closer to her because it was the only way left to try to beat her." She drew a long breath and let it out white. "Whatever happened in the third, it happened to somebody who was busy. That\'s the only version of it I could ever have lived with. It\'s the one I got."')
  ('chapter-38.md', 47, '"I wanted you to know that I\'m not. In case you\'d been bracing for it." Quenna looked ahead at the main hall door. "Your consent is in the file, with my initials beside it. It says exactly what you meant it to say. Nobody is going to read it any other way while I\'m the one who holds the file."')
  ('chapter-38.md', 145, 'He was at the far end of the long table with the observation notebook shut on the table before him and his bad arm resting in its loose linen across his lap. He had not seen her for two days, and it had taken him until Friday afternoon to notice, which embarrassed him once he had. Since the autumn Karis had kept time like the water clock: every session, every meal, every evening in the stable, and the archive so regularly that the bells might have been set by her. Then, from Brom\'s room on the night of the match, nothing. The only sighting was Brom\'s: Saturday at first light, a figure crossing the yard at speed with the collar up, gone before he could call out. When Cael asked Lira, Lira said only, "She\'s writing something she doesn\'t want watched." Lira would know. Lira had once lost two days of her own to four paragraphs from Fenmark, and he did not ask.')
  ('chapter-38.md', 181, 'She stood, and she did not reach for the page; she left it in his hands, and that said something too, because it meant she was not going to carry it off and improve it. It was finished. It was what she had found.')
  ('chapter-39.md', 253, "He did not reach for it, because Lira's rule held until the third day and the third day was not yet; he only attended to it. It was where it had been since Thursday, low and warm, not doing anything, and it still felt like her, the precision still there in the warmth, and the patience, the small commitments kept exactly. He had stopped being startled by that and had begun, instead, to be glad of it, in a way he did not yet know how to write down, and perhaps never would. Some things you carried before you filed them.")
  ('chapter-39.md', 265, "It answered on the left turn and the right, on the third sequence and the eleventh, and on the double that Lira threw at the end, two pushes one on top of the other, the way Wray had broken the count at the first sitting, and it read both off her shoulders and slipped him out of both, and there was no empty pocket anywhere in it. It was quick and light and his, the oldest thing he owned, and the quickness in it was Lira's and always had been.")
  ('chapter-39.md', 275, '"Three clean days," said Lira. "That\'s the rule, and it\'s met." She took the rope off the pump\'s nail and began to coil it, and did not look at him while she did it. "You can touch the new one now. Carefully. Small. With somebody in the room." She hung the rope back. "Tomorrow. Not today. Today you\'ve earned a breakfast."')
  ('chapter-40.md', 111, '"My point is a held heat. I lay it and it\'s mine, and it stays as long as I hold it, and it dies when I let go. That\'s the lag you timed, the dying." She straightened. "There\'s no point here. There was never a point here. You didn\'t lay anything. There\'s a fire in the wood, and the wood was keeping it, and it would have gone on keeping it until it ran out of wood." She looked at him, and her face was doing the thing it had done in the formal yard, the researcher arriving first and something else coming up behind her. "It doesn\'t behave like fire at all. Not like mine. Mine never catches anything unless I make it."')
  ('chapter-41.md', 67, '"Was it quiet? Inside you. You said it goes very quiet."')
  ('chapter-41.md', 131, 'She nodded, once, as she did when a sum came out. Then she turned the slate over in her arms and looked at it and did not write anything on it, which he had never seen her do before. She was holding a small card in the same hand as the slate, pressed against its frame, just as he had once held a card of his own that said nothing anybody could read, and she did not show it to him and he did not look at it. It was hers. It was the first thing she had ever owned that nobody else would ever be allowed to read, and she held it as if she already knew that.')
  ('chapter-41.md', 261, '"The standard procedure is service by the regional channel. A sealed packet to the provost by courier, a copy to the magistrate\'s office, the date set from there." The senior officer looked at the packet on the desk, which was now as thick as a hand and tied in two directions. "It\'s a good packet. I\'ve read it. It will stand up by itself. You don\'t need to walk it up a hill."')
  ('chapter-41.md', 265, "He did not try to explain the rest, because the rest did not fit on a form, and the senior officer had been in the service long enough to know that a man who said *I don't need to* in that voice was not finished. A packet sent by courier was read first by a provost alone at his desk, and then explained to its subject secondhand, by people who loved him and were frightened for him and would explain it in their own words. Secondhand bred errors. Errors bred appeals. And under that, where he did not need to look at it for long, there was a borrowed office in Ardenmere and a boy with his hands pressed white on his knees saying *Thank you*. Coss had stamped a summons closed-pending that afternoon with more force than a stamp required. He did not intend to do this one at the end of a courier's arm.")
  ('chapter-42.md', 13, 'He had sat across a borrowed desk from that patience once, in Ardenmere, with his hands pressed together on his knees and a paper of his own that he had found in a district archive in two days. He had learned its shape that afternoon without meaning to: a man who wrote everything down and never seemed to hurry, and never once, in the whole afternoon, wrote anything that was not so. The shape was older now, and more travelled, and there was more grey in it. It was built the same way underneath.')
  ('chapter-42.md', 75, '"I have. That needs explaining first." Coss took something small from the case and held it so the room could see it. It was a card, stamped and plainly printed, not much bigger than a playing card, and Naveth did not look at it. He had seen it already, Cael realized, alone, before anybody went to fetch the observer from the archive.')
  ('chapter-42.md', 117, '"You were fourteen," he said. "You came into that office in Ardenmere with a paper you\'d found in two days, and it was right, and I had to concede it. I was as gentle that afternoon as the work allowed. I hoped that was the part you\'d remember." He did not look away, and he did not soften. "This is also the job. Hear that as plainly as I can say it. It isn\'t easier for being true. It isn\'t less true for being hard."')
  ('chapter-43.md', 85, "He saw it before he saw her face. The hollow circle was gone, and in its place hung the academy's ordinary crest, a tower above a road, the same tag Brom wore, cut from the same tin by the same clerk. She had polished it. It caught the light from the stable door.")
  ('chapter-43.md', 111, '"I know what they\'re going to decide." Her chin came up. "I mean it isn\'t fair that it\'s you. You\'re the only one who never once told me anything that wasn\'t so. I\'m going to be in the gallery. On the first day. It says the record will be public, so I\'m allowed. I checked."')
  ('chapter-43.md', 151, '"Why?" said Lira. She did not raise her voice. "You said you\'d tell us why. Why can\'t it be won?"')
  ('chapter-43.md', 201, '"I\'ll study it the way I study anybody I have to fight. Systems have architecture. Architecture has seams." He shrugged a little. "I\'ve been finding seams in people since I was twelve, and this one doesn\'t sweat or breathe, but it\'s still made by people. I\'ll be using my eyes on a page instead of on a stance, that\'s all."')
  ('chapter-44.md', 163, "*The man from the borrowed office in Ardenmere came up the hill on Monday with correct papers. He was let in because the papers were correct. He has filed a challenge to my enrollment, and there will be a hearing in three weeks, in public, with a magistrate of the Compact. He was honest with me about what he is. He was gentle about it. It's the job, he says. I believe him.*")
  ('chapter-45.md', 73, 'He did not trust it, because a thing that size never deserved trust the first time you saw it, and he had lost his worst bouts by throwing himself at a seam before he had made sure it was really there. There had to be a page he had missed, in this building or in a records hall three districts away: an amendment, an annex, an order somewhere that made the flag lawful. Nothing ran for centuries on nothing.')
  ('chapter-45.md', 137, '"I\'ve found how they wrote." She set her spoon down. "Before the code was put in the order it\'s in now, the registers weren\'t boxes. They were sentences. An assessor at a waystation would write a whole paragraph about a person: what they\'d seen, what they thought, what they couldn\'t decide. Some of them argued with themselves on the page. Some of them crossed things out and wrote *I was wrong about this* in the margin a year later." She looked at Cael. "Nobody does that now. Now there\'s a box, and you\'re either in it or you\'re a flag."')
  ('chapter-45.md', 227, '"I found it on the third," she said. "The wait. I could hit it every time after that, the instant it opened." She was quiet a moment. "I didn\'t like hitting it. It\'s like hitting somebody who\'s holding a door for you."')
  ('chapter-46.md', 339, '"Then it\'ll take me a week." She drew her lamp nearer. "The founding shelves are mine in the day. This is mine in the evenings. I don\'t need much sleep. I never have." She did not look up from the page. "It isn\'t the argument, before you say it. It\'s a second reading. That\'s a different thing. Any finding worth having has two."')
  ('chapter-46.md', 357, "*The code is architecture. Architecture has seams. I've spent the better part of two years learning where the joins are in things that have them: a fighter's stance, a Path's timing, and now a system's rules about what it may do to people. It's the same work. The difference is that nobody limps away from it.*")
  ('chapter-47.md', 73, 'Coss looked at the case in the porter\'s arms. "It suits you. You were always careful." He said it plainly, with no weight on it at all, and turned back to the magistrate before Havel could decide what to do with it.')
  ('chapter-47.md', 237, 'Karis was already at the long table, in from the founding shelves with the long flat box set at her elbow and the fourth volume of the schedule open in front of her, her pen lying capped beside it. She did not look up when he sat down across from her, and he did not speak, because a second reader who was spoken to while reading was, he supposed, an echo with an interruption.')
  ('chapter-48.md', 121, '"I\'ve lost," said Prynn. "I\'ll say so, so nobody has to wait for it. The first article I could have had and the cross-index I couldn\'t, and the amendment takes the whole floor out from under both. One word does it." She closed the enforcement volume, carefully, with both hands. "Now. The inventory."')
  ('chapter-48.md', 217, '"I\'m thinking about the general clause," said Karis. She put her finger on the line. "*Any holding of the respondent institution.* And the cross-index says a holding is any paper the institution keeps, by whatever hand made." She looked up then, and her face was doing the careful thing it did before she said something about a timing she would rather not have seen. "I carry my satchel to the founding shelves every morning. I set it on the end of the reading shelf in the third case. My notebooks are in it. My ledger is in it. It sits on Prynn\'s shelf, in Prynn\'s archive, all day, for anybody to see."')
  ('chapter-49.md', 69, "Cael looked at it. It was the form from the first afternoon, or a certified copy of it, with Prynn's small *Correct* pencilled at the foot. His own name stood at the bottom of it in a hand that was a term younger than the one he used now and not much different.")
  ('chapter-50.md', 43, '"For what it\'s worth," said Coss, "I hope the argument\'s a good one. Whatever it is." He settled his coat on his shoulders. "I\'ve seen this provision put under strain three times, and every time the people who stood up for it stood up with feeling. Feeling and delay. I\'ve never once seen anybody stand up for it with a reading." He began to walk again, toward the gate. Then, over his shoulder, as plainly as the hour: "Five days."')
  ('chapter-50.md', 89, 'He had known for five days that she would say it, and he had told himself every night that knowing was the same as hearing it. It was not. Hearing it was like a weight being set down on the table between them, and he felt the table take it.')
  ('chapter-51.md', 127, "*Capabilities observed across multiple signature families. No single designation applicable per standard practice. Assessor's judgment: designate UNBOUND per provisional custom, pending further study. Study not recorded as completed.*")
  ('chapter-52.md', 229, 'Havel saw her do it. He did not see the words; he saw her eyes go to the one line and stop there, and her lips move, very slightly, over it. It was not a check. She had checked it already, twice, and entered it, and referred it, and there was nothing left in it for a check to find. She looked at it the way Havel had once seen a carter look at a wheel that had come off his cart on a straight dry road, with nothing anywhere to have struck it. The carter had not been looking to learn how it happened. He had looked because the wheel was still lying there.')

