chapter-59.md ---  He left before dawn with the aide and the cases, as he had come.
chapter-60.md They walked the long turns of the cart road between the stone walls in the hard bright cold, and at the bottom they went along the street worth walking toward the draper's, slowly, as they had once walked the market streets of Ardenmere with nothing to buy and a great deal to look at.
chapter-60.md On the way back across the yard Cael passed the defensive floor's high windows and stopped, as he always did, to look in.
chapter-60.md At the far end of the floor Edran was building his third structure on his off arm and letting it go, as he had every morning since the frost.
chapter-60.md Karis sat across from him reading her narrow brown notebook, and he saw her eyes go once to the binder and away, as they had gone to it once before at a breakfast in the seventeenth week, and as they had not gone to it since.
chapter-61.md Everything else he banked, as he had banked it since the first morning.
chapter-61.md The page stayed open, as it had been since the autumn, and he closed the book on it.
chapter-61.md She wrote this box's number and its case and the date, as she had written the others, and closed the ledger.
chapter-61.md ---  They found the wall at dusk, the four of them, as they had found walls in more towns than he had kept count of: the low one by the residence wing door where Lira sat on evenings when there was nothing to do.
chapter-61.md Karis said the quiet thing first, as she generally did.
chapter-61.md Under them the hill was cold and old and took their weight, as it had taken the weight of everybody who had ever stopped on it on the way to somewhere else.

