editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.md:143:**Nits, optional.** ch43 "There were six of them and five chairs, and Brom stood" — Quenna also stands (at the window, by habit), so one chair is empty; the line is good enough to keep. ch43's opening ("before the seventh bell") sits earlier in the Monday evening than ch42's close on the wall (lamps going out), and ch42's last line has him picturing the case "floor to ceiling, as deep as an old woman's arm" before ch43 shows it to him — this was in the pre-repair text, the editorial review passed the calendar, and the two readings reconcile if the notice and the archive come between supper and the wall; outside this recheck's scope. The ch41 ≥40 share stays 6.8% by design.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.md:151:1. **ch44 scene 1 (division of labour), ~900 words — rebuild from the event list.** This is the scene that still counts as skeleton paraphrase. The event list is fixed (Cael and Prynn take the code; Karis the founding shelves, truth not argument; Brom the witnesses, uncoached, the estate line; Naveth and Quenna procedure and lawful delay; Lira keeps him a fighter — "Not a lawyer wearing your face."). Handles the author already owns: let the claims come out of order and by interruption rather than round the desk (Lira first, not last, since she has been silent and furious; Brom's witness line said on the stair, not in the office); let the plan be made on Karis's ruled page, each name written as it is claimed, so the scene has an object; let Cael's "this was what he needed most" reflection be replaced by something he does (he writes Lira's hour into the page before his own). Keep every quoted line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.md:152:2. **ch44 scene 4, from "Nothing left open overnight" to "a third way a thing could go unscarred", ~500 words.** The rules can be a card on the table he reads and breaks one by one (the slip entry already points there); the "angry with it / studying it" exchange can be moved off the first morning or made Prynn's question from the high desk while he is mid-page; the three ages can be read off the concordance's columns (dates, not voices) so the paragraph has a different spine.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.md:153:3. **ch45 scene 4, from "Is that a compliment?" to "Fourth shelf, second case", ~510 words.** Let Cael's true answer come before the clever one, or let Prynn ask her question with the tea tin already in her hand and her back half turned; let the argument summary be read off the page by Prynn, not said by Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.md:155:5. **Recommended, same pass, if the author is in those scenes:** ch43 council — Naveth's three speeches and Cael's third-way speech re-cut so that the points come in a different order (e.g. Naveth names the withdrawal before he explains why the merits are lost); ch42 scene 4 — the three speeches given their own sentence shapes (Karis's can be two lines, not four).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.md:169:old: `"Do," said Naveth. "Thank me if it works."\n\nKaris caught him at the foot of the worn stair after the others had gone, with her notebook shut under her arm and her face doing the careful thing it did when she had seen a fault in somebody's timing and had not yet decided whether she was allowed to say so.\n\n"He's right," she said. "The provost. About the charter."\n\n"I know he's right."\n\n"I wrote it down when the Warden said it. On Monday, in the stable, from what you told us. *Exposes its own charter to review.*" She held the notebook a little tighter. "I underlined it, and then I didn't look at it again, because I didn't want it to be true." She looked at him. "That's a bad habit in a researcher. I'm telling you so you'll watch me for it."\n\n"I'll watch you for it."\n\n"Good." She went two steps up the passage toward the archive and turned. "Whatever you find about your word, find out about his lever as well. A finding that only covers half the question isn't a finding. It's a hope with a citation."`
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.md:170:new: `"Do," said Naveth. "Thank me if it works."`
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.md:173:old: `At the foot of the worn stair the six of them came apart in five directions without anybody saying goodbye. The provost went to find his counsel, and Quenna went toward her folders, already reading one as she walked. Karis went toward the residence wing at the stride she kept for beginnings, and Brom out into the yard, where Edran would be at the high windows. Lira went to the floor desk to put their names down for dusk. Cael stood alone where the passages met and watched the five of them go, every one of them off to rearrange a life round something he had said in a room an hour ago, and found he did not have anywhere to put the feeling. So he went to the archive, because that was where the work was.`
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.md:174:new: `At the foot of the worn stair the six of them came apart without anybody saying goodbye. The provost went to find his counsel, and Quenna went toward her folders, already reading one as she walked. Brom went out into the yard, where Edran would be at the high windows, and Lira to the floor desk to put their names down for dusk. Cael stood where the passages met and watched them go, every one of them off to rearrange a life round something he had said in a room an hour ago, and found he did not have anywhere to put the feeling.\n\nKaris had not gone. She caught him at the foot of the worn stair when the others were out of hearing, with her notebook shut under her arm and her face doing the careful thing it did when she had seen a fault in somebody's timing and had not yet decided whether she was allowed to say so.\n\n"He's right," she said. "The provost. About the charter."\n\n"I know he's right."\n\n"I wrote it down when the Warden said it. On Monday, in the stable, from what you told us. *Exposes its own charter to review.*" She held the notebook a little tighter. "I underlined it, and then I didn't look at it again, because I didn't want it to be true." She looked at him. "That's a bad habit in a researcher. I'm telling you so you'll watch me for it."\n\n"I'll watch you for it."\n\n"Good." She went two steps up the passage toward the archive and turned. "Whatever you find about your word, find out about his lever as well. A finding that only covers half the question isn't a finding. It's a hope with a citation."\n\nShe went on ahead of him. He stood a moment longer, and then he went to the archive too, because that was where the work was.`
