editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/review-editorial.md:9:**Where interest caught.** Breakfast on the Tuesday, with Lira counting terms on her fingers and Brom supplying "Buckets," and then the cook's woodpile: "She calls it kindling." That is the first laugh of the movement and it is earned by three people's characters at once. The first deliberate test in the quiet room is the best-written stretch of the seven chapters: the candle "not lit and then is, with nothing in between," the cost arriving *after* like a missed stair in the dark, Lira chafing his hand and saying "You looked like you were thinking about your dinner," and Karis kneeling at the post and saying, to nobody, "That isn't my point." The bench in the grey building (ch41) is the emotional high point: Denvash coming up "the way weather comes, into his body, without asking," two hands on a thin rigid shoulder, taken away as she stands and not before, and "Anchor. Anchor Path. Copper." followed by "It's ordinary." I would read the book for that scene alone. Later, D6 with a pen moving at the back of the hall, and Coss stopping writing, is a quiet scene that carries real suspense; the gauntlet is a clean, trackable fight with a loss that buys knowledge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/review-editorial.md:13:**Which person matters.** Oona, by a distance, and then Prynn. Oona is the category's true user walking out of it, and the author lets that fact do its own work without a speech. Prynn's affection arrives entirely through tea, an unlocked door, a dusty laugh and "It's a good road. It's only the wrong one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/review-editorial.md:15:**Which event I expect next.** The delegation, and the first time Coss and Cael are in the same room after D6. I also expect Karis to find nothing in the schedule, and to say so.
