editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/review-cold.md:7:**Orientation (arriving at chapter 40 mid-book):** Oriented within two pages. The chapters re-seat a newcomer on everything load-bearing without a recap: Cael, fifteen, on Greyvane's "unclassified observer track" under a borrowed category; Kindled at Denvash a year ago and given a word that is not a Path; the expulsion (Hesk's workshop, the courier, the bag on the bed); the Ardenmere circuit; a burned arm from a recent formal-yard match; the four at the scarred table (Lira, Brom, Karis, Cael); Oona, the other observer-track student; Quenna, Wray, Naveth, Prynn; and Coss, the Warden who held his file. The one thing I could not place and would want from earlier chapters: "the notice" that already used the words *contact-adjacent* and *single channel* before Cael had ever tested the spark (40, "That's what the notice said"; 41, "It's in the notice"). It reads as a deliberate carry-over rather than a gap, and Quenna's record in 46 picks up the same language, so I trust it is planted. "The word" written next to his name in Denvash is withheld throughout; that also reads as deliberate, and it works, but a reader who missed it earlier has nothing to hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/review-cold.md:13:**Where interest caught.** Immediately, at breakfast in 40: five terms counted on fingers, Brom adding "Buckets," and the cook's woodpile being called kindling. The experiment that follows is the best kind of fantasy-science scene: "Nothing glowed," then the ring of char and the thread of smoke "straight as a plumb line," and a cost that arrives *after*, cold down the arm, that nobody watching can see. Karis's "You haven't got fire. You've got a spark" is the sentence I would quote to someone to sell the book. Interest caught again at the bench in 41 (Denvash standing "in the passage in front of the real one"), and hardest at the gate in 42, where Coss is let in "because nothing about him was wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/review-cold.md:17:**Which person matters.** Cael, and then Lira, who is the movement's spine: she takes "the part nobody's said" in 43, claps behind him in 44, and in 46 sets the rule "Three times. Not four." Coss is the most interesting figure in the movement precisely because he is decent; "he isn't worth burning anything on. The machine" is the right note and I believed Lira when she said it. Oona steals 41 outright. Brom's gauntlet in 45 is the movement's physical set piece and earns its length.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-006/review-cold.md:19:**Which event I expect next.** The hearing (first bell, first day of week twenty-two). Specifically: Coss standing up with the first draft "in that leaning hand"; the fourth file's eleven words read into the record; Karis finding something on the founding shelves that is not the argument but is the truth; and Hesk coming anyway despite "Please don't come." I also expect Coss to answer the seam not by defending the Compact's authority over Cael but by pointing at Greyvane's charter, which is the lever he actually named in 42; the manuscript has not yet let anyone on Cael's side say that out loud.
