editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-08.md:81:A year ago, in his first months, the column of things not told had been five long. It had sat in him like a stone in a boot all winter, until, in the spring, he had put all five in one letter and sent it over the hills. He had promised then to tell the next thing as it came. He went through the letter now in his head, line by line, as he went through the exchanges of a bout, and looked for anything he had left out because it was easier left out. The trials: told. The rule: told. The notice changing its story: told, and told plainly, with the part he did not understand left in as not understood. The fan and its empty quarter: told. The farmer: told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-08.md:89:They did it properly, because there was no other way he could bear to do it. She chalked the cross. She fetched her measuring stick, the one she had cut from a broom handle in the spring and notched in fingers' widths, and laid it on the stone beside the cross. He stood at the post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-08.md:155:He thought about the half-breath at the end of every burst, and Lira's sentence, and the people on the benches who were not there for the fun of it. Somebody was going to wait for the landing. He had known it since the spring. He had spent ten trials and two days of a hollow chest finding out what the landing was worth, and he had a rule now, and a half-breath that was no longer only a wound. He had not yet once spent it on purpose against anybody but Lira. He did not know what it was worth with a stranger's strike in it.
