editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-09.md:165:Cael stood where he was. His left arm hung at his side, and he could not feel his fingers, and under his ribs there was a cold hollow where the giving face had drawn on him. His hip had begun its long familiar complaint from the crest of the bone to the knee. He did not mind any of it. He stood on the stone a step from where he had landed and found that he was shaking very slightly, not from the hit or the cost, but from what he had seen in the lock. He had seen a whole committed strike from start to finish, as plainly as print.
