editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-06.md:87:The first face was the one he had found at Torvin's trough in the spring, night after night in the yard by the pump, with the water going flat under his hand. It was the taking face. When he called it, a weight came into his forearm and shoulder that was not mass, like bones gone full of water. A blow that landed on the weighted arm did not stay where it landed. It spread, and went down through him, and into the floor. It was slow, though; it needed most of a breath to gather. And it showed: when it came, he settled lower and his feet went wider, and anyone watching could see him decide to be hit.
